export const A2 = [
  {"id":"a2-01","level":"A2","icon":"🕘","title":"A normal working day","grammar":"Present tense of regular verbs","summary":"ik: the stem. jij, u, hij, zij: stem + t. wij, jullie, zij: the infinitive. Jij or je directly after the verb loses its t. Spell the stem as a Dutch word: maak, stop, reis.","explain":"The present tense (*de tegenwoordige tijd*) is the tense Dutch uses most: for what is happening now, for habits, for facts, and very often for the future too (`Morgen werk ik thuis`). There is only one present tense: `ik werk` is both \"I work\" and \"I am working\". Getting it right depends on one thing: finding the **stem** of the verb.\n\n### The stem and the endings\n\nTake the infinitive and remove `-en`. What is left is the stem: `werken → werk`.\n\n| | werken | ending |\n| ik | werk | stem |\n| jij / je, u | werkt | stem + t |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | werkt | stem + t |\n| wij / we, jullie, zij / ze | werken | infinitive |\n\n> Ik werk op een kantoor. — I work in an office.\n> Mijn collega werkt thuis. — My colleague works from home.\n> Wij werken van negen tot vijf. — We work from nine to five.\n\n### Spelling the stem\n\nThe stem has to **sound** like the infinitive, and it has to look like a Dutch word. That gives three rules.\n\n- **A long vowel is written double.** In `ma-ken` the `a` is long because its syllable is open. Without `-en` the syllable closes, so the vowel is doubled: `maken → maak`, `lopen → loop`, `praten → praat`, `sturen → stuur`.\n- **A double consonant becomes single.** No Dutch word ends in two of the same consonant: `stoppen → stop`, `zeggen → zeg`, `beginnen → begin`.\n- **v becomes f, z becomes s.** No Dutch word ends in v or z: `leven → leef`, `schrijven → schrijf`, `reizen → reis`, `lezen → lees`.\n\n> Ik maak eerst koffie. — I make coffee first.\n> De vergadering begint om tien uur. — The meeting starts at ten o'clock.\n> Hij reist veel voor zijn werk. — He travels a lot for his work.\n\nIn the plural the infinitive comes back, and with it the single vowel and the v or z: `ik lees, hij leest`, but `wij lezen`; `ik maak`, but `wij maken`.\n\n### Stems that end in t or d\n\n- A stem that ends in **t** never gets a second t: `praten → hij praat`, `zitten → zij zit`, `wachten → hij wacht`.\n- A stem that ends in **d** does get the t, although you cannot hear it: `worden → hij wordt`, `vinden → zij vindt`. (Unit B1 · 13 is all about this.)\n\n### Jij after the verb: no t\n\nWhen `jij` or `je` comes directly **after** the verb, the t disappears. That happens in questions and whenever the sentence starts with something other than the subject.\n\n> Jij begint vroeg. — You start early.\n> Begin jij altijd zo vroeg? — Do you always start this early?\n> Morgen werk je thuis. — Tomorrow you are working from home.\n\nThis is only true for jij and je. `Hij` and `u` keep their t: `Werkt hij vandaag?`, `Werkt u hier?`\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The ik-form is the bare stem: `ik werk`, never *ik werkt or *ik werken.\n- The t of hij and zij is part of the spelling even where fast speech swallows it: `hij werkt`, never *hij werk.\n- Write the stem as a Dutch word: `ik reis`, not *ik reiz; `ik maak`, not *ik mak; `ik stop`, not *ik stopp.\n- `Wij`, `jullie` and plural `zij` always take the infinitive: `jullie werken`, never *jullie werkt.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has a different ending for every person (`працюю, працюєш, працює…`), so the pronoun can be left out. Dutch has only three forms, and the subject must always be there: `Працюю вдома` is `Ik werk thuis`, never just *Werk thuis.","words":[{"nl":"collega","art":"de","pl":"collega's","en":"colleague","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kantoor","art":"het","pl":"kantoren","en":"office","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vergadering","art":"de","pl":"vergaderingen","en":"meeting","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pauze","art":"de","pl":"pauzes","en":"break","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afspraak","art":"de","pl":"afspraken","en":"appointment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"baas","art":"de","pl":"bazen","en":"boss","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bericht","art":"het","pl":"berichten","en":"message","pos":"n"},{"nl":"taak","art":"de","pl":"taken","en":"task","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beginnen","past":"begon","perf":"is begonnen","en":"to begin / to start","pos":"v"},{"nl":"stoppen","past":"stopte","perf":"heeft/is gestopt","en":"to stop","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vergaderen","past":"vergaderde","perf":"heeft vergaderd","en":"to have a meeting","pos":"v"},{"nl":"sturen","past":"stuurde","perf":"heeft gestuurd","en":"to send","pos":"v"},{"nl":"reizen","past":"reisde","perf":"heeft/is gereisd","en":"to travel","pos":"v"},{"nl":"regelen","past":"regelde","perf":"heeft geregeld","en":"to arrange / to organise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"druk","en":"busy","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"meestal","en":"usually","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik werk op een kantoor in Utrecht.","en":"I work in an office in Utrecht.","hint":["werken","kantoor"],"note":"The ik-form is the bare stem: werken → werk, with no ending."},{"nl":"Mijn collega begint elke dag om acht uur.","en":"My colleague starts at eight o'clock every day.","hint":["collega","beginnen","elke dag","uur"],"note":"Mijn collega is a hij/zij-subject: stem + t. The stem of beginnen is begin, with one n.","alt":["Elke dag begint mijn collega om acht uur."]},{"nl":"Wij vergaderen meestal op maandag.","en":"We usually have a meeting on Mondays.","hint":["vergaderen","meestal","maandag"],"note":"With wij the verb is simply the infinitive: vergaderen.","alt":["Meestal vergaderen wij op maandag."]},{"nl":"De baas stuurt veel berichten.","en":"The boss sends a lot of messages.","hint":["baas","sturen","veel","bericht"],"note":"sturen → stem stuur: the long u is written double once -en is gone. Then + t for de baas."},{"nl":"Reis jij vaak voor je werk?","en":"Do you often travel for your work?","hint":["reizen","vaak","werk"],"note":"reizen → stem reis (z becomes s). Jij comes directly after the verb, so there is no t.","alt":["Reis je vaak voor je werk?"]},{"nl":"Hoe laat stop je vandaag?","en":"What time do you stop today?","hint":["hoe laat","stoppen","vandaag"],"note":"stoppen → stem stop, with one p. Je follows the verb, so the t is dropped: stop je.","alt":["Hoe laat stop jij vandaag?"]},{"nl":"Zij regelt alle afspraken voor ons.","en":"She arranges all the appointments for us.","hint":["regelen","alle","afspraak"],"note":"regelen → stem regel, + t for zij (she). The plural zij would be: zij regelen.","alt":["Ze regelt alle afspraken voor ons."]},{"nl":"In de pauze praat ik met mijn collega's.","en":"During the break I talk to my colleagues.","hint":["pauze","praten","collega"],"note":"praten → stem praat: the long a is doubled. The ik-form has no ending, wherever ik stands in the sentence.","alt":["Ik praat in de pauze met mijn collega's.","Tijdens de pauze praat ik met mijn collega's.","Ik praat tijdens de pauze met mijn collega's."]},{"nl":"Werkt u morgen ook thuis?","en":"Are you (formal) working from home tomorrow too?","hint":["werken","morgen","ook","thuis"],"note":"U keeps its t in a question. Only jij and je lose it after the verb."},{"nl":"Hij leest zijn berichten in de trein.","en":"He reads his messages on the train.","hint":["lezen","bericht","trein"],"note":"lezen → stem lees: z becomes s and the long e is doubled. Then + t for hij.","alt":["In de trein leest hij zijn berichten.","Hij leest in de trein zijn berichten."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Wij ___ om negen uur.","options":["begin","begint","beginnen"],"correct":2,"en":"We start at nine o'clock.","note":"Wij takes the infinitive: beginnen. Begin is the ik-form, begint the form for jij, u, hij and zij."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn collega ___ elke dag met de trein.","options":["reis","reist","reizen","reizt"],"correct":1,"en":"My colleague travels by train every day.","note":"The stem of reizen is reis, because a Dutch word cannot end in z. Mijn collega is a hij/zij-subject, so stem + t: reist."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ jij vandaag op kantoor?","options":["Werk","Werkt","Werken"],"correct":0,"en":"Are you working at the office today?","note":"Jij stands directly after the verb, so the t disappears: Werk jij? With hij or u it would stay: Werkt hij? Werkt u?"},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ elke ochtend koffie voor mijn collega's.","options":["mak","maakt","maak","maken"],"correct":2,"en":"I make coffee for my colleagues every morning.","note":"maken → stem maak: the a is long, so it is written double once -en is gone. Ik takes the bare stem."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De baas ___ (sturen) ons elke maandag een lijst met taken.","answers":["stuurt"],"en":"The boss sends us a list of tasks every Monday.","note":"The stem of sturen is stuur, with the long u doubled. De baas is a hij/zij-subject, so stem + t: stuurt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik ___ (lezen) eerst mijn berichten.","answers":["lees"],"en":"I read my messages first.","note":"lezen → lees: the z becomes s and the long e is doubled. Ik takes the stem without an ending."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hoe laat ___ (stoppen) jullie vandaag?","answers":["stoppen"],"en":"What time do you (plural) stop today?","note":"Jullie is plural, so the verb is the infinitive, also in a question: stoppen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Je ___ (praten) te veel in de pauze.","answers":["praat"],"en":"You talk too much during the break.","note":"The stem praat already ends in t, and a second t is never added: je praat, hij praat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op drukke dagen ___ (beginnen) ik om zeven uur.","answers":["begin"],"en":"On busy days I start at seven o'clock.","note":"Ik takes the bare stem wherever it stands in the sentence: begin, with one n and without t."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["we","vergaderen","om twee uur"],"answers":["Morgen vergaderen we om twee uur."],"en":"Tomorrow we have a meeting at two o'clock.","note":"The verb keeps its form wherever the subject stands: we takes the infinitive, vergaderen. (Why the verb comes before we is the subject of unit A2 · 3.)"},{"type":"order","words":["de vergadering","hoe","begint","laat"],"answers":["Hoe laat begint de vergadering?"],"en":"What time does the meeting start?","note":"After the question words comes the verb, and it agrees with de vergadering: stem + t, begint."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Change the subject to hij.","from":"Ik reis elke week naar Brussel.","answers":["Hij reist elke week naar Brussel."],"en":"He travels to Brussels every week.","note":"Hij needs stem + t. The stem stays reis, with s: reist, never reizt."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a question (start with the verb).","from":"Jij werkt vandaag thuis.","answers":["Werk jij vandaag thuis?","Werk je vandaag thuis?"],"en":"Are you working from home today?","note":"In the question jij comes after the verb, and then the t is dropped: Werk jij?"},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn collega werk op vrijdag thuis.","answers":["Mijn collega werkt op vrijdag thuis."],"en":"My colleague works from home on Fridays.","note":"Mijn collega is a hij/zij-subject and needs the t: werkt. Werk without t is only for ik, and for jij after the verb."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik leez elke ochtend mijn berichten.","answers":["Ik lees elke ochtend mijn berichten."],"en":"I read my messages every morning.","note":"A Dutch word never ends in z: the stem of lezen is lees. The z only comes back in the plural, wij lezen."}]},
  {"id":"a2-02","level":"A2","icon":"👨‍👩‍👧","title":"Family and who you are","grammar":"Zijn, hebben and other irregular present forms","summary":"zijn: ben, bent, is, zijn. hebben: heb, hebt, heeft, hebben. Short verbs: ik ga, hij gaat; ik doe; ik zie; ik kom, hij komt. A profession or an age takes zijn; honger, gelijk, haast and zin take hebben.","explain":"`Zijn` (to be) and `hebben` (to have) are the two most used verbs in Dutch, and both are irregular: their forms have to be learned by heart. A few very short verbs (`gaan`, `staan`, `slaan`, `doen`, `zien`) and `komen` are almost regular, but each has one thing to watch.\n\n### Zijn and hebben\n\n| | zijn | hebben |\n| ik | ben | heb |\n| jij / je | bent | hebt |\n| u | bent | hebt / heeft |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | is | heeft |\n| wij / we, jullie, zij / ze | zijn | hebben |\n\n> Ik ben de zus van Tom. — I am Tom's sister.\n> Mijn oom heeft drie kinderen. — My uncle has three children.\n> Wij zijn neef en nicht. — We are cousins.\n\n`U hebt` and `u heeft` are both right. As with every verb, `jij` or `je` directly after the verb loses the t: `Ben je getrouwd?`, `Heb jij kinderen?` But `Bent u getrouwd?`\n\n### The short verbs\n\n`Gaan` (to go), `staan` (to stand), `slaan` (to hit), `doen` (to do) and `zien` (to see) end in `-n`, not `-en`. Remove the `-n` to find the stem. The long a is written with one letter at the end of a word (`ik ga`) and with two before the t (`hij gaat`); it sounds the same.\n\n| | gaan | doen | zien |\n| ik | ga | doe | zie |\n| jij / je, u | gaat | doet | ziet |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | gaat | doet | ziet |\n| wij / we, jullie, zij / ze | gaan | doen | zien |\n\n`Staan` and `slaan` go like `gaan`: `ik sta, hij staat`; `ik sla, hij slaat`.\n\n> Ik ga vanavond naar mijn schoonmoeder. — I am going to my mother-in-law's this evening.\n> Wat doet je tante voor werk? — What does your aunt do for a living?\n> Zie je je neef vaak? — Do you see your cousin often?\n\n### Komen\n\n`Komen` has a long o in the infinitive and the plural, but a **short o** in the singular: `ik kom, jij komt, hij komt`, and `wij komen`. So not *ik koom, *hij koomt.\n\n> Mijn schoonmoeder komt uit Polen. — My mother-in-law comes from Poland.\n\n### What zijn is used for\n\n- **Profession and nationality: no article.** `Zij is arts`, `Mijn tante is verpleegkundige`, `Hij is Nederlander`. With an adjective the article comes back: `Zij is een goede arts`.\n- **Age.** `Ik ben dertig` or `Ik ben dertig jaar`. Never hebben: *Ik heb dertig jaar.\n\n### Fixed expressions with hebben\n\nWhere English says \"to be hungry\", Dutch says \"to have hunger\". These expressions take `hebben`, and the noun has no article:\n\n- `honger hebben` (to be hungry), `dorst hebben` (to be thirsty)\n- `gelijk hebben` (to be right), `haast hebben` (to be in a hurry)\n- `zin hebben in` (to feel like), `last hebben van` (to be bothered by, to suffer from)\n\n> Je hebt gelijk. — You are right.\n> Ik heb zin in koffie. — I feel like coffee.\n> Mijn oom heeft last van zijn rug. — My uncle has trouble with his back.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- `Hij heeft`, never *hij heb or *hij hebt. And `jij hebt`, never *jij heeft: only `u` can take both.\n- *Ik ben honger and *Ik ben gelijk are wrong: `Ik heb honger`, `Ik heb gelijk`.\n- Not *Zij is een arts but `Zij is arts`: a profession after `zijn` has no article.\n- Spell `ik ga`, `ik sta` with one a, and `hij gaat`, `hij staat` with two.\n- The t goes when `jij` or `je` follows the verb: `Ga je naar huis?`, `Doe jij dat?`, `Zie je het?`, `Kom je ook?`\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian leaves \"is\" out in the present: `Вона лікар`. Dutch never can: `Zij is arts`. And where Ukrainian says `У мене є брат` (\"at me there is a brother\"), Dutch makes the owner the subject of `hebben`: `Ik heb een broer`.\n\nThe modal verbs (`kunnen`, `moeten`, `mogen`, `willen`, `zullen`) are irregular too; they have their own unit, A2 · 11.","words":[{"nl":"gezin","art":"het","pl":"gezinnen","en":"family (parents and children)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"oom","art":"de","pl":"ooms","en":"uncle","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tante","art":"de","pl":"tantes","en":"aunt","pos":"n"},{"nl":"neef","art":"de","pl":"neven","en":"cousin (male) / nephew","pos":"n"},{"nl":"nicht","art":"de","pl":"nichten","en":"cousin (female) / niece","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kleinkind","art":"het","pl":"kleinkinderen","en":"grandchild","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schoonmoeder","art":"de","pl":"schoonmoeders","en":"mother-in-law","pos":"n"},{"nl":"arts","art":"de","pl":"artsen","en":"doctor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verpleegkundige","art":"de","pl":"verpleegkundigen","en":"nurse","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beroep","art":"het","pl":"beroepen","en":"profession / occupation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"getrouwd","en":"married","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"gescheiden","en":"divorced","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"aardig","en":"nice / kind","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"haast hebben","en":"to be in a hurry","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"gelijk hebben","en":"to be right","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"zin hebben in","en":"to feel like (something)","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik ben getrouwd en ik heb twee kinderen.","en":"I am married and I have two children.","hint":["zijn","getrouwd","hebben","kind"],"note":"The two ik-forms side by side: ben from zijn, heb from hebben. Neither has an ending.","alt":["Ik ben getrouwd en heb twee kinderen."]},{"nl":"Mijn tante is verpleegkundige in een groot ziekenhuis.","en":"My aunt is a nurse in a big hospital.","hint":["tante","zijn","verpleegkundige","ziekenhuis"],"note":"A profession after zijn has no article: is verpleegkundige, not is een verpleegkundige."},{"nl":"Heb jij veel neven en nichten?","en":"Do you have many cousins (male and female)?","hint":["hebben","veel","neef","nicht"],"note":"Jij hebt, but jij stands directly after the verb here, so the t is dropped: Heb jij?","alt":["Heb je veel neven en nichten?"]},{"nl":"Mijn oom heeft altijd haast.","en":"My uncle is always in a hurry.","hint":["oom","hebben","altijd","haast"],"note":"English uses to be, Dutch uses hebben: haast hebben. Mijn oom is a hij-subject, so heeft."},{"nl":"Mijn schoonmoeder is zeventig, maar ze werkt nog.","en":"My mother-in-law is seventy, but she still works.","hint":["schoonmoeder","zijn","zeventig","werken","nog"],"note":"Age takes zijn, never hebben: is zeventig. The word jaar may be added or left out.","alt":["Mijn schoonmoeder is zeventig, maar zij werkt nog.","Mijn schoonmoeder is zeventig jaar, maar ze werkt nog.","Mijn schoonmoeder is zeventig jaar, maar zij werkt nog.","Mijn schoonmoeder is zeventig, maar ze werkt nog steeds.","Mijn schoonmoeder is zeventig, maar zij werkt nog steeds."]},{"nl":"We gaan elke zondag naar de kleinkinderen.","en":"We go to the grandchildren every Sunday.","hint":["gaan","elke","zondag","kleinkind"],"note":"With we the verb is the infinitive, and the infinitive of this short verb is gaan.","alt":["Wij gaan elke zondag naar de kleinkinderen.","Elke zondag gaan we naar de kleinkinderen."]},{"nl":"Wat doet je neef voor werk?","en":"What does your cousin (male) do for a living?","hint":["wat","doen","neef","werk"],"note":"doen → stem doe, + t for je neef: doet. The subject is je neef, not je, so the t stays.","alt":["Wat doet jouw neef voor werk?"]},{"nl":"Mijn nicht is niet getrouwd, ze is gescheiden.","en":"My cousin (female) is not married, she is divorced.","hint":["nicht","zijn","getrouwd","gescheiden"],"note":"Is is the form of zijn for hij, zij and het. It can never be left out, not even in the second part.","alt":["Mijn nicht is niet getrouwd, zij is gescheiden."]},{"nl":"Hebben jullie zin in koffie?","en":"Do you (plural) feel like coffee?","hint":["hebben","zin","koffie"],"note":"Jullie takes the plural form hebben. To feel like something is zin hebben in, with hebben and without an article."},{"nl":"Komt u ook uit een groot gezin?","en":"Do you (formal) come from a big family too?","hint":["komen","ook","groot","gezin"],"note":"komen has a short o in the singular: komt. U keeps its t after the verb; only jij and je lose it."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn oom ___ drie kinderen.","options":["heb","hebt","heeft","hebben"],"correct":2,"en":"My uncle has three children.","note":"Mijn oom is a hij-subject, and the hij-form of hebben is heeft. Heb is for ik, hebt for jij and u."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Jullie ___ gelijk.","options":["zijn","heeft","hebben"],"correct":2,"en":"You (plural) are right.","note":"To be right is gelijk hebben, with hebben and not with zijn. Jullie is plural, so the form is hebben."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn nicht is ___ arts.","options":["een","het","–"],"correct":2,"en":"My cousin is a doctor.","note":"After zijn a profession has no article: Mijn nicht is arts. English needs 'a' here, Dutch does not."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ je zin in koffie?","options":["Heb","Hebt","Ben","Bent"],"correct":0,"en":"Do you feel like coffee?","note":"The expression is zin hebben in, so the verb is hebben. Je comes directly after the verb, so the t is dropped: Heb je?"},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wat ___ uw beroep?","options":["bent","is","zijn"],"correct":1,"en":"What is your (formal) occupation?","note":"The subject is uw beroep, one thing, so the verb is is. Bent would only be right if u itself were the subject: Wat bent u van beroep?"},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn tante ___ (gaan) elke week naar haar kleinkinderen.","answers":["gaat"],"en":"My aunt goes to her grandchildren every week.","note":"The stem of gaan is ga. Before the t of the hij/zij-form the long a is written double: gaat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wat ___ (doen) jij voor werk?","answers":["doe"],"en":"What do you do for a living?","note":"Jij doet, but here jij follows the verb, so the t is dropped: doe jij."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik ___ (zien) mijn neef bijna elke week.","answers":["zie"],"en":"I see my cousin almost every week.","note":"zien loses only its n: the stem is zie, and ik takes the bare stem."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mevrouw, ___ (hebben) u kinderen?","answers":["hebt","heeft"],"en":"Madam, do you have children?","note":"With u both hebt and heeft are right. The t stays after the verb, because only jij and je lose it."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op de foto ___ (staan) mijn oom naast mijn tante.","answers":["staat"],"en":"In the photo my uncle is standing next to my aunt.","note":"The subject is mijn oom, a hij-subject: stem sta + t, and the a is doubled before the t: staat."},{"type":"order","words":["je","een kop koffie","heb","zin in"],"answers":["Heb je zin in een kop koffie?"],"en":"Do you feel like a cup of coffee?","note":"Heb without t is only possible when je follows the verb, so this must be a question: Heb je …? In a statement it would be Je hebt."},{"type":"order","lead":"Mijn tante","words":["haar rug","last van","heeft"],"answers":["Mijn tante heeft last van haar rug."],"en":"My aunt has trouble with her back.","note":"The expression is last hebben van: the verb heeft comes straight after the subject, then last van and what causes the trouble."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Change the subject to hij.","from":"Ik heb vandaag veel haast.","answers":["Hij heeft vandaag veel haast."],"en":"He is in a great hurry today.","note":"The hij-form of hebben is heeft, not heb or hebt."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a question (start with the verb).","from":"Jij bent de oom van Emma.","answers":["Ben jij de oom van Emma?","Ben je de oom van Emma?"],"en":"Are you Emma's uncle?","note":"In the question jij comes after the verb, and bent loses its t: Ben jij?"},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb dertig jaar.","answers":["Ik ben dertig jaar.","Ik ben dertig jaar oud.","Ik ben dertig."],"en":"I am thirty years old.","note":"Dutch gives an age with zijn, never with hebben: Ik ben dertig jaar. Jaar may be left out, and oud may be added."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn aardige buurman koomt uit een groot gezin.","answers":["Mijn aardige buurman komt uit een groot gezin."],"en":"My nice neighbour comes from a big family.","note":"komen has a short o in the singular, written with one o: ik kom, hij komt. The long o is only in komen."}]},
  {"id":"a2-03","level":"A2","icon":"📅","title":"My week","grammar":"Word order: the verb in second place","summary":"In a main clause the conjugated verb is always the second element. If something else comes first (a time, a place, an object), the subject follows the verb: Morgen werk ik. En, maar, want and of do not count.","explain":"In a Dutch main clause the conjugated verb (the form with the ending: `werk`, `werkt`, `heeft`) always stands in **second place**. This is the most important rule of Dutch word order. English almost always puts the subject before the verb, and Ukrainian moves words around freely; Dutch keeps the verb in its place and lets something else go first. When the subject then moves behind the verb, that is called *inversion* (de inversie).\n\n### The verb is element number two\n\nThe first place can hold the subject, but also a time, a place or an object. Whatever stands first, the verb comes next. If the first place is not the subject, the subject moves to the place **directly after the verb**.\n\n| 1 | 2: verb | subject | the rest |\n| Ik | zing | – | op dinsdag in een koor. |\n| Op dinsdag | zing | ik | in een koor. |\n| Dat lied | ken | ik | goed. |\n\n> Ik wandel elke zondag met mijn buurvrouw. — I go for a walk with my neighbour every Sunday.\n> Elke zondag wandel ik met mijn buurvrouw. — Every Sunday I go for a walk with my neighbour.\n> Dat lied ken ik goed. — I know that song well.\n\nThe meaning hardly changes. What stands first is the starting point of what you say: often the time, or something you were already talking about.\n\n### What counts as one element\n\nCount **elements**, not words. An element is a group of words that belongs together and moves as a whole: a time phrase, a place with its preposition, or the subject with everything that belongs to it.\n\n> Volgende week woensdag ben ik de hele dag vrij. — On Wednesday next week I am free all day.\n> In de zomer logeren mijn ouders bij ons. — In the summer my parents stay with us.\n> Mijn zus en ik zingen in hetzelfde koor. — My sister and I sing in the same choir.\n\nThere is only **one** element before the verb, never two:\n\n- *Morgen ik werk is wrong: `Morgen werk ik` or `Ik werk morgen`.\n- *Morgen om acht uur ik werk is wrong too. `Morgen om acht uur` is one element, so the verb follows it: `Morgen om acht uur werk ik`.\n\n### Jij after the verb\n\nYou know from unit A2 · 1 that the verb loses its t when `jij` or `je` comes directly after it. Inversion is exactly that situation: `jij werkt`, but `zaterdag werk jij`. `Hij`, `zij` and `u` keep the t: `Zaterdag werkt hij`.\n\n> Zaterdag werk je tot zes uur. — On Saturday you work until six.\n\nFor every other subject the verb form stays the same, wherever the subject stands: `ik zing`, `op dinsdag zing ik`, never *zingt ik.\n\n### The middle of the sentence\n\nWords such as `vaak`, `altijd`, `nooit`, `ook` and `af en toe` come **after** the verb, unless they take the first place themselves (`Vaak wandel ik`). In English they can stand between the subject and the verb (*I often walk*), in Dutch never: `Ik wandel vaak`.\n\nWhen a time and a place both stand after the verb, the time usually comes first: `Ik ben morgen thuis`, `We wandelen zondag in de duinen`. The other order sounds like something you add as an afterthought.\n\n> We wandelen zondag in de duinen. — We are going for a walk in the dunes on Sunday.\n\nWhen there are two verbs, only the conjugated one stands second; the infinitive goes to the end: `Zaterdag ga ik zwemmen` (more in units A2 · 11 and A2 · 17).\n\n### En, maar, want, of\n\nThese four words join two sentences and do not count as an element. After them you count again from the start: the next element is number one.\n\n> Ik werk doordeweeks, maar zaterdag ben ik vrij. — I work on weekdays, but on Saturday I am free.\n> Ik blijf thuis, want ik ben moe. — I am staying at home, because I am tired.\n\nWords like `daarna`, `dan` and `eerst` are different: they **do** count, so the verb comes right after them: `Daarna ga ik naar huis`. (Joining sentences is the subject of unit A2 · 22.)\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Something first, then the subject: *Morgen ik heb een dienst → `Morgen heb ik een dienst`.\n- A long element is still one element: *Volgende week zaterdag wij hebben vrij → `Volgende week zaterdag hebben wij vrij`.\n- No adverb between subject and verb: *Ik vaak zing → `Ik zing vaak`.\n- `daarna` counts, `en` does not: *Daarna ik ga naar huis → `Daarna ga ik naar huis`.\n- Jij after the verb: *Zaterdag werkt jij → `Zaterdag werk jij`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian word order is free, because the endings show who does what: `Завтра я працюю` and `Я завтра працюю` are both fine. Dutch nouns have no case endings, so Dutch relies on position, and the verb is fixed in second place. `Завтра я працюю` is `Morgen werk ik`, never *Morgen ik werk.","words":[{"nl":"rooster","art":"het","pl":"roosters","en":"schedule / timetable","pos":"n"},{"nl":"dienst","art":"de","pl":"diensten","en":"shift (at work)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kapper","art":"de","pl":"kappers","en":"hairdresser","pos":"n"},{"nl":"koor","art":"het","pl":"koren","en":"choir","pos":"n"},{"nl":"repetitie","art":"de","pl":"repetities","en":"rehearsal","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitje","art":"het","pl":"uitjes","en":"outing / day out","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wandelen","past":"wandelde","perf":"heeft/is gewandeld","en":"to walk / to go for a walk","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zingen","past":"zong","perf":"heeft gezongen","en":"to sing","pos":"v"},{"nl":"oefenen","past":"oefende","perf":"heeft geoefend","en":"to practise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"logeren","past":"logeerde","perf":"heeft gelogeerd","en":"to stay (with someone) / to stay over","pos":"v"},{"nl":"klussen","past":"kluste","perf":"heeft geklust","en":"to do odd jobs / to do DIY","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vrij","en":"free / off (not working)","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"doordeweeks","en":"on weekdays","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"daarna","en":"after that / afterwards","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"af en toe","en":"now and then","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"om de week","en":"every other week","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Mijn rooster verandert elke week.","en":"My schedule changes every week.","hint":["rooster","veranderen","elke week"],"note":"The subject mijn rooster stands first and the verb verandert comes second. This is the plain order, the same as in English.","alt":["Elke week verandert mijn rooster."]},{"nl":"Op dinsdag zing ik in een koor.","en":"On Tuesdays I sing in a choir.","hint":["dinsdag","zingen","koor"],"note":"Op dinsdag fills the first place, so the verb zing comes second and ik follows it. The ik-form stays zing, wherever ik stands.","alt":["Ik zing op dinsdag in een koor."]},{"nl":"Volgende week donderdag heb ik een afspraak bij de kapper.","en":"On Thursday next week I have an appointment at the hairdresser's.","hint":["volgende week","donderdag","afspraak","kapper"],"note":"Volgende week donderdag is three words but one element, so heb is still the second element and ik comes directly after it.","alt":["Ik heb volgende week donderdag een afspraak bij de kapper.","Donderdag volgende week heb ik een afspraak bij de kapper."]},{"nl":"In het weekend wandelen we vaak in het bos.","en":"At the weekend we often go for a walk in the woods.","hint":["weekend","wandelen","vaak","bos"],"note":"In het weekend is the first element, so wandelen comes second and we follows. Vaak stands after the verb, not before it as in English.","alt":["We wandelen in het weekend vaak in het bos.","We wandelen vaak in het weekend in het bos."]},{"nl":"Om de week heb ik een late dienst.","en":"Every other week I have a late shift.","hint":["om de week","hebben","laat","dienst"],"note":"Om de week is one element in first place. Then comes the verb heb, and then the subject ik.","alt":["Ik heb om de week een late dienst."]},{"nl":"Na de repetitie drink je nog iets met de anderen.","en":"After the rehearsal you have a drink with the others.","hint":["na","repetitie","drinken","iets","ander"],"note":"Na de repetitie stands first, so je comes directly after the verb. That is why drinkt loses its t: drink je.","alt":["Na de repetitie drink jij nog iets met de anderen.","Je drinkt na de repetitie nog iets met de anderen.","Jij drinkt na de repetitie nog iets met de anderen.","Na de repetitie drink je iets met de anderen.","Na de repetitie drink jij iets met de anderen.","Je drinkt na de repetitie iets met de anderen.","Jij drinkt na de repetitie iets met de anderen."]},{"nl":"Volgend weekend logeert mijn zus bij ons.","en":"Next weekend my sister is staying with us.","hint":["volgend","weekend","logeren","zus"],"note":"A noun subject goes behind the verb just like a pronoun: logeert mijn zus. The verb still agrees with mijn zus: stem + t.","alt":["Mijn zus logeert volgend weekend bij ons."]},{"nl":"Af en toe klust mijn man in de garage.","en":"Now and then my husband does odd jobs in the garage.","hint":["af en toe","klussen","man","garage"],"note":"Af en toe is three words but one element. After it comes the verb klust, then the subject mijn man.","alt":["Mijn man klust af en toe in de garage."]},{"nl":"Ik werk doordeweeks, maar in het weekend ben ik vrij.","en":"I work on weekdays, but at the weekend I am free.","hint":["werken","doordeweeks","maar","weekend","vrij"],"note":"Maar does not count as an element. After maar, in het weekend is number one, so ben comes next and then ik.","alt":["Doordeweeks werk ik, maar in het weekend ben ik vrij.","Ik werk doordeweeks, maar ik ben in het weekend vrij.","Doordeweeks werk ik, maar ik ben in het weekend vrij."]},{"nl":"Dat lied oefenen we al drie weken.","en":"We have been practising that song for three weeks.","hint":["lied","oefenen","al","week"],"note":"The object dat lied can stand first, because you were already talking about it. The verb oefenen stays second and we follows. Al + present tense says how long something has been going on.","alt":["We oefenen dat lied al drie weken."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Morgen ___ naar de kapper.","options":["ik ga","ga ik","gaat ik"],"correct":1,"en":"Tomorrow I am going to the hairdresser's.","note":"Morgen takes the first place, so the verb comes second and ik follows it. The ik-form stays ga: never gaat ik."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik werk tot vier uur en daarna ___ naar huis.","options":["ik fiets","fiets ik","fietst ik"],"correct":1,"en":"I work until four and after that I cycle home.","note":"En does not count, but daarna does: it is the first element of the second sentence. So the verb fiets comes next, and then ik."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik blijf vanavond thuis, want ___ moe.","options":["ben ik","ik ben","ik is"],"correct":1,"en":"I am staying at home tonight, because I am tired.","note":"Want does not count as an element. After want the subject ik is number one, so the order is the normal one: ik ben."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Elke zomer ___ (logeren) mijn ouders drie weken bij ons.","answers":["logeren"],"en":"Every summer my parents stay with us for three weeks.","note":"The subject stands after the verb, but the verb still agrees with it: mijn ouders is plural, so the infinitive logeren."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Na het werk ___ (wandelen) ik een half uur in het park.","answers":["wandel"],"en":"After work I go for a walk in the park for half an hour.","note":"Na het werk is the first element, so the verb comes second and ik after it. Ik takes the bare stem: wandel."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Volgende maand ___ (hebben) ons koor een uitje naar Gent.","answers":["heeft"],"en":"Next month our choir has an outing to Ghent.","note":"Volgende maand is the first element, then the verb, then the subject ons koor. That is one choir, so heeft."},{"type":"order","lead":"Volgende week dinsdag","words":["ga","ik","naar de tandarts"],"answers":["Volgende week dinsdag ga ik naar de tandarts."],"en":"On Tuesday next week I am going to the dentist.","note":"Volgende week dinsdag is one element, so the verb ga follows it at once and ik comes next."},{"type":"order","lead":"In het weekend","words":["vaak","we","wandelen","in de duinen"],"answers":["In het weekend wandelen we vaak in de duinen."],"en":"At the weekend we often go for a walk in the dunes.","note":"After the first element come the verb and then the subject. Vaak stands after the subject, and the place goes last."},{"type":"order","lead":"Dat lied","words":["we","elke week","oefenen"],"answers":["Dat lied oefenen we elke week."],"en":"We practise that song every week.","note":"The object dat lied stands first, so the verb oefenen comes second and the subject we directly after it."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen om acht uur","words":["de repetitie","begint","in de kerk"],"answers":["Morgen om acht uur begint de repetitie in de kerk."],"en":"Tomorrow at eight o'clock the rehearsal starts in the church.","note":"Morgen om acht uur is one element, so begint comes right after it and the subject de repetitie follows. Never *Morgen om acht uur de repetitie begint."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Op vrijdag.","from":"Ik ga op vrijdag altijd naar de kapper.","answers":["Op vrijdag ga ik altijd naar de kapper."],"en":"On Fridays I always go to the hairdresser's.","note":"Op vrijdag takes the first place, the verb ga stays second, and ik moves behind it. Altijd stays after the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with In het weekend.","from":"Jij werkt nooit in het weekend.","answers":["In het weekend werk jij nooit.","In het weekend werk je nooit."],"en":"You never work at the weekend.","note":"Jij now comes directly after the verb, so werkt loses its t: werk jij."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with maar.","from":"Ik werk op zaterdag. Op zondag ben ik vrij.","answers":["Ik werk op zaterdag, maar op zondag ben ik vrij.","Ik werk op zaterdag, maar ik ben op zondag vrij."],"en":"I work on Saturdays, but on Sundays I am free.","note":"Maar does not count as an element, so the second sentence keeps its own order: op zondag is first and ben second."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Morgen ik heb een late dienst.","answers":["Morgen heb ik een late dienst.","Ik heb morgen een late dienst."],"en":"Tomorrow I have a late shift.","note":"Only one element goes before the verb. Morgen is already the first element, so heb must come next and ik after it."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik vaak wandel na het eten.","answers":["Ik wandel vaak na het eten.","Ik wandel na het eten vaak.","Na het eten wandel ik vaak.","Vaak wandel ik na het eten."],"en":"I often go for a walk after dinner.","note":"The verb is the second element, so nothing goes between the subject and the verb. Vaak comes after the verb, not before it as in English."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Op zondag wandelt jij met de hond.","answers":["Op zondag wandel jij met de hond.","Op zondag wandel je met de hond.","Jij wandelt op zondag met de hond.","Je wandelt op zondag met de hond."],"en":"On Sundays you walk the dog.","note":"The word order is right, but jij now stands directly after the verb, and then the t disappears: wandel jij."}]},
  {"id":"a2-04","level":"A2","icon":"❓","title":"Getting to know someone","grammar":"Questions: yes/no questions and question words","summary":"Yes/no question: verb first, then the subject (Woon je hier?). With a question word: question word, verb, subject (Waar woon je?). Welke with de-words and plurals, welk with het-words. Jij after the verb loses its t.","explain":"Dutch makes a question by changing the word order: the verb moves in front of the subject. There is no helper verb like English *do*. There are two kinds of question, and in both the subject comes **directly after the verb**.\n\n### Yes/no questions: the verb first\n\nA question that you answer with ja or nee starts with the conjugated verb. Then comes the subject, then the rest.\n\n> Woon je hier al lang? — Have you lived here long?\n> Spreekt u Engels? — Do you (formal) speak English?\n> Zijn jullie hier ook nieuw? — Are you (plural) new here too?\n\nJij and je after the verb make it lose its t, as in unit A2 · 1: `jij woont`, but `Woon jij hier?` `Hij` and `u` keep the t: `Woont hij hier?`, `Woont u hier?`\n\n### Questions with a question word\n\nThe question word goes first, then the verb, then the subject. It is the rule of unit A2 · 3: the question word is element number one, the verb number two.\n\n| question word | meaning | example |\n| wie | who | Wie is dat? |\n| wat | what | Wat doe je? |\n| waar | where | Waar woon je? |\n| wanneer | when | Wanneer begin je? |\n| waarom | why | Waarom lach je? |\n| hoe | how | Hoe gaat het? |\n| hoeveel | how much, how many | Hoeveel kinderen heeft u? |\n| hoe laat | what time | Hoe laat is het? |\n| hoe lang | how long | Hoe lang blijf je? |\n| hoe vaak | how often | Hoe vaak sport je? |\n| hoe oud | how old | Hoe oud is hij? |\n\n> Hoe lang woont u al in Nederland? — How long have you (formal) lived in the Netherlands?\n\n### Welk or welke, and wat voor\n\n`Welke` (which) goes with de-words and with every plural. `Welk` goes only with a het-word in the singular.\n\n> Welke taal spreek je thuis? — Which language do you speak at home? (de taal)\n> Uit welk land kom je? — Which country are you from? (het land)\n\n`Wat voor` asks what kind: `Wat voor werk doe je?` With a singular noun you can count, `een` is often added: `Wat voor (een) auto heb je?` With plurals and with nouns you cannot count there is no een: `Wat voor boeken lees je?`\n\n### Waar … vandaan and waar … naartoe\n\nThese question words are split: `waar` goes first, and `vandaan` (from) or `naartoe` (to) goes to the **end**. Instead of naartoe you can also say `heen`.\n\n> Waar kom je vandaan? — Where are you from?\n> Waar gaan jullie in de zomer naartoe? — Where are you (plural) going in the summer?\n\n### Wie or wat as the subject\n\nWhen `wie` or `wat` is itself the subject, the verb follows it directly, and it is normally singular, even if the answer could be several people.\n\n> Wie woont hier? — Who lives here?\n\nWhen the speaker expects several people, a plural verb is also correct, often with `er` and `allemaal`: `Wie komen er allemaal?` The singular is always right.\n\nIn `Wie zijn die mensen?` the subject is `die mensen`, so the verb is plural.\n\n### A preposition goes in front\n\nEnglish puts the preposition at the end (*Who do you live with?*). Dutch puts it in front of `wie`: `Met wie woon je?`, `Van wie is die jas?` For things, Dutch usually says `waarmee`, `waarover` and so on (more in B1).\n\n### Answering: ja, nee and jawel\n\nTo say yes to a negative question or statement, and so contradict it, Dutch uses `jawel`. `Ja` alone sounds unclear, and `nee` agrees with the negative.\n\n> Ben je niet moe? Jawel, heel erg. — Aren't you tired? Yes, I am, very.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The verb comes before the subject: *Waar jij woont? → `Waar woon jij?`\n- The t goes: *Woont jij hier? → `Woon jij hier?`\n- No *do*: *Doe je spreken Engels? → `Spreek je Engels?`\n- *Welk taal → `Welke taal`; *Welke land → `Welk land`.\n- *Waar kom je van? → `Waar kom je vandaan?`\n- *Wie woon je mee? → `Met wie woon je?`\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian can make a question just with the voice or with `чи`: `Ти тут живеш?` Dutch needs the word order: `Woon jij hier?` And where Ukrainian says just `Звідки ти?`, Dutch needs a verb: `Waar kom je vandaan?`","words":[{"nl":"achternaam","art":"de","pl":"achternamen","en":"surname","pos":"n"},{"nl":"woonplaats","art":"de","pl":"woonplaatsen","en":"place of residence","pos":"n"},{"nl":"nationaliteit","art":"de","pl":"nationaliteiten","en":"nationality","pos":"n"},{"nl":"leeftijd","art":"de","pl":"leeftijden","en":"age","pos":"n"},{"nl":"moedertaal","art":"de","pl":"moedertalen","en":"mother tongue","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kennis","art":"de","pl":"kennissen","en":"acquaintance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gesprek","art":"het","pl":"gesprekken","en":"conversation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"heten","past":"heette","perf":"heeft geheten","en":"to be called","pos":"v"},{"nl":"spellen","past":"spelde","perf":"heeft gespeld","en":"to spell","pos":"v"},{"nl":"bedoelen","past":"bedoelde","perf":"heeft bedoeld","en":"to mean","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontmoeten","past":"ontmoette","perf":"heeft ontmoet","en":"to meet","pos":"v"},{"nl":"geboren","en":"born","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"eigenlijk","en":"actually / really","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"vandaan","en":"from (waar … vandaan: where from)","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"naartoe","en":"to (waar … naartoe: where to)","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"aangenaam","en":"pleased to meet you","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Hoe spel je je achternaam?","en":"How do you spell your surname?","hint":["hoe","spellen","achternaam"],"note":"Question word, verb, subject. Je comes directly after the verb, so the t of spelt disappears: spel je.","alt":["Hoe spel jij je achternaam?","Hoe spel je jouw achternaam?","Hoe spel jij jouw achternaam?"]},{"nl":"Waar kom je eigenlijk vandaan?","en":"Where are you actually from?","hint":["waar","komen","eigenlijk","vandaan"],"note":"Waar goes first and vandaan goes to the very end. Between them the normal question order: verb, subject, the rest.","alt":["Waar kom jij eigenlijk vandaan?"]},{"nl":"Is Oekraïens uw moedertaal?","en":"Is Ukrainian your (formal) mother tongue?","hint":["zijn","Oekraïens","moedertaal"],"note":"A yes/no question starts with the verb: is. The subject Oekraïens follows it directly.","alt":["Is uw moedertaal Oekraïens?"]},{"nl":"Is zij een vriendin of een kennis?","en":"Is she a friend or an acquaintance?","hint":["zijn","vriendin","kennis"],"note":"The verb comes first and the subject zij second. Of only joins the two choices and changes nothing in the order.","alt":["Is ze een vriendin of een kennis?"]},{"nl":"Welke talen spreek je thuis?","en":"Which languages do you speak at home?","hint":["welke","taal","spreken","thuis"],"note":"Talen is a plural, and every plural takes welke. Je after the verb: spreek, without t.","alt":["Welke talen spreek jij thuis?"]},{"nl":"In welk land ben je geboren?","en":"In which country were you born?","hint":["welk","land","zijn","geboren"],"note":"Land is a het-word in the singular, so welk without e. The preposition in goes in front of the question word.","alt":["In welk land ben jij geboren?"]},{"nl":"Wat voor werk doe je?","en":"What kind of work do you do?","hint":["wat voor","werk","doen"],"note":"Wat voor asks what kind. Werk is a noun you cannot count, so there is no een.","alt":["Wat voor werk doe jij?"]},{"nl":"Wie spreekt hier Nederlands?","en":"Who speaks Dutch here?","hint":["wie","spreken","hier","Nederlands"],"note":"Wie is the subject here, so the verb follows it directly and is singular: spreekt, even if several people may answer.","alt":["Wie spreekt er hier Nederlands?"]},{"nl":"Aangenaam, ik ben Olena. Hoe heet jij?","en":"Pleased to meet you, I am Olena. What is your name?","hint":["aangenaam","zijn","hoe","heten"],"note":"Hoe heet jij? is how Dutch asks for a name: literally 'how are you called'. Heten has a stem ending in t, so heet jij and jij heet look the same.","alt":["Aangenaam, ik ben Olena. Hoe heet je?","Aangenaam, ik heet Olena. Hoe heet jij?","Aangenaam, ik heet Olena. Hoe heet je?"]},{"nl":"Woon je hier niet? Jawel, al drie jaar.","en":"Don't you live here? Yes, I do: for three years now.","hint":["wonen","hier","jawel","jaar"],"note":"The question starts with the verb: woon je, without t. To say yes to a negative question you use jawel, not ja.","alt":["Woon jij hier niet? Jawel, al drie jaar."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Hoe ___ eigenlijk?","options":["jullie heten","heten jullie","heet jullie"],"correct":1,"en":"What are your (plural) names, actually?","note":"After the question word comes the verb, then the subject: heten jullie. Only jij and je make the verb lose its t; jullie keeps the infinitive."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Uit ___ land komt uw man?","options":["welke","welk","wat"],"correct":1,"en":"Which country is your (formal) husband from?","note":"Land is a het-word and singular, so welk without e. Welke is for de-words and plurals; wat cannot stand before a noun like this."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wie ___ de nieuwe buurvrouw al?","options":["ken","ken je","kent"],"correct":2,"en":"Who knows the new neighbour already?","note":"Wie is the subject, so the verb follows it directly in the singular hij-form: kent. With ken je, wie would no longer be the subject."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ben je niet moe? ___, ik ben heel moe.","options":["Nee","Jawel","Geen"],"correct":1,"en":"Aren't you tired? Yes, I am very tired.","note":"Jawel contradicts a negative question: yes, I am. Nee would agree with niet and mean 'no, I am not tired'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ is uw woonplaats?","answers":["Wat"],"en":"What is your (formal) place of residence?","note":"Wat asks for a piece of information, like a name or a place on a form. Welke only stands in front of a noun: welke stad."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ voor muziek vind je mooi?","answers":["Wat"],"en":"What kind of music do you like?","note":"Wat voor means 'what kind of'. Muziek cannot be counted, so there is no een after wat voor."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wat ___ (bedoelen) je precies?","answers":["bedoel"],"en":"What exactly do you mean?","note":"Je comes directly after the verb, so the t disappears: bedoel je. With hij it stays: Wat bedoelt hij?"},{"type":"gap","nl":"Met ___ heb je morgen een gesprek?","answers":["wie"],"en":"Who are you having a conversation with tomorrow?","note":"For a person after a preposition Dutch uses wie, and the preposition goes in front of it: met wie. Never *wie … met at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Waar","words":["ga","je","in de zomer","naartoe"],"answers":["Waar ga je in de zomer naartoe?","Waar ga je naartoe in de zomer?"],"en":"Where are you going in the summer?","note":"Waar first, then the verb and the subject. Naartoe normally closes the sentence; in speech the time can also follow it."},{"type":"order","lead":"Waar","words":["jullie","ontmoeten","elkaar","meestal"],"answers":["Waar ontmoeten jullie elkaar meestal?"],"en":"Where do you (plural) usually meet?","note":"Question word, verb, subject. A short word like elkaar comes right after the subject, before meestal."},{"type":"order","words":["wonen","hoe lang","uw buren","al","in deze straat"],"answers":["Hoe lang wonen uw buren al in deze straat?"],"en":"How long have your (formal) neighbours lived in this street?","note":"Hoe lang is one question word and stands first. The verb wonen comes second and agrees with the plural subject uw buren."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Ask where he is from, with waar … vandaan.","from":"Hij komt uit Polen.","answers":["Waar komt hij vandaan?"],"en":"Where is he from?","note":"Waar takes the first place and vandaan goes to the end. Hij keeps the t: komt hij."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Ask the same thing in everyday Dutch, with hoe oud.","from":"Wat is uw leeftijd?","answers":["Hoe oud bent u?"],"en":"How old are you (formal)?","note":"Hoe oud is the question word, then the verb and the subject: bent u. Wat is uw leeftijd? is what a form says."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Waar jij woont?","answers":["Waar woon jij?","Waar woon je?"],"en":"Where do you live?","note":"After the question word comes the verb, then the subject. And jij after the verb loses the t: woon jij."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Welk nationaliteit heeft hij?","answers":["Welke nationaliteit heeft hij?"],"en":"What nationality is he?","note":"Nationaliteit is a de-word, like every word in -teit, so welke. Welk is only for het-words in the singular."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Woont jij hier al lang?","answers":["Woon jij hier al lang?","Woon je hier al lang?"],"en":"Have you lived here long?","note":"In a yes/no question jij stands directly after the verb, and then the verb loses its t: woon jij."}]},
  {"id":"a2-05","level":"A2","icon":"🏠","title":"At home","grammar":"De and het: the articles","summary":"Every noun is a de-word or a het-word; een goes with both. Every plural takes de. No article with an indefinite plural, with uncountables in general or with a job after zijn. Learn each noun with its article.","explain":"Dutch has two words for *the*: `de` and `het`. Which one a noun takes depends on its gender: most nouns are de-words, the rest are het-words. The word for *a* is `een` for both. Ukrainian has no articles at all, so this is a habit to build from the start.\n\n### The forms\n\n| | de-word | het-word |\n| the (one) | de kamer | het raam |\n| a (one) | een kamer | een raam |\n| the (more) | de kamers | de ramen |\n| (more, not known) | kamers | ramen |\n\n- In the **plural** every noun takes `de`: `het raam`, but `de ramen`.\n- `Een` has no plural: `een kamer`, but `kamers`.\n\n> De kamer is groot. — The room is big.\n> Het raam is open. — The window is open.\n> De ramen zijn schoon. — The windows are clean.\n\n### When to use de/het, een or nothing\n\n- `de` or `het`: something known, already mentioned, or the only one there is: `het dak van ons huis`.\n- `een`: one thing that is new to the listener: `We hebben een zolder.`\n- **nothing**: a plural that is not known (`Ik koop stoelen`), something you cannot count, said in general (`Hout is duur`), and a job after `zijn` or `worden` (`Zij is kapper`). With an adjective a job does get een: `een goede kapper`.\n\n> We kopen nieuwe stoelen. — We are buying new chairs.\n> Mijn buurman is schilder. — My neighbour is a painter.\n\n### Rules that help: het\n\n- All **diminutives** (*verkleinwoorden*), even of de-words: `de kamer`, but `het kamertje`.\n- An **infinitive** used as a noun: `het eten`, `het koken`.\n- **Languages**: `het Nederlands`, `het Oekraïens`.\n- **Metals**: `het goud`, `het ijzer`.\n- Words in **-isme, -ment, -sel, -um**: `het toerisme`, `het appartement`, `het raadsel`, `het museum`. Exception: `de datum`. This is about the suffix -sel (`raadsel` from raden): in `de mossel` and `de oksel` the letters are not a suffix.\n- Most two-syllable words starting with the unstressed prefix **ge-, be-, ver-**: `het gebouw`, `het begin`, `het verhaal`, `het behang`. Exception: `de verkoop`. Where the first syllable is stressed it is no prefix, and the rule does not apply: `de beker`, `de gevel`.\n\n> Het kamertje is klein. — The little room is small.\n> Na het eten zitten we op de bank. — After dinner we sit on the sofa.\n\n### Rules that help: de\n\n- **People and jobs**: `de man`, `de buurvrouw`, `de kapper`. But `het kind`, and `het meisje` (a diminutive).\n- Words in **-ing, -heid, -ie, -tie, -teit**: `de woning`, `de gezondheid`, `de familie`, `de informatie`, `de universiteit`.\n- Words in **-erij** and most in **-ij**: `de bakkerij`, `de partij`. Exception: `het schilderij`.\n- Persons in **-er, -aar, -eur**: `de bakker`, `de leraar`, `de chauffeur`. (Things in -er can be het: `het nummer`.)\n- **Fruit, trees and flowers**: `de appel`, `de eik`, `de tulp`; **rivers and mountains**; **letters and numbers**: `de a`, `de drie`.\n\nAbout two thirds of all nouns are de-words. For the rest there is no rule: `het plafond`, `het raam`, `het huis`. That is why the best advice is: **always learn a noun with its article**. In a compound the **last part** decides: `het bad` + `de kamer` = `de badkamer`; `de keuken` + `het raam` = `het keukenraam`.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nDe or het decides other words too: the ending of an adjective (`een grote kamer`, but `een groot raam`), `deze/dit`, `die/dat`, `onze/ons` and `welke/welk`. You will meet each of these in its own unit.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The plural is always de: *het ramen → `de ramen`.\n- No een before a plural: *een stoelen → `stoelen`.\n- A diminutive is always het: *de kamertje → `het kamertje`.\n- A job after zijn has no article: `Hij is kapper`. (`Hij is de kapper` means he is the hairdresser you both know.)\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nThe Ukrainian gender does not help: `місто` is neuter, but `de stad` is a de-word. Think of `de/het` as *this one we know*, `een` as *one, new*, and nothing as *things in general*.","words":[{"nl":"woonkamer","art":"de","pl":"woonkamers","en":"living room","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zolder","art":"de","pl":"zolders","en":"attic","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gang","art":"de","pl":"gangen","en":"hall / corridor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"trap","art":"de","pl":"trappen","en":"stairs / staircase","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vloer","art":"de","pl":"vloeren","en":"floor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"muur","art":"de","pl":"muren","en":"wall","pos":"n"},{"nl":"plafond","art":"het","pl":"plafonds","en":"ceiling","pos":"n"},{"nl":"raam","art":"het","pl":"ramen","en":"window","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bank","art":"de","pl":"banken","en":"sofa / couch","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kussen","art":"het","pl":"kussens","en":"cushion / pillow","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gordijn","art":"het","pl":"gordijnen","en":"curtain","pos":"n"},{"nl":"meubel","art":"het","pl":"meubels","en":"piece of furniture","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schilderij","art":"het","pl":"schilderijen","en":"painting","pos":"n"},{"nl":"behang","art":"het","pl":"","en":"wallpaper","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verwarming","art":"de","pl":"","en":"heating","pos":"n"},{"nl":"licht","en":"light / bright","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De woonkamer is licht en het raam is groot.","en":"The living room is bright and the window is big.","hint":["woonkamer","licht","raam","groot"],"note":"Woonkamer ends in kamer, a de-word, and the last part of a compound decides: de woonkamer. Raam is a het-word you simply have to learn."},{"nl":"Het schilderij hangt boven de bank.","en":"The painting hangs above the sofa.","hint":["schilderij","hangen","boven","bank"],"note":"Most words in -ij are de-words, but schilderij is the well-known exception: het schilderij."},{"nl":"We kopen gordijnen voor de slaapkamer.","en":"We are buying curtains for the bedroom.","hint":["kopen","gordijn","slaapkamer"],"note":"Gordijnen is a plural that is not known yet, so it has no article. One curtain would be een gordijn, and the curtain het gordijn.","alt":["Wij kopen gordijnen voor de slaapkamer."]},{"nl":"De trap naar de zolder is erg steil.","en":"The stairs to the attic are very steep.","hint":["trap","zolder","steil"],"note":"English says stairs, but Dutch de trap is one word in the singular, so the verb is is. Trap and zolder are both de-words.","alt":["De trap naar de zolder is heel steil."]},{"nl":"Het plafond in de badkamer is wit.","en":"The ceiling in the bathroom is white.","hint":["plafond","badkamer","wit"],"note":"Plafond is a het-word without a rule to help. Badkamer is de, because kamer is the last part."},{"nl":"Is de verwarming al aan?","en":"Is the heating on already?","hint":["zijn","verwarming","al","aan"],"note":"Every word in -ing is a de-word: de verwarming, de woning, de vergadering."},{"nl":"Mijn broer is schilder en verft vooral muren.","en":"My brother is a painter and mainly paints walls.","hint":["broer","schilder","verven","vooral","muur"],"note":"A job after zijn takes no article: is schilder. Muren is a plural said in general, so no article either."},{"nl":"Het schoonmaken van de ramen duurt lang.","en":"Cleaning the windows takes a long time.","hint":["schoonmaken","raam","duren","lang"],"note":"An infinitive used as a noun is always a het-word: het schoonmaken. De ramen is plural, so de, although it is het raam."},{"nl":"Het kamertje boven de trap is van mijn zoon.","en":"The little room above the stairs is my son's.","hint":["kamer","boven","trap","zoon"],"note":"A diminutive is always a het-word, even when the noun itself is de: de kamer, het kamertje."},{"nl":"Behang is duur, maar het is mooi.","en":"Wallpaper is expensive, but it is beautiful.","hint":["behang","duur","mooi"],"note":"Wallpaper in general, a thing you cannot count: no article. Behang is a het-word: be- + one syllable, like het begin."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ kussens liggen op de bank.","options":["De","Het","Een"],"correct":0,"en":"The cushions are on the sofa.","note":"Every plural takes de, also when the singular is a het-word: het kussen, de kussens. Een never goes with a plural."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik zoek een lamp voor ___ woonkamer.","options":["het","de","–"],"correct":1,"en":"I am looking for a lamp for the living room.","note":"In a compound the last part decides: de kamer, so de woonkamer. A countable noun in the singular cannot stand without an article."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ kamertje boven de trap is klein.","options":["De","Het","–"],"correct":1,"en":"The little room above the stairs is small.","note":"A diminutive is always het, even though it is de kamer. A singular noun like this needs an article."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ gordijn in de slaapkamer is te kort.","options":["De","–","Het"],"correct":2,"en":"The curtain in the bedroom is too short.","note":"Gordijn is a het-word without a rule to show it: het gordijn. Learn it together with its article."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (de/het) plafond in de keuken is laag.","answers":["Het"],"en":"The ceiling in the kitchen is low.","note":"Plafond is a het-word. No rule tells you that, so learn it as one unit: het plafond."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het kussen ligt op ___ (de/het) vloer.","answers":["de"],"en":"The cushion is lying on the floor.","note":"Vloer is a de-word, like about two thirds of all nouns: de vloer."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hoe zeg je 'sofa' in ___ (de/het) Nederlands?","answers":["het"],"en":"How do you say 'sofa' in Dutch?","note":"The name of a language is a het-word: het Nederlands, het Oekraïens, het Engels."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Na ___ (de/het) eten zitten we in de woonkamer.","answers":["het"],"en":"After dinner we sit in the living room.","note":"Eten here is an infinitive used as a noun, and those are always het-words: het eten."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (de/het) woonkamer is erg licht.","answers":["De"],"en":"The living room is very bright.","note":"The last part of the compound decides: kamer is de, so de woonkamer."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the noun plural.","from":"Het kussen is zacht.","answers":["De kussens zijn zacht."],"en":"The cushions are soft.","note":"In the plural het becomes de, and the verb becomes plural too: de kussens zijn."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the noun plural.","from":"Het schilderij hangt in de gang.","answers":["De schilderijen hangen in de gang."],"en":"The paintings hang in the hall.","note":"Het schilderij, but de schilderijen: every plural takes de. Hangt becomes hangen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Talk about one curtain instead of several.","from":"Ik koop gordijnen.","answers":["Ik koop een gordijn."],"en":"I am buying a curtain.","note":"A plural that is not known has no article. One thing that is new to the listener gets een, for de-words and het-words alike."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het ramen zijn schoon.","answers":["De ramen zijn schoon."],"en":"The windows are clean.","note":"Het raam is a het-word, but every plural takes de: de ramen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De behang in de gang is oud.","answers":["Het behang in de gang is oud."],"en":"The wallpaper in the hall is old.","note":"Behang has two syllables and starts with be-, like het begin and het bezoek: it is a het-word."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Wij hebben een nieuwe meubels.","answers":["Wij hebben nieuwe meubels.","We hebben nieuwe meubels."],"en":"We have new furniture.","note":"Een means 'one' and never goes with a plural. A plural that is not known has no article at all."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het muur in de slaapkamer is blauw.","answers":["De muur in de slaapkamer is blauw."],"en":"The wall in the bedroom is blue.","note":"Muur is a de-word: de muur. Most nouns are de-words; when you are not sure, de is the better guess."}]},
  {"id":"a2-06","level":"A2","icon":"🛒","title":"Shopping for food","grammar":"Plural nouns","summary":"Most nouns: -en (fles, flessen; tomaat, tomaten). -s after unstressed -el, -em, -en, -er, -je. -'s after a, i, o, u, y (kilo's). Every plural takes de. After a number a unit stays singular: twee kilo, drie euro.","explain":"Dutch makes most plurals with **-en** or **-s**. Which one a noun takes depends mostly on how it ends, and the spelling follows the rules you know from unit A2 · 1. Every plural takes `de`, whatever the singular has: `het ei`, `de eieren`.\n\n### -en: the main ending\n\nMost nouns, and nearly all nouns of one syllable, get **-en**. Say the plural aloud: the vowel keeps its sound, and the spelling changes to show that.\n\n| singular | plural | why |\n| de fles | de flessen | short vowel: double the consonant |\n| het pak | de pakken | short vowel: double the consonant |\n| de tomaat | de tomaten | long vowel: write it single |\n| het brood | de broden | long vowel: write it single |\n| de doos | de dozen | s becomes z |\n| de brief | de brieven | f becomes v |\n\n- A **short vowel** stays short only if the consonant is doubled: `de zak → zakken`, `de man → mannen`.\n- A **long vowel** written double loses a letter, because the syllable is now open: `de boom → bomen`.\n- **s → z** and **f → v** happen in many words: `het huis → huizen`, `de kaas → kazen`. But not in all: `de kans → kansen`, `de mens → mensen`.\n\n> Ik koop twee flessen water. — I am buying two bottles of water.\n> De tomaten zijn rood. — The tomatoes are red.\n\n### -s\n\n- After an unstressed **-el, -em, -en, -er** and after **-je**: `de tafel → tafels`, `de bezem → bezems`, `de jongen → jongens`, `de komkommer → komkommers`, `het mandje → mandjes`. A few -el words also have -en: `aardappels` or `aardappelen`.\n- Many words from English and French: `de film → films`, `het restaurant → restaurants`.\n\n> De komkommers liggen naast de uien. — The cucumbers are next to the onions.\n\n### -'s\n\nAfter **a, i, o, u, y** at the end, Dutch writes **'s**: `de kilo → kilo's`, `de auto → auto's`, `de taxi → taxi's`, `het menu → menu's`, `de baby → baby's`. Without the apostrophe you would read *kilos* as ki-los, with the short o of *los*; the apostrophe keeps the vowel long. Words in **é** just get s: `het café → cafés`.\n\n### Irregular plurals\n\n- `het kind → kinderen`, `het ei → eieren`.\n- The vowel becomes long: `de stad → steden`, `het schip → schepen`, `het lid → leden`, `de dag → dagen`, `het glas → glazen`. Dag and glas keep one letter: the a is now long.\n- Words in **-heid** get **-heden**: `de mogelijkheid → mogelijkheden`.\n\n> De eieren liggen in de koelkast. — The eggs are in the fridge.\n\n### Numbers and quantities\n\nAfter a number bigger than one, a noun is plural: `twee appels`, `vijf eieren`. But **units of measure stay singular**: `twee kilo`, `drie euro`, `vijf jaar`, `twee uur`, `tien procent`, `twee liter`, `drie keer`. Containers and units of time like `dag` and `week` normally do take the plural: `twee flessen`, `drie pakken`, `vier dagen`, `drie weken`. (In speech a container used as a measure is sometimes left singular, `twee pak melk`; the plural is always right.)\n\n> We hebben nog drie pakken melk. — We still have three cartons of milk.\n\nWhat comes after a quantity: something you count is plural (`een kilo tomaten`, `een zak uien`), something you cannot count is singular (`een pak melk`, `twee flessen water`).\n\n> Een kilo appels kost drie euro. — A kilo of apples costs three euros.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *tomaats → `tomaten`; *broods → `broden`.\n- *flesen → `flessen`: keep the vowel short.\n- *kilos → `kilo's`; but *pauze's → `pauzes`, because e needs no apostrophe.\n- *twee kilo's appels → `twee kilo appels`.\n- *eien → `eieren`; *kinds → `kinderen`.\n- *het eieren → `de eieren`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian changes the noun after a number: `два яблука`, but `п'ять яблук`. Dutch has one plural for every number from two up: `twee appels`, `vijf appels`. And where Ukrainian says `два кілограми`, Dutch keeps the unit singular: `twee kilo`.","words":[{"nl":"fles","art":"de","pl":"flessen","en":"bottle","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pak","art":"het","pl":"pakken","en":"carton / packet","pos":"n"},{"nl":"blik","art":"het","pl":"blikken","en":"tin / can","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zak","art":"de","pl":"zakken","en":"bag","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pot","art":"de","pl":"potten","en":"jar / pot","pos":"n"},{"nl":"doos","art":"de","pl":"dozen","en":"box","pos":"n"},{"nl":"mandje","art":"het","pl":"mandjes","en":"basket (in a shop)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verpakking","art":"de","pl":"verpakkingen","en":"packaging","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kilo","art":"het/de","pl":"kilo's","en":"kilo","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ei","art":"het","pl":"eieren","en":"egg","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tomaat","art":"de","pl":"tomaten","en":"tomato","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ui","art":"de","pl":"uien","en":"onion","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aardappel","art":"de","pl":"aardappels","en":"potato","pos":"n","tag":"also: aardappelen"},{"nl":"komkommer","art":"de","pl":"komkommers","en":"cucumber","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wegen","past":"woog","perf":"heeft gewogen","en":"to weigh","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vers","en":"fresh","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik koop twee kilo aardappels en drie uien.","en":"I am buying two kilos of potatoes and three onions.","hint":["kopen","kilo","aardappel","ui"],"note":"Kilo is a unit of measure and stays singular after a number. Aardappel ends in unstressed -el and gets -s; ui gets -en.","alt":["Ik koop twee kilo aardappelen en drie uien."]},{"nl":"Hoeveel eieren heb je nodig?","en":"How many eggs do you need?","hint":["hoeveel","ei","nodig hebben"],"note":"Ei has an irregular plural: eieren, never eien. Like kind, kinderen, it adds -er before -en.","alt":["Hoeveel eieren heb jij nodig?"]},{"nl":"De tomaten liggen naast de komkommers.","en":"The tomatoes are next to the cucumbers.","hint":["tomaat","liggen","naast","komkommer"],"note":"Tomaat → tomaten: the long aa is written single in the open syllable to-ma-ten. Komkommer ends in unstressed -er, so it takes -s."},{"nl":"We hebben drie flessen water nodig.","en":"We need three bottles of water.","hint":["drie","fles","water","nodig hebben"],"note":"Fles has a short e, so the s is doubled: flessen. Water cannot be counted and stays singular after the container."},{"nl":"In de kast staan vijf blikken tomaten.","en":"There are five tins of tomatoes in the cupboard.","hint":["kast","staan","blik","tomaat"],"note":"Blik has a short i, so the k is doubled: blikken. Tomaten is plural because you can count tomatoes.","alt":["Er staan vijf blikken tomaten in de kast."]},{"nl":"De dozen met boodschappen zijn zwaar.","en":"The boxes of groceries are heavy.","hint":["doos","boodschappen","zwaar"],"note":"Doos → dozen: the long oo is written single, and the s becomes z, as in huis, huizen."},{"nl":"Een kilo appels kost drie euro.","en":"A kilo of apples costs three euros.","hint":["kilo","appel","kosten","euro"],"note":"Euro is a unit and stays singular after drie, unlike English euros. Appels is plural: you count apples."},{"nl":"De kinderen dragen de mandjes naar de kassa.","en":"The children carry the baskets to the checkout.","hint":["kind","dragen","mandje","kassa"],"note":"Kind has an irregular plural: kinderen. Every word in -je takes -s: mandjes."},{"nl":"Hoeveel weegt een zak aardappels?","en":"How much does a bag of potatoes weigh?","hint":["hoeveel","wegen","zak","aardappel"],"note":"After a container, something you count is plural: een zak aardappels. Een zak is singular, so the verb is too: weegt.","alt":["Hoeveel weegt een zak aardappelen?"]},{"nl":"Zijn de broden nog vers?","en":"Are the loaves still fresh?","hint":["zijn","brood","nog","vers"],"note":"Brood → broden: the long oo is written single in the open syllable bro-den. Never broods."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik koop twee ___ melk.","options":["paks","pakken","paken"],"correct":1,"en":"I am buying two cartons of milk.","note":"Pak has a short a. To keep it short in the plural, the k is doubled: pakken. Paken would be read with a long a."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Voor de soep heb ik drie ___ uien nodig.","options":["kilo's","kilo","kilos"],"correct":1,"en":"For the soup I need three kilos of onions.","note":"A unit of measure stays singular after a number: drie kilo. Unlike English, Dutch does not say drie kilo's for an amount."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De ___ staan bij de ingang van de supermarkt.","options":["mandjen","mandje's","mandjes"],"correct":2,"en":"The baskets are at the entrance of the supermarket.","note":"Every word in -je takes -s, without an apostrophe: mandjes. The 's is only for a, i, o, u and y."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Hoeveel ___ zitten er in een doos?","options":["eien","eieren","eis"],"correct":1,"en":"How many eggs are there in a box?","note":"Ei is irregular: eieren, just like kind, kinderen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik koop een kilo ___ (tomaat).","answers":["tomaten"],"en":"I am buying a kilo of tomatoes.","note":"After een kilo, something you count is plural. Tomaat → tomaten: the long aa is written single in the open syllable."},{"type":"gap","nl":"In de kast staan twee ___ (pot) jam.","answers":["potten"],"en":"There are two jars of jam in the cupboard.","note":"Pot has a short o, so the t is doubled: potten. Jam stays singular: you do not count it."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Plastic ___ (verpakking) zijn slecht voor het milieu.","answers":["verpakkingen"],"en":"Plastic packaging is bad for the environment.","note":"Words in -ing take -en: verpakkingen. Dutch uses the plural here, where English packaging has none."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op de markt liggen tien ___ (kaas).","answers":["kazen"],"en":"There are ten cheeses at the market.","note":"Kaas → kazen: the long aa is written single, and the s becomes z, as in doos, dozen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De boodschappen wegen samen tien ___ (kilo).","answers":["kilo"],"en":"Together the groceries weigh ten kilos.","note":"Kilo is a unit of measure and stays singular after a number: tien kilo, not tien kilo's."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hoeveel ___ (ui) heb je nog?","answers":["uien"],"en":"How many onions do you have left?","note":"Ui simply gets -en: uien. After hoeveel a noun you count is always plural."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the noun plural.","from":"Het ei is vers.","answers":["De eieren zijn vers."],"en":"The eggs are fresh.","note":"Ei → eieren, and every plural takes de: het ei, de eieren. The verb becomes plural too."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Buy two instead of one.","from":"Ik koop een fles water.","answers":["Ik koop twee flessen water."],"en":"I am buying two bottles of water.","note":"A container takes the plural after twee: flessen, with ss to keep the e short. Water stays as it is."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Buy three instead of one.","from":"Ik koop een kilo tomaten.","answers":["Ik koop drie kilo tomaten."],"en":"I am buying three kilos of tomatoes.","note":"Kilo is a unit of measure, so it stays singular after a number. Only tomaten, the thing you count, is plural."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik koop drie komkommers en vier tomaats.","answers":["Ik koop drie komkommers en vier tomaten."],"en":"I am buying three cucumbers and four tomatoes.","note":"Komkommer takes -s because it ends in unstressed -er, but tomaat takes -en: tomaten."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik koop twee kilo's appels.","answers":["Ik koop twee kilo appels."],"en":"I am buying two kilos of apples.","note":"After a number a unit of measure stays singular: twee kilo. English kilos, Dutch kilo."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het verpakkingen zijn van plastic.","answers":["De verpakkingen zijn van plastic."],"en":"The packaging is made of plastic.","note":"Every plural takes de. Here it would be de anyway, because verpakking is a de-word, like every word in -ing."}]},
  {"id":"a2-07","level":"A2","icon":"🍽️","title":"Eating out","grammar":"Negation: niet and geen","summary":"Geen replaces een or no article in front of a noun; niet does the rest. Niet stands late: after a definite object, but before a preposition phrase, an adjective and the final verbs. One negation per sentence.","explain":"Dutch has two words for \"not\". `Geen` goes with a noun that has `een` or no article; `niet` does all the rest. Choosing between them is the easy part. The harder part is where `niet` stands, because that depends on what else is in the sentence.\n\n### Geen: where the positive sentence has een or no article\n\nLook at the positive sentence. A noun with `een`, or with no article at all (a plural, or something you cannot count), gets `geen` in that place.\n\n| positive | negative |\n| Ik neem een voorgerecht. | Ik neem geen voorgerecht. |\n| Ik eet vlees. | Ik eet geen vlees. |\n| Hij lust noten. | Hij lust geen noten. |\n\n> Ik heb geen honger. — I am not hungry.\n> Dat is geen probleem. — That is not a problem.\n\nA noun with `de`, `het`, `mijn`, `deze` or `dit` is definite and takes `niet`: `Ik neem het voorgerecht niet.`\n\n### Niet: late in the sentence\n\n`Niet` comes **after** the verb, after a definite object or a pronoun, and after a time word like `vandaag` or `vanavond`. If nothing else follows, it is the last word.\n\n> Ik rook niet. — I do not smoke.\n> Ik ken die ober niet. — I do not know that waiter.\n> We komen vanavond niet. — We are not coming tonight.\n\nBut `niet` comes **in front of** the part that completes the sentence:\n\n- a phrase with a preposition that says where or where to, or that belongs to the verb: `Ik woon niet in Utrecht`, `We gaan niet naar huis`, `Ik wacht niet op het nagerecht`. A noun inside such a phrase keeps `een`: `Ik werk niet in een restaurant.`\n- an adjective, and an adverb that says how or how often: `niet duur`, `niet lekker`, `niet goed`, `niet vaak`, `niet graag`\n- the verbs at the end, an infinitive or a past participle: `Ik kan niet komen`, `We hebben niet gereserveerd`\n\n> De soep is niet warm. — The soup is not warm.\n> Mijn man kookt niet vaak. — My husband does not cook often.\n\n### Niet … maar …: a correction\n\nTo correct one part of the sentence, put `niet` directly in front of that part and the right version after `maar`.\n\n> We komen niet vandaag, maar morgen. — We are coming not today, but tomorrow.\n\n### Nooit, niets, niemand, nergens: one negation\n\n`nooit` (never), `niets` (nothing), `niemand` (nobody) and `nergens` (nowhere) are the negation themselves. Standard Dutch does not add `niet` or `geen` to them, and after them it uses the positive words `iets`, `iemand`, `ooit`, `ergens`.\n\n> Ik drink nooit koffie. — I never drink coffee.\n> Niemand heeft iets gehoord. — Nobody has heard anything.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian piles negations up: `Ніхто нічого не сказав` has three. Dutch has one: `Niemand heeft iets gezegd`. `Я ніколи не їм м'яса` is `Ik eet nooit vlees`, never *Ik eet nooit geen vlees. And where Ukrainian puts `не` on the verb, Dutch often needs `geen` on the noun: `Я не маю часу` is `Ik heb geen tijd`.\n\n### Not yet, no longer, and wel\n\n- `nog niet`, `nog geen`: not yet. `Het eten is nog niet klaar.` `Ik heb nog geen honger.`\n- `niet meer`, `geen … meer`: no longer, no more. `Hij werkt hier niet meer.` `We hebben geen soep meer.`\n- `wel` is the opposite of `niet`: it stresses that something is so, mostly after a negative. `Ik drink geen wijn, maar wel bier.` `Ik niet, maar hij wel.`\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Ik heb niet honger → `Ik heb geen honger`: no article in the positive sentence, so `geen`.\n- *Ik niet rook → `Ik rook niet`: in a main clause `niet` never stands between the subject and the verb.\n- *Ik woon in Utrecht niet → `Ik woon niet in Utrecht`.\n- *Niemand heeft niets gezegd → `Niemand heeft iets gezegd`.","words":[{"nl":"ober","art":"de","pl":"obers","en":"waiter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"menukaart","art":"de","pl":"menukaarten","en":"menu","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gerecht","art":"het","pl":"gerechten","en":"dish","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voorgerecht","art":"het","pl":"voorgerechten","en":"starter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hoofdgerecht","art":"het","pl":"hoofdgerechten","en":"main course","pos":"n"},{"nl":"nagerecht","art":"het","pl":"nagerechten","en":"dessert","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rekening","art":"de","pl":"rekeningen","en":"bill","pos":"n"},{"nl":"fooi","art":"de","pl":"fooien","en":"tip","pos":"n"},{"nl":"allergie","art":"de","pl":"allergieën","en":"allergy","pos":"n"},{"nl":"noot","art":"de","pl":"noten","en":"nut","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bestellen","past":"bestelde","perf":"heeft besteld","en":"to order","pos":"v"},{"nl":"reserveren","past":"reserveerde","perf":"heeft gereserveerd","en":"to book / to reserve","pos":"v"},{"nl":"proeven","past":"proefde","perf":"heeft geproefd","en":"to taste / to try (food)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"lusten","past":"lustte","perf":"heeft gelust","en":"to like (food or drink)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vegetarisch","en":"vegetarian","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"allergisch","en":"allergic","pos":"adj","tag":"allergisch voor"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik eet geen vlees, maar wel vis.","en":"I do not eat meat, but I do eat fish.","hint":["eten","vlees","wel","vis"],"note":"The positive sentence is Ik eet vlees, a noun without an article, so the negative takes geen. Wel is the opposite of the negation: it stresses that fish is fine.","alt":["Ik eet geen vlees, maar ik eet wel vis."]},{"nl":"Lust je de soep niet?","en":"Don't you like the soup?","hint":["lusten","soep"],"note":"De soep is a definite object, so the negation is niet, and it comes after the object, at the end. Lusten is 'to like' for food and drink only.","alt":["Lust jij de soep niet?"]},{"nl":"Hij geeft nooit een fooi.","en":"He never gives a tip.","hint":["geven","nooit","fooi"],"note":"Nooit is the negation, and it is the only one: een fooi keeps een. *Hij geeft nooit geen fooi would be the Ukrainian habit of two negatives.","alt":["Hij geeft nooit fooi."]},{"nl":"Wij eten niet vaak in een restaurant.","en":"We do not often eat in a restaurant.","hint":["eten","vaak","restaurant"],"note":"Niet stands in front of the adverb vaak. The noun after the preposition in keeps een: niet negates the whole phrase.","alt":["We eten niet vaak in een restaurant."]},{"nl":"Uw naam staat niet op de lijst.","en":"Your (formal) name is not on the list.","hint":["naam","staan","lijst"],"note":"Op de lijst is a phrase with a preposition that says where: niet goes in front of it, never after it."},{"nl":"In dit gerecht zitten geen noten.","en":"There are no nuts in this dish.","hint":["gerecht","zitten","noot"],"note":"The positive sentence has a plural without an article (… zitten noten), so the negative is geen noten. Dutch says that nuts 'sit' in a dish.","alt":["Er zitten geen noten in dit gerecht."]},{"nl":"Het hoofdgerecht is nog niet klaar.","en":"The main course is not ready yet.","hint":["hoofdgerecht","nog niet","klaar"],"note":"Niet stands in front of the adjective klaar, which completes the sentence. Nog niet is 'not yet'."},{"nl":"Waarom nemen jullie geen nagerecht?","en":"Why are you (plural) not having a dessert?","hint":["waarom","nemen","nagerecht"],"note":"The positive sentence is Jullie nemen een nagerecht: een becomes geen, in the same place."},{"nl":"Niemand zegt iets over de rekening.","en":"Nobody says anything about the bill.","hint":["niemand","zeggen","iets","rekening"],"note":"Niemand is the negation, so the next word is the positive iets, not niets. Ukrainian would use three negatives here, Dutch one."},{"nl":"We kunnen vanavond niet komen.","en":"We cannot come tonight.","hint":["kunnen","vanavond","komen"],"note":"Niet stands after the time word vanavond and in front of the infinitive at the end.","alt":["Wij kunnen vanavond niet komen.","Vanavond kunnen we niet komen.","Vanavond kunnen wij niet komen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Nee, dank u, ik heb ___ dorst.","options":["niet","geen","niets"],"correct":1,"en":"No, thank you, I am not thirsty.","note":"Dorst has no article in the positive sentence (Ik heb dorst), so the negative is geen dorst, never *niet dorst."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Dit voorgerecht is ___ vegetarisch.","options":["geen","niets","niet"],"correct":2,"en":"This starter is not vegetarian.","note":"Vegetarisch is an adjective, not a noun, so the negation is niet, and it stands in front of the adjective. Geen needs a noun after it."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Niemand heeft ___ besteld.","options":["niets","iets","geen"],"correct":1,"en":"Nobody has ordered anything.","note":"Niemand already is the negation. After it Dutch uses the positive word iets; with niets as well, the sentence would mean that everybody ordered something."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We hebben ___ menukaart.","options":["niet","nog geen","nog niet"],"correct":1,"en":"We do not have a menu yet.","note":"We hebben een menukaart: een becomes geen, and 'not yet' is then nog geen. Nog niet is for sentences where niet is the negation: Het eten is nog niet klaar."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ben je allergisch voor noten? Nee, ik heb ___ (niet / geen) allergie.","answers":["geen"],"en":"Are you allergic to nuts? No, I do not have an allergy.","note":"The positive sentence is Ik heb een allergie. Een becomes geen: geen allergie."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Heeft u gereserveerd? Nee, we hebben ___ (niet / geen) gereserveerd.","answers":["niet"],"en":"Have you (formal) booked? No, we have not booked.","note":"There is no noun to negate, so it is niet, and it stands in front of the past participle at the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ken je hier iemand? Nee, ik ken hier ___ (iemand).","answers":["niemand"],"en":"Do you know anyone here? No, I do not know anyone here.","note":"The negative of iemand is niemand, and it is the whole negation: no niet with it. English 'not … anyone' is one word in Dutch."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["proef","de knoflook","niet"],"answers":["Ik proef de knoflook niet."],"en":"I cannot taste the garlic.","note":"De knoflook is a definite object and nothing else follows, so niet is the last word of the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","de rekening","nog niet","betaald"],"answers":["Ik heb de rekening nog niet betaald."],"en":"I have not paid the bill yet.","note":"Nog niet comes after the definite object de rekening and in front of the past participle, which stands at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Mijn man","words":["gaat","niet","graag","naar een restaurant"],"answers":["Mijn man gaat niet graag naar een restaurant."],"en":"My husband does not like going to a restaurant.","note":"Niet stands in front of the adverb graag, and both come before the phrase with the preposition: niet graag naar een restaurant."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative.","from":"De ober spreekt Engels.","answers":["De ober spreekt geen Engels."],"en":"The waiter does not speak English.","note":"Engels is a noun without an article here, so the negative is geen Engels. The same goes for geen Nederlands spreken."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative.","from":"Ik zie de ober.","answers":["Ik zie de ober niet."],"en":"I do not see the waiter.","note":"De ober is definite, so the negation is niet, and it comes after the object. Geen is only for a noun with een or without an article."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Correct it with niet … maar …: you are having the salad (de salade), not the soup.","from":"Ik neem de soep.","answers":["Ik neem niet de soep, maar de salade.","Ik neem de soep niet, maar de salade."],"en":"I am having not the soup, but the salad.","note":"In a correction niet stands directly in front of the part that is wrong, here de soep, and maar brings the right version. With niet after de soep the sentence is right as well."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn zoon lust niet vis.","answers":["Mijn zoon lust geen vis."],"en":"My son does not like fish.","note":"Vis has no article (Hij lust vis), so the negation is geen: geen vis. Niet would be right with a definite noun: Hij lust de vis niet."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De ober niet komt.","answers":["De ober komt niet."],"en":"The waiter is not coming.","note":"The verb is the second element of a main clause, so niet cannot stand between the subject and the verb. It comes after the verb."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Wij eten vanavond in een restaurant niet.","answers":["Wij eten vanavond niet in een restaurant."],"en":"We are not eating in a restaurant tonight.","note":"In een restaurant says where and completes the sentence, so niet stands in front of it. The time word vanavond stays before niet."}]},
  {"id":"a2-08","level":"A2","icon":"📱","title":"Keeping in touch","grammar":"Personal pronouns: subject and object forms","summary":"Subject: ik, je/jij, u, hij, ze/zij, het, we/wij, jullie, ze/zij. Object: me/mij, je/jou, u, hem, haar, het, ons, jullie, hen/hun/ze. After a preposition always the object form: met hem. It's me: Ik ben het.","explain":"Personal pronouns (*persoonlijke voornaamwoorden*) are the small words for people and things: I, you, him, them. Dutch has two sets: a **subject form** for the one who does something, and an **object form** for everything else. Most of them also have a short, unstressed form, and that short form is the one you hear most.\n\n### Subject and object\n\n| | subject | object |\n| I / me | ik | mij / me |\n| you | jij / je | jou / je |\n| you (formal) | u | u |\n| he / him | hij | hem |\n| she / her | zij / ze | haar |\n| it | het | het |\n| we / us | wij / we | ons |\n| you (plural) | jullie | jullie |\n| they / them | zij / ze | hen / hun / ze |\n\nThe subject form goes with the conjugated verb. The object form is for the person the action is done to, for the person who receives something, and for every pronoun after a preposition.\n\n> Ik bel hem vanavond. — I am calling him tonight.\n> Hij appt haar elke dag. — He texts her every day.\n\n### Short and stressed forms\n\n`je`, `ze`, `we` and `me` are the normal forms in speech and in most writing. The long forms `jij`, `zij`, `wij`, `mij` and `jou` are for **emphasis or contrast**, when the pronoun itself is stressed. A short form can never carry stress, so in a contrast only the long form is possible.\n\n> Zie je hem vaak? — Do you see him often?\n> Hij belt mij, niet jou. — He is calling me, not you.\n\n`ze` can mean \"she\" or \"they\"; the verb shows which: `ze belt` (she), `ze bellen` (they).\n\n### After a preposition\n\nAfter every preposition comes the object form: `met mij`, `voor hem`, `van haar`, `bij ons`, `zonder jullie`, `naast hen`.\n\n> Ik praat vaak met haar. — I often talk to her.\n> Ik eet vanavond bij hen. — I am having dinner at their place tonight.\n\nOn the phone you say who you are with `met`: `Met Anna` (Anna speaking), and to a friend `Hoi, met mij!` (Hi, it's me!).\n\n### Hen, hun or ze?\n\nFor people in the plural, written standard Dutch has two object forms:\n\n- `hen` when the people undergo the action, and after a preposition: `Ik bel hen`, `Ik woon bij hen`.\n- `hun` for the people who **receive** something, without a preposition (with `geven`, `vertellen`, `sturen`): `Ik geef hun een cadeau` = `Ik geef een cadeau aan hen`.\n\n> Ik vertel hun het nieuws. — I tell them the news.\n\nThat is the traditional rule. In practice `hen` is also used and accepted for the people who receive something (`Ik geef hen een cadeau`); `hun` for the people who undergo the action (*Ik bel hun) is still counted as a mistake. In speech and in informal messages `ze` is always right for people, in both cases: `Ik bel ze`, `Ik vertel ze het nieuws`. When in doubt, say `ze`. `hen` and `hun` are only for people; things in the plural are always `ze`.\n\n### Things: hij, hem or het\n\nIn everyday Dutch a thing is replaced according to its article: a de-word by `hij` and `hem`, a het-word by `het`, a plural by `ze`. So English \"it\" is often `hij` or `hem`.\n\n> Mijn telefoon? Hij ligt op tafel. — My phone? It is on the table.\n> Ik zoek mijn sleutels, maar ik zie ze nergens. — I am looking for my keys, but I don't see them anywhere.\n\nAfter a preposition a thing becomes `ermee`, `erop` and so on; that is unit B1 · 5.\n\n### It's me\n\nDutch turns \"it's me\" around: the person is the subject, `het` comes after the verb, and the verb agrees with the person.\n\n> Ik ben het. — It's me.\n> Ben jij het? — Is it you?\n\n### Watch out\n\n- After a preposition, the object form: `met hem`, never *met hij.\n- `hun` is never a subject: `Ze bellen me`, not *Hun bellen me. You hear it, but it is a clear mistake.\n- In a contrast, the long form: `Hij belt mij, niet jou`, not *Hij belt me, niet je.\n- A de-word is `hij` or `hem`: `Mijn jas? Ik draag hem vandaag`, not *Ik draag het vandaag.\n- \"It's me\" is `Ik ben het`, never *Het is mij.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian often leaves the pronoun out (`Дзвоню тобі`); Dutch never does: `Ik bel je`. Where Ukrainian has `мене`, `мені` and `мною`, Dutch has only `mij`/`me`: these few object forms are all that is left of case. Like `він` and `вона` for things, Dutch chooses by the word: `de telefoon` is `hij`. And `Це я` is `Ik ben het`.","words":[{"nl":"groepsapp","art":"de","pl":"groepsapps","en":"group chat (on WhatsApp)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"contact","art":"het","pl":"contacten","en":"contact","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezoek","art":"het","pl":"bezoeken","en":"visit / visitors","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gast","art":"de","pl":"gasten","en":"guest","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vriendschap","art":"de","pl":"vriendschappen","en":"friendship","pos":"n"},{"nl":"buitenland","art":"het","pl":"","en":"abroad / foreign countries","pos":"n","tag":"in het buitenland"},{"nl":"appen","past":"appte","perf":"heeft geappt","en":"to text (on WhatsApp)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"missen","past":"miste","perf":"heeft gemist","en":"to miss","pos":"v"},{"nl":"bezoeken","past":"bezocht","perf":"heeft bezocht","en":"to visit","pos":"v"},{"nl":"videobellen","past":"videobelde","perf":"heeft gevideobeld","en":"to video-call","pos":"v"},{"nl":"reageren","past":"reageerde","perf":"heeft gereageerd","en":"to reply / to react","pos":"v","tag":"reageren op"},{"nl":"vertellen","past":"vertelde","perf":"heeft verteld","en":"to tell","pos":"v"},{"nl":"bereikbaar","en":"reachable (by phone)","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"elkaar","en":"each other","pos":"pron"},{"nl":"de groeten doen","en":"to say hello (from someone)","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik mis je heel erg.","en":"I miss you very much.","hint":["missen","heel erg"],"note":"Je is the object here: the one who is missed. The short form je is normal; jou would put stress on 'you'.","alt":["Ik mis jou heel erg."]},{"nl":"Ben je morgen bereikbaar? Dan bel ik je.","en":"Can you be reached tomorrow? Then I will call you.","hint":["zijn","morgen","bereikbaar","dan","bellen"],"note":"The same short form je twice: first as subject (ben je), then as object (bel ik je). Only the long forms differ: jij and jou.","alt":["Ben jij morgen bereikbaar? Dan bel ik je.","Ben je morgen bereikbaar? Dan bel ik jou.","Ben jij morgen bereikbaar? Dan bel ik jou."]},{"nl":"Mijn moeder doet je de groeten.","en":"My mother says hello to you.","hint":["moeder","de groeten doen"],"note":"Je is the one who receives the greetings. Dutch uses the same object form for 'you' whether something is done to you or given to you.","alt":["Mijn moeder doet jou de groeten."]},{"nl":"Volgend weekend bezoeken we jullie in Amsterdam.","en":"Next weekend we are visiting you (plural) in Amsterdam.","hint":["volgend","weekend","bezoeken","Amsterdam"],"note":"Jullie is both the subject and the object form. We is the subject: it follows the verb because the time comes first.","alt":["We bezoeken jullie volgend weekend in Amsterdam."]},{"nl":"Ik heb geen contact meer met hem.","en":"I have no contact with him any more.","hint":["hebben","contact","meer"],"note":"After the preposition met comes the object form: met hem, never met hij.","alt":["Ik heb met hem geen contact meer."]},{"nl":"Ik vertel hun alles over Nederland.","en":"I tell them everything about the Netherlands.","hint":["vertellen","alles","Nederland"],"note":"They receive the story, without a preposition, so the traditional written form is hun; hen is accepted too. In speech ze is just as right.","alt":["Ik vertel ze alles over Nederland.","Ik vertel hen alles over Nederland."]},{"nl":"Elke zondag videobel ik met hen.","en":"Every Sunday I have a video call with them.","hint":["elke","zondag","videobellen"],"note":"After a preposition the plural is hen in writing, not hun. In speech met ze is also fine.","alt":["Ik videobel elke zondag met hen.","Elke zondag videobel ik met ze.","Ik videobel elke zondag met ze."]},{"nl":"Waar is mijn telefoon? Ik zie hem nergens.","en":"Where is my phone? I don't see it anywhere.","hint":["waar","telefoon","zien","nergens"],"note":"De telefoon is a de-word, so 'it' is hem as an object (and hij as a subject). Het is only for het-words."},{"nl":"Ben jij het? Ik herken je stem niet.","en":"Is it you? I don't recognise your voice.","hint":["zijn","herkennen","stem"],"note":"Dutch says 'are you it': Ben jij het? The verb agrees with jij, and het comes after it.","alt":["Ben jij het? Ik herken jouw stem niet."]},{"nl":"Hij belt mij elke week, maar ik bel hem nooit.","en":"He calls me every week, but I never call him.","hint":["bellen","elke week","nooit"],"note":"The two people are contrasted, so the long form mij fits best. Hem has no separate long form.","alt":["Hij belt me elke week, maar ik bel hem nooit.","Elke week belt hij mij, maar ik bel hem nooit.","Elke week belt hij me, maar ik bel hem nooit."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Bel jij hem? – Nee, hij belt ___.","options":["me","mij","ik"],"correct":1,"en":"Are you calling him? – No, he is calling me.","note":"'Me' carries the stress here, in contrast with 'him', and only the long form can be stressed: mij. Ik is the subject form."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Kom je morgen bij ___ op bezoek?","options":["wij","ons","onze"],"correct":1,"en":"Are you coming to visit us tomorrow?","note":"After the preposition bij comes the object form: bij ons. Wij is the subject form, and onze means 'our'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn telefoon is oud, maar ___ doet het nog.","options":["het","zij","hij"],"correct":2,"en":"My phone is old, but it still works.","note":"De telefoon is a de-word, so 'it' is hij. Het only replaces a het-word."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik zie mijn vrienden niet vaak, maar ik bel ___ elke week.","options":["hun","hen","zij"],"correct":1,"en":"I don't see my friends often, but I call them every week.","note":"The friends are the ones who are called, so the written form is hen. Hun is for those who receive something; zij is a subject. In speech: ik bel ze."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Waar zijn mijn sleutels? Ik zie ___ nergens.","options":["hen","ze","hun"],"correct":1,"en":"Where are my keys? I don't see them anywhere.","note":"Things in the plural are always ze. Hen and hun are only used for people."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vriendschap is heel belangrijk voor ___ (ik).","answers":["mij","me"],"en":"Friendship is very important to me.","note":"After the preposition voor comes the object form: voor mij, or unstressed voor me. Never voor ik."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn ouders wonen ver weg. Ik mis ___ (zij, they) heel erg.","answers":["hen","ze"],"en":"My parents live far away. I miss them very much.","note":"The parents are the ones who are missed: hen in writing, ze in speech. Not hun, and not zij, which is a subject form."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik vertel ___ (zij, they) morgen het nieuws.","answers":["hun","ze","hen"],"en":"I will tell them the news tomorrow.","note":"They receive the news, without a preposition, so the traditional written form is hun (= aan hen); hen is accepted here too. Ze is always right in speech."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wie is daar? – Ik ben ___.","answers":["het"],"en":"Who is there? – It's me.","note":"Dutch turns 'it's me' around: Ik ben het. Ik is the subject, so the verb is ben."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["bel","ik","je","om negen uur"],"answers":["Morgen bel ik je om negen uur.","Morgen om negen uur bel ik je."],"en":"Tomorrow I will call you at nine o'clock.","note":"The verb comes second and the subject ik follows it. A short object pronoun like je comes straight after the subject."},{"type":"order","words":["zien","weer","wanneer","elkaar","we"],"answers":["Wanneer zien we elkaar weer?"],"en":"When will we see each other again?","note":"After the question word comes the verb, then the subject we. Elkaar (each other) is the object, so it comes after the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace mijn vrienden by a personal pronoun.","from":"Mijn vrienden reageren nooit in de groepsapp.","answers":["Ze reageren nooit in de groepsapp.","Zij reageren nooit in de groepsapp."],"en":"They never reply in the group chat.","note":"Mijn vrienden is the subject, so the subject form ze or zij. Hun can never be a subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace de gasten by a personal pronoun.","from":"Ik geef de gasten koffie.","answers":["Ik geef hun koffie.","Ik geef ze koffie.","Ik geef hen koffie."],"en":"I give them coffee.","note":"The guests receive the coffee, without a preposition: by the traditional rule hun in writing, though hen is accepted too; ze in speech."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace het bezoek by a personal pronoun.","from":"Het bezoek duurt erg lang.","answers":["Het duurt erg lang."],"en":"It takes a very long time.","note":"Bezoek is a het-word, so 'it' is het. A de-word would become hij: De film duurt lang → Hij duurt lang."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik praat vaak met hij over vroeger.","answers":["Ik praat vaak met hem over vroeger."],"en":"I often talk to him about the old days.","note":"After a preposition the pronoun is always in the object form: met hem."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Hun appen me elke dag vanuit het buitenland.","answers":["Ze appen me elke dag vanuit het buitenland.","Zij appen me elke dag vanuit het buitenland."],"en":"They text me every day from abroad.","note":"The subject form for 'they' is ze or zij. Hun as a subject is heard a lot, but it is a clear mistake."}]},
  {"id":"a2-09","level":"A2","icon":"🐕","title":"Whose is it?","grammar":"Possessives: mijn, jouw, zijn, haar, ons, hun","summary":"mijn, jouw/je, uw, zijn, haar, ons/onze, jullie/je, hun. The form depends on the owner only. ons with a het-word in the singular, onze with all others. Also: de fiets van Anna, Anna's fiets. Whose? Van wie is …?","explain":"Possessives (*bezittelijke voornaamwoorden*) say whose something is: my key, her cat, our house. In Dutch the form depends on **the owner** and hardly ever on the thing: `mijn` fiets, `mijn` huis and `mijn` sleutels all use the same word.\n\n### The forms\n\n| owner | possessive | example |\n| ik | mijn | mijn sleutel |\n| jij / je | jouw / je | jouw kamer |\n| u | uw | uw fiets |\n| hij, het | zijn | zijn hond |\n| zij / ze (she) | haar | haar kat |\n| wij / we | ons / onze | ons huis, onze kat |\n| jullie | jullie / je | jullie tuin |\n| zij / ze (they) | hun | hun spullen |\n\n> Mijn huisgenoot leent vaak mijn spullen. — My housemate often borrows my things.\n> Is dit uw portemonnee, meneer? — Is this your wallet, sir?\n\n`je` is the short, unstressed form of `jouw`. Use `jouw` for stress or contrast: `Dat is jouw probleem, niet mijn probleem.` In chats and speech you will also see `m'n` and `z'n` for `mijn` and `zijn`; write the full forms.\n\n### Ons or onze\n\nThis is the only possessive that changes with the thing. `ons` goes with a **het-word in the singular**; `onze` goes with every de-word and with every plural.\n\n> Ons huis is oud, maar onze buren zijn heel aardig. — Our house is old, but our neighbours are very nice.\n\nSo: `ons konijn`, but `onze konijnen`; `onze kat`, `onze katten`.\n\n### Zijn or haar: look at the owner\n\n`zijn` is for a male owner and for an owner that is a thing or an animal; `haar` is for a female owner. What is owned does not matter. (In formal writing an organisation often gets `haar`: `de gemeente en haar inwoners`.)\n\n> Anna zorgt goed voor haar konijn. — Anna takes good care of her rabbit.\n> Peter zoekt zijn oplader. — Peter is looking for his charger.\n\n### Jullie as the subject: usually je\n\nWhen `jullie` is the subject, the possessive in the same sentence is usually `je`, because jullie … jullie sounds clumsy (it is not wrong). In other sentences `jullie` is the normal possessive.\n\n> Hebben jullie je sleutels bij je? — Do you (plural) have your keys with you?\n> Is dit jullie hond? — Is this your (plural) dog?\n\n### Other ways to say whose\n\n- `van` + the owner: `de fiets van Anna`, `de kat van de buren`. This works with every noun and every pronoun, which takes the object form: `van mij`, `van hem`, `van ons`.\n- A name + `s`: `Peters auto`. After a, i, o, u or y the s gets an apostrophe, so the vowel keeps its sound: `Anna's fiets`, `Timo's kamer`. This is mainly for names; with other nouns use `van`.\n\nTo ask whose something is: `Van wie is …?` The answer uses `van` too.\n\n> Van wie is die oplader? Is hij van jou? — Whose is that charger? Is it yours?\n> Dat is van mij. — That is mine.\n\nThere are also forms like `de mijne` and `het jouwe` (mine, yours). You will meet them later; for now say `van mij`, `van jou`.\n\n### Hun: their or them\n\n`hun` before a noun means \"their\": `hun kat`. Unit A2 · 8 also used `hun` (and `hen`) for \"them\". Recognise the difference: `Dat is hun auto` (their car) against `Ik help hen` (I help them).\n\n### Watch out\n\n- `jouw` is \"your\", `jou` is \"you\": `Is dit jouw sleutel?`, never *Is dit jou sleutel?\n- The owner decides: `Anna en haar hond`, not *Anna en zijn hond.\n- `onze kat`, `onze huizen`, but `ons huis`: never *ons kat or *onze huis.\n- No apostrophe after a consonant: `Peters auto`, not *Peter's auto.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\n`мій, моя, моє, мої` change with the thing; Dutch possessives change only with the owner. And Dutch has **no word for свій**: you always choose the form that fits the owner. `Він любить свою роботу` is `Hij houdt van zijn werk`, `Вона любить свою роботу` is `Zij houdt van haar werk`, `Ми любимо свою роботу` is `Wij houden van ons werk`. Where it could be someone else's, add `eigen` (own): `Hij gebruikt zijn eigen oplader`.","words":[{"nl":"huisdier","art":"het","pl":"huisdieren","en":"pet","pos":"n"},{"nl":"konijn","art":"het","pl":"konijnen","en":"rabbit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"huisgenoot","art":"de","pl":"huisgenoten","en":"housemate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"eigenaar","art":"de","pl":"eigenaren / eigenaars","en":"owner","pos":"n"},{"nl":"spullen","art":"de","pl":"","en":"things / belongings","pos":"n","tag":"always plural"},{"nl":"sleutel","art":"de","pl":"sleutels","en":"key","pos":"n"},{"nl":"oplader","art":"de","pl":"opladers","en":"charger","pos":"n"},{"nl":"portemonnee","art":"de","pl":"portemonnees","en":"wallet / purse","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lenen","past":"leende","perf":"heeft geleend","en":"to borrow / to lend","pos":"v"},{"nl":"delen","past":"deelde","perf":"heeft gedeeld","en":"to share","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zorgen voor","past":"zorgde voor","perf":"heeft gezorgd voor","en":"to take care of / to look after","pos":"v"},{"nl":"voeren","past":"voerde","perf":"heeft gevoerd","en":"to feed (an animal)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"kwijt","en":"lost / missing","pos":"adj","tag":"kwijt zijn"},{"nl":"eigen","en":"own","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"kapot","en":"broken","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Mijn huisgenoot leent vaak mijn spullen.","en":"My housemate often borrows my things.","hint":["huisgenoot","lenen","vaak","spullen"],"note":"Mijn is the same for every noun: mijn huisgenoot (de), mijn spullen (plural), mijn huis (het).","alt":["Mijn huisgenoot leent mijn spullen vaak."]},{"nl":"Van wie is die oplader? Is hij van jou?","en":"Whose is that charger? Is it yours?","hint":["van wie","oplader"],"note":"Whose? is Van wie …? and 'yours' is van jou, with the object form after van. De oplader is a de-word, so 'it' is hij.","alt":["Van wie is die oplader? Is die van jou?","Van wie is die oplader? Is het van jou?"]},{"nl":"Anna zorgt goed voor haar konijn.","en":"Anna takes good care of her rabbit.","hint":["zorgen voor","goed","konijn"],"note":"The owner is a woman, so haar. That konijn is a het-word does not matter."},{"nl":"Mijn zus is haar sleutels weer kwijt.","en":"My sister has lost her keys again.","hint":["zus","sleutel","weer","kwijt"],"note":"Ukrainian would say свої; Dutch chooses by the owner: mijn zus → haar.","alt":["Mijn zus is weer haar sleutels kwijt."]},{"nl":"We delen de keuken, maar we hebben allemaal onze eigen kamer.","en":"We share the kitchen, but we all have our own room.","hint":["delen","keuken","allemaal","eigen","kamer"],"note":"Kamer is a de-word, so onze. Eigen (own) comes between the possessive and the noun and never changes."},{"nl":"Ons huis is oud, maar onze buren zijn heel aardig.","en":"Our house is old, but our neighbours are very nice.","hint":["huis","oud","buur","aardig"],"note":"Huis is a het-word in the singular: ons. Buren is a plural: onze."},{"nl":"Hebben jullie je sleutels bij je?","en":"Do you (plural) have your keys with you?","hint":["hebben","sleutel","bij je hebben"],"note":"With jullie as the subject, the possessive is usually je; jullie jullie sleutels is not wrong, but sounds clumsy.","alt":["Hebben jullie jullie sleutels bij je?"]},{"nl":"Is dit uw portemonnee, meneer?","en":"Is this your wallet, sir?","hint":["portemonnee","meneer"],"note":"To someone you call u, the possessive is uw."},{"nl":"Mijn buren zijn weg, dus ik voer hun kat.","en":"My neighbours are away, so I am feeding their cat.","hint":["buur","weg","dus","voeren","kat"],"note":"Hun before a noun means 'their'. Never hen kat: hen only means 'them'.","alt":["Mijn buren zijn weg, dus voer ik hun kat."]},{"nl":"Ik leen vandaag Peters auto.","en":"Today I am borrowing Peter's car.","hint":["lenen","vandaag","auto"],"note":"A name + s shows the owner. Peter ends in a consonant, so there is no apostrophe: Peters.","alt":["Vandaag leen ik Peters auto.","Ik leen vandaag de auto van Peter.","Vandaag leen ik de auto van Peter."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn zus zorgt goed voor ___ eigen konijn.","options":["zijn","haar","hun"],"correct":1,"en":"My sister takes good care of her own rabbit.","note":"The owner, mijn zus, is a woman: haar. Konijn is a het-word, but the thing owned does not choose the possessive."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wij wonen hier al tien jaar. Dit is ___ huis.","options":["onze","ons","wij"],"correct":1,"en":"We have lived here for ten years. This is our house.","note":"Huis is a het-word in the singular, so ons. Onze is for de-words and plurals."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Van wie is de oplader? Is hij van ___?","options":["jouw","jou","jij"],"correct":1,"en":"Whose is the charger? Is it yours?","note":"After van comes the object form jou. Jouw is the possessive and needs a noun after it: jouw oplader."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De eigenaar van de hond is een oude vrouw. Ze wandelt elke dag met ___ hond.","options":["zijn","hun","haar"],"correct":2,"en":"The owner of the dog is an old woman. She walks with her dog every day.","note":"The owner is a woman, so haar, even though de eigenaar is a de-word. Zijn would mean someone else's dog."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ kat slaapt de hele dag.","options":["Ons","Onze","Wij"],"correct":1,"en":"Our cat sleeps all day.","note":"Kat is a de-word, so onze. Ons is only for a het-word in the singular: ons konijn."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De buren lenen vaak ___ (wij) ladder.","answers":["onze"],"en":"The neighbours often borrow our ladder.","note":"Ladder is a de-word, so the possessive of wij is onze."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zetten jullie ___ (jullie) fietsen in de gang?","answers":["je","jullie"],"en":"Are you (plural) putting your bikes in the hall?","note":"With jullie as the subject the possessive is usually je. Jullie fietsen is also correct, but sounds heavier."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn buren zijn op vakantie, dus ik zorg voor ___ (zij, they) konijn.","answers":["hun"],"en":"My neighbours are on holiday, so I am looking after their rabbit.","note":"'Their' is hun. Hen and ze mean 'them' and cannot stand before a noun."},{"type":"order","lead":"Dit","words":["de fiets","is","mijn zus","van"],"answers":["Dit is de fiets van mijn zus."],"en":"This is my sister's bike.","note":"With van the owner comes after the thing: de fiets van mijn zus. The s is mainly for names: Anna's fiets."},{"type":"order","lead":"Mijn huisgenoot","words":["deelt","zijn eten","nooit","met mij"],"answers":["Mijn huisgenoot deelt zijn eten nooit met mij.","Mijn huisgenoot deelt nooit zijn eten met mij."],"en":"My housemate never shares his food with me.","note":"The owner of the food is the housemate, a man: zijn eten. After met comes the object form mij."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use the name with s instead of van.","from":"Dit is de auto van Peter.","answers":["Dit is Peters auto."],"en":"This is Peter's car.","note":"Name + s, without an apostrophe after a consonant: Peters auto. The article de disappears."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use van instead of the name with s.","from":"Mijn oplader is kapot, dus ik leen Anna's oplader.","answers":["Mijn oplader is kapot, dus ik leen de oplader van Anna."],"en":"My charger is broken, so I am borrowing Anna's charger.","note":"With van the article comes back: de oplader van Anna. Anna's has an apostrophe because the name ends in a."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Change the subject to mijn zus.","from":"Mijn broer zorgt voor zijn konijn.","answers":["Mijn zus zorgt voor haar konijn."],"en":"My sister looks after her rabbit.","note":"With a new owner the possessive changes too: zijn becomes haar. Dutch has no word like свій that stays the same."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Change the subject to wij.","from":"Ik geef mijn huisdieren elke ochtend eten.","answers":["Wij geven onze huisdieren elke ochtend eten."],"en":"We give our pets food every morning.","note":"Wij needs the plural verb geven, and the possessive follows the owner: onze, because huisdieren is a plural."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Is dit jou sleutel?","answers":["Is dit jouw sleutel?","Is dit je sleutel?"],"en":"Is this your key?","note":"'Your' is jouw (or short je). Jou means 'you', as in van jou."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn zus heeft nu zijn eigen kamer.","answers":["Mijn zus heeft nu haar eigen kamer."],"en":"My sister has her own room now.","note":"Eigen means the owner is the subject, mijn zus, so it must be haar. Zijn eigen would only fit a man or a het-word."}]},
  {"id":"a2-10","level":"A2","icon":"👕","title":"Clothes and colours","grammar":"Adjectives: with or without -e","summary":"After the verb an adjective never changes: de trui is rood. Before a noun it gets -e (een rode trui, het rode vest), except with a het-word in the singular after een, geen or no article: een rood vest.","explain":"An adjective (*bijvoeglijk naamwoord*) describes a noun: red, warm, tight. Dutch adjectives have only two forms, with and without `-e`: `rood` and `rode`. Which one you need depends on where the adjective stands and, before a noun, on the noun.\n\n### After the verb: no change\n\nAfter `zijn`, `worden` and `blijven` the adjective says something about the subject and never changes, whatever the noun is.\n\n> Mijn nieuwe trui is paars. — My new sweater is purple.\n> Het witte overhemd is te groot. — The white shirt is too big.\n\n### Before the noun: -e, with one exception\n\nBefore a noun the adjective gets `-e`. The **one exception**: a het-word in the singular after `een`, `geen` or no article at all. Then there is no `-e`.\n\n| | de, het, mijn, dit … | een, geen, no article |\n| de-word | de rode trui | een rode trui |\n| het-word | het rode vest | een rood vest |\n| plural | de rode truien | rode truien |\n\nSo only one box in the table has no `-e`; with no article that is a het-word like `mooi weer` or `koud water`. That means:\n\n- a **de-word** always gets `-e`: `een warme trui`, `de warme trui`.\n- a **plural** always gets `-e`: `warme truien`, `geen warme truien`.\n- `het`, `dit`, `dat` and every possessive (`mijn`, `jouw`, `ons` …) bring the `-e` back, also with a het-word: `het nieuwe pak`, `dit oude vest`, `mijn nieuwe overhemd`.\n\n> Hij draagt een wit overhemd en een grijs pak. — He is wearing a white shirt and a grey suit.\n> Ik zoek een warm vest voor de winter. — I am looking for a warm cardigan for the winter.\n> Ze draagt een strakke spijkerbroek en een wijde trui. — She is wearing tight jeans and a loose sweater.\n\nThe same exception holds after `elk`, `ieder`, `veel`, `weinig`, `welk`, `zo'n` and `wat voor (een)`: `zo'n lelijk vest`, `elk klein kind`. With a de-word or a plural the `-e` stays: `zo'n lelijke trui`.\n\n### Spelling when -e is added\n\nThe `-e` adds a syllable, and the spelling follows the same rules as with verbs (unit A2 · 1):\n\n- a double vowel loses one letter when it ends the syllable: `groot → gro-te`, `geel → ge-le`. Before two consonants it stays: `paars → paarse`.\n- a short vowel doubles the consonant: `wit → witte`, `dun → dunne`, `strak → strakke`.\n- f and s: after a short vowel they double like any consonant: `fris → frisse`, `laf → laffe`. After a long vowel or `ij`, `ei`, `ui` they usually become v and z, as you hear it: `lief → lieve`, `vies → vieze`, `grijs → grijze`, `boos → boze`. After a consonant they mostly stay: `paars → paarse`, `vals → valse`, but `half → halve`. There are exceptions (`heus → heuse`), so check a new word in the dictionary.\n\n### Adjectives that never get -e\n\n- materials ending in `-en`: `wollen`, `leren`, `houten`, `gouden`, `zijden`. The same goes for `eigen` and `open`.\n- `oranje`, `roze`, `lila`, `plastic` and `nylon`.\n\n> Mijn oma breit een wollen sjaal voor me. — My grandmother is knitting a woollen scarf for me.\n> Ze draagt een roze jurk met een oranje sjaal. — She is wearing a pink dress with an orange scarf.\n\nAfter the verb you name the material with `van`: `Mijn laarzen zijn van leer`; before the noun it is `leren laarzen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- After the verb, no `-e`: `De trui is rood`, never *De trui is rode.\n- A het-word after `een`: `een zwart pak`, not *een zwarte pak.\n- With `het` or a possessive the `-e` returns: `het rode vest`, `mijn nieuwe pak`, not *het rood vest or *mijn nieuw pak.\n- A material in `-en` stays as it is: `een wollen sjaal`, not *een wollene sjaal.\n- Check the spelling: `witte`, `grote`, not *wite or *groote.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian adjectives agree with the noun in gender, number and case: `червоний, червона, червоне, червоні` and all their case forms. Dutch has only this one choice, with or without `-e`, and the only thing that can take the `-e` away is a het-word in the singular after `een`, `geen` or nothing. Comparing (`groter`, `het grootst`) is unit A2 · 19.","words":[{"nl":"trui","art":"de","pl":"truien","en":"sweater / jumper","pos":"n"},{"nl":"spijkerbroek","art":"de","pl":"spijkerbroeken","en":"jeans","pos":"n"},{"nl":"jurk","art":"de","pl":"jurken","en":"dress","pos":"n"},{"nl":"overhemd","art":"het","pl":"overhemden","en":"shirt (with buttons)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vest","art":"het","pl":"vesten","en":"cardigan","pos":"n"},{"nl":"sjaal","art":"de","pl":"sjaals","en":"scarf","pos":"n"},{"nl":"laars","art":"de","pl":"laarzen","en":"boot","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stof","art":"de","pl":"stoffen","en":"fabric / material","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wol","art":"de","pl":"","en":"wool","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"leer","art":"het","pl":"","en":"leather","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"paars","en":"purple","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"strak","en":"tight","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"wijd","en":"loose / wide","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"gestreept","en":"striped","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"lelijk","en":"ugly","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"dun","en":"thin","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Mijn nieuwe trui is paars.","en":"My new sweater is purple.","hint":["nieuw","trui","paars"],"note":"Before the noun: nieuwe, with -e after mijn. After is: paars, which never changes."},{"nl":"Ze draagt een strakke spijkerbroek en een wijde trui.","en":"She is wearing tight jeans and a loose sweater.","hint":["dragen","strak","spijkerbroek","wijd","trui"],"note":"Both nouns are de-words, so both adjectives get -e, also after een. Strak has a short a, so the k is doubled: strakke."},{"nl":"Hij draagt een wit overhemd en een grijs pak.","en":"He is wearing a white shirt and a grey suit.","hint":["dragen","wit","overhemd","grijs","pak"],"note":"Overhemd and pak are het-words in the singular after een: the one case without -e."},{"nl":"Het witte overhemd is te groot.","en":"The white shirt is too big.","hint":["wit","overhemd","te","groot"],"note":"With het the -e comes back: het witte overhemd, with tt for the short i. Groot after is does not change."},{"nl":"Ik zoek een warm vest voor de winter.","en":"I am looking for a warm cardigan for the winter.","hint":["zoeken","warm","vest","winter"],"note":"Vest is a het-word, singular, after een: warm without -e. With a de-word it would be een warme trui."},{"nl":"Mijn laarzen zijn van leer.","en":"My boots are made of leather.","hint":["laars","zijn","van","leer"],"note":"After the verb the material is van leer. Before the noun it would be leren laarzen, and leren never gets -e.","alt":["Mijn laarzen zijn gemaakt van leer.","Mijn laarzen zijn van leer gemaakt."]},{"nl":"Mijn oma breit een wollen sjaal voor me.","en":"My grandmother is knitting a woollen scarf for me.","hint":["oma","breien","wollen","sjaal"],"note":"Sjaal is a de-word, but wollen is a material in -en and never gets an -e.","alt":["Mijn oma breit een wollen sjaal voor mij.","Mijn oma breit voor me een wollen sjaal.","Mijn oma breit voor mij een wollen sjaal."]},{"nl":"Ze draagt een roze jurk met een oranje sjaal.","en":"She is wearing a pink dress with an orange scarf.","hint":["dragen","roze","jurk","met","oranje","sjaal"],"note":"Roze and oranje already end in -e and never change: never rozee or oranjee."},{"nl":"De gestreepte stof is mooi, maar te dun.","en":"The striped fabric is nice, but too thin.","hint":["gestreept","stof","mooi","te","dun"],"note":"Before the noun: gestreepte. After is, mooi and dun stay as they are; before a noun dun would become dunne."},{"nl":"Zo'n lelijk vest draag ik nooit.","en":"I never wear an ugly cardigan like that.","hint":["zo'n","lelijk","vest","dragen","nooit"],"note":"Zo'n works like een: with a het-word in the singular the adjective has no -e. With a de-word: zo'n lelijke trui.","alt":["Ik draag nooit zo'n lelijk vest.","Ik draag zo'n lelijk vest nooit."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De jurk is ___.","options":["rode","rood","roode"],"correct":1,"en":"The dress is red.","note":"After the verb the adjective never changes: rood. Roode is wrong spelling: the oo is only needed when the syllable is closed."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik zoek ___ voor mijn zoon.","options":["een warme vest","een warm vest","het warm vest","de warme vest"],"correct":1,"en":"I am looking for a warm cardigan for my son.","note":"Vest is a het-word: so not de, and after een there is no -e. After het it would be het warme vest."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Hij draagt vandaag het ___ overhemd.","options":["wit","wite","witte"],"correct":2,"en":"He is wearing the white shirt today.","note":"After het the adjective gets -e. The i in wit is short, so the t is doubled: witte."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Oma geeft me een ___ sjaal.","options":["wollen","wollene","wol"],"correct":0,"en":"Grandma is giving me a woollen scarf.","note":"Adjectives of material in -en never get -e: een wollen sjaal. Wol is the noun (wool)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn ___ spijkerbroek is te strak.","options":["oud","oude","ouden"],"correct":1,"en":"My old jeans are too tight.","note":"Spijkerbroek is a de-word, and after mijn the adjective always gets -e: oude."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik zoek ___ (lang) laarzen voor de winter.","answers":["lange"],"en":"I am looking for long boots for the winter.","note":"A plural always gets -e, also without an article: lange laarzen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij draagt zijn ___ (nieuw) pak op de bruiloft.","answers":["nieuwe"],"en":"He is wearing his new suit at the wedding.","note":"Pak is a het-word, but after a possessive like zijn the adjective gets -e. The exception is only after een, geen or no article."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zo'n ___ (dun) overhemd is te koud voor de winter.","answers":["dun"],"en":"A thin shirt like that is too cold for the winter.","note":"Zo'n works like een: overhemd is a het-word in the singular, so no -e."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ze draagt een ___ (grijs) trui.","answers":["grijze"],"en":"She is wearing a grey sweater.","note":"Trui is a de-word, so -e. In grijs the s becomes z when -e is added: grijze, as you hear it."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit is geen ___ (goedkoop) overhemd.","answers":["goedkoop"],"en":"This is not a cheap shirt.","note":"Geen works like een: a het-word in the singular after geen has no -e."},{"type":"order","lead":"Vandaag","words":["draagt","ze","een","paarse","jurk"],"answers":["Vandaag draagt ze een paarse jurk."],"en":"Today she is wearing a purple dress.","note":"The adjective stands between een and the noun. Jurk is a de-word, so paarse, with -e."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences into one, with the adjective before the noun.","from":"Ik koop een vest. Het vest is groen.","answers":["Ik koop een groen vest."],"en":"I am buying a green cardigan.","note":"After is the adjective is groen. Before a het-word after een it stays groen: een groen vest."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace een by mijn.","from":"Ik draag vandaag een gestreept overhemd.","answers":["Ik draag vandaag mijn gestreepte overhemd."],"en":"Today I am wearing my striped shirt.","note":"After een a het-word has no -e, but after mijn it does: mijn gestreepte overhemd."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it plural.","from":"Dat is een lelijk vest.","answers":["Dat zijn lelijke vesten."],"en":"Those are ugly cardigans.","note":"In the plural een disappears, the verb becomes zijn, and a plural always gets -e: lelijke vesten."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik zoek een zwarte pak voor het feest.","answers":["Ik zoek een zwart pak voor het feest."],"en":"I am looking for a black suit for the party.","note":"Pak is a het-word, singular, after een: no -e. Zwarte would be right with de, het or a plural."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn spijkerbroek is te wijde.","answers":["Mijn spijkerbroek is te wijd."],"en":"My jeans are too loose.","note":"After the verb the adjective never gets -e: te wijd."}]},
  {"id":"a2-11","level":"A2","icon":"🩺","title":"At the doctor's","grammar":"Modal verbs: kunnen, moeten, mogen, willen, zullen","summary":"kan, moet, mag, wil, zal: no t for hij. The modal is the conjugated verb; the other verb goes to the end as an infinitive. niet mogen = must not; niet hoeven te = do not have to.","explain":"Modal verbs (*modale werkwoorden*) say whether something is possible, necessary, allowed or wanted: can, must, may, want. They are irregular, and they change the word order: the modal is the conjugated verb, and the verb that says what happens goes to the end.\n\n### The forms\n\n| | kunnen | moeten | mogen |\n| ik | kan | moet | mag |\n| jij / je, u | kunt / kan | moet | mag |\n| hij, zij, het | kan | moet | mag |\n| wij, jullie, zij | kunnen | moeten | mogen |\n\n| | willen | zullen |\n| ik | wil | zal |\n| jij / je, u | wilt / wil | zult / zal |\n| hij, zij, het | wil | zal |\n| wij, jullie, zij | willen | zullen |\n\n- The hij-form has **no t**: `hij kan`, `hij mag`, `hij wil`, `hij zal`. (`moet` ends in t because the stem does.)\n- With jij, both forms are correct: `jij kunt` and `jij kan`. With u, `kunt` and `wilt` are the usual written forms.\n- After the verb: `kun je` or `kan je`, `wil je`, `zul je` or `zal je`; the t drops as with every verb. With u the usual forms keep it: `kunt u`, `wilt u` (`kan u` and `wil u` are also correct, but less usual in the Netherlands).\n\n### Word order: the infinitive at the end\n\nThe modal stands where the conjugated verb always stands: second in a statement, first in a yes/no question. The other verb is an **infinitive at the end**. A separable verb stays in one piece there.\n\n> Ik moet morgen naar de huisarts gaan. — I have to go to the GP tomorrow.\n> Kunt u mijn keel onderzoeken? — Can you examine my throat?\n> Je moet dit medicijn drie keer per dag innemen. — You have to take this medicine three times a day.\n\n### What they mean\n\n- `kunnen`: can, be able to; also \"is possible\".\n- `moeten`: must, have to.\n- `mogen`: may, be allowed to.\n- `willen`: want to. **Never** the English \"will\": `Ik wil komen` is \"I want to come\".\n- `zullen`: an offer or a proposal in a question. (For the future and for promises see unit A2 · 17.)\n\n> Zal ik de apotheek voor je bellen? — Shall I call the pharmacy for you?\n\n### The negative: the real trap\n\n`niet mogen` means **must not**: it is not allowed. For \"do not have to\" Dutch uses a different verb, `hoeven` (ik hoef, hij hoeft, wij hoeven), with `niet` or `geen` and **te** before the infinitive.\n\n> Met koorts mag je niet sporten. — You are not allowed to do sport with a fever.\n> Je hoeft niet te komen. — You don't have to come.\n> Je hoeft geen afspraak te maken. — You don't need to make an appointment.\n\n`Je moet niet …` is not \"you don't have to\"; it is advice, \"you shouldn't\": `Je moet niet zo veel werken`. So: not allowed is `mag niet`, not necessary is `hoeft niet te`.\n\n### Without an infinitive\n\nWhen it is clear what is meant, the infinitive is left out. This is very common with a direction and with `dat`.\n\n> Ik moet naar huis. — I have to go home.\n> Dat kan niet. — That is not possible.\n> Dat mag niet. — That is not allowed.\n> Wil je een glas water? — Do you want a glass of water?\n\n### Polite requests\n\n- Asking for something: `Ik wil graag een afspraak maken` (without `graag`, `ik wil` sounds blunt).\n- Asking someone to do something: `Kunt u …?`\n- Asking permission: `Mag ik …?` `Mag ik hier mijn recept ophalen?`\n- Offering or proposing: `Zal ik …?` `Zullen we gaan?`\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No t for hij: `hij wil`, never *hij wilt; `zij kan`, never *zij kant.\n- The infinitive goes last: `Ik moet morgen werken`, not *Ik moet werken morgen.\n- No te after the five modals: `Ik moet gaan`, not *Ik moet te gaan. Only `hoeven` takes te.\n- \"Do not have to\" is `hoeven niet te`: `Je hoeft niet te betalen`, not *Je mag niet betalen (that means you are not allowed to).\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian also puts an infinitive after `могти`, `мусити` or `хотіти`, where its place is free; in Dutch it goes to the end: `Я мушу завтра йти до лікаря` is `Ik moet morgen naar de dokter gaan`. The negatives match well: `не можна` is `mag niet`, `не треба` is `hoeft niet`.","words":[{"nl":"huisarts","art":"de","pl":"huisartsen","en":"GP / family doctor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"apotheek","art":"de","pl":"apotheken","en":"pharmacy","pos":"n"},{"nl":"recept","art":"het","pl":"recepten","en":"prescription / recipe","pos":"n"},{"nl":"medicijn","art":"het","pl":"medicijnen","en":"medicine","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pil","art":"de","pl":"pillen","en":"pill","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pijn","art":"de","pl":"pijnen","en":"pain","pos":"n","tag":"pijn doen: to hurt"},{"nl":"koorts","art":"de","pl":"","en":"fever / temperature","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"keel","art":"de","pl":"kelen","en":"throat","pos":"n"},{"nl":"buik","art":"de","pl":"buiken","en":"belly / stomach","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rug","art":"de","pl":"ruggen","en":"back (of the body)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"spreekuur","art":"het","pl":"spreekuren","en":"consulting hours / surgery hours","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hoesten","past":"hoestte","perf":"heeft gehoest","en":"to cough","pos":"v"},{"nl":"slikken","past":"slikte","perf":"heeft geslikt","en":"to swallow","pos":"v"},{"nl":"onderzoeken","past":"onderzocht","perf":"heeft onderzocht","en":"to examine","pos":"v"},{"nl":"innemen","past":"nam in","perf":"heeft ingenomen","en":"to take (medicine)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verkouden","en":"having a cold","pos":"adj","tag":"verkouden zijn"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik moet morgen naar de huisarts.","en":"I have to go to the GP tomorrow.","hint":["moeten","morgen","huisarts"],"note":"With a direction the infinitive gaan is usually left out: Ik moet naar … is enough.","alt":["Ik moet morgen naar de huisarts gaan.","Morgen moet ik naar de huisarts.","Morgen moet ik naar de huisarts gaan."]},{"nl":"Kunt u mijn keel onderzoeken?","en":"Can you (formal) examine my throat?","hint":["kunnen","keel","onderzoeken"],"note":"Kunt u …? is a polite request. The modal comes first in the question and onderzoeken goes to the end.","alt":["Kan u mijn keel onderzoeken?"]},{"nl":"Je moet dit medicijn drie keer per dag innemen.","en":"You have to take this medicine three times a day.","hint":["moeten","medicijn","keer","per dag","innemen"],"note":"The separable verb innemen stays in one piece at the end, after the modal moet.","alt":["Jij moet dit medicijn drie keer per dag innemen.","Dit medicijn moet je drie keer per dag innemen."]},{"nl":"Met koorts mag je niet sporten.","en":"You are not allowed to do sport with a fever.","hint":["koorts","mogen","niet","sporten"],"note":"Niet mogen means it is not allowed. It is never 'you don't have to'.","alt":["Je mag met koorts niet sporten.","Jij mag met koorts niet sporten.","Met koorts mag jij niet sporten.","Je mag niet sporten met koorts.","Jij mag niet sporten met koorts."]},{"nl":"Je hoeft geen afspraak te maken voor het spreekuur.","en":"You don't need to make an appointment for the consulting hours.","hint":["hoeven","afspraak","maken","spreekuur"],"note":"'Don't need to' is hoeven with geen or niet, and te before the infinitive: hoeft geen … te maken.","alt":["Voor het spreekuur hoef je geen afspraak te maken.","Jij hoeft geen afspraak te maken voor het spreekuur.","Voor het spreekuur hoef jij geen afspraak te maken.","Je hoeft voor het spreekuur geen afspraak te maken.","Jij hoeft voor het spreekuur geen afspraak te maken."]},{"nl":"Mijn dochter kan niet goed slikken, want haar keel doet pijn.","en":"My daughter cannot swallow properly, because her throat hurts.","hint":["dochter","kunnen","goed","slikken","keel","pijn doen"],"note":"Kan for zij, without t. Niet goed stands before the infinitive slikken at the end of the first clause."},{"nl":"Mag ik hier mijn recept ophalen?","en":"Can I pick up my prescription here?","hint":["mogen","hier","recept","ophalen"],"note":"Mag ik …? asks permission. Ophalen is separable and stays together at the end.","alt":["Kan ik hier mijn recept ophalen?","Mag ik mijn recept hier ophalen?","Kan ik mijn recept hier ophalen?"]},{"nl":"Wil je een glas water?","en":"Do you want a glass of water?","hint":["willen","glas","water"],"note":"Jij wilt, but with je after the verb the t drops: wil je. No infinitive is needed here.","alt":["Wil jij een glas water?"]},{"nl":"Zal ik de apotheek voor je bellen?","en":"Shall I call the pharmacy for you?","hint":["zullen","apotheek","bellen"],"note":"Zal ik …? is an offer. Bellen, the infinitive, goes to the end.","alt":["Zal ik de apotheek voor jou bellen?","Zal ik voor je de apotheek bellen?","Zal ik voor jou de apotheek bellen?"]},{"nl":"Hij wil niet naar de huisarts, maar hij hoest al een week.","en":"He doesn't want to go to the GP, but he has been coughing for a week.","hint":["willen","huisarts","hoesten","week"],"note":"Hij wil, never hij wilt: the hij-form of willen has no t. With naar the infinitive gaan can be left out.","alt":["Hij wil niet naar de huisarts gaan, maar hij hoest al een week."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn zoon is verkouden en heeft koorts. Hij ___ vandaag niet naar school.","options":["kant","kan","kunnen"],"correct":1,"en":"My son has a cold and a fever. He can't go to school today.","note":"The hij-form of kunnen is kan, without t. Kunnen is for wij, jullie and zij."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ u morgen om tien uur komen?","options":["Kunnen","Kun","Kunt"],"correct":2,"en":"Can you (formal) come tomorrow at ten o'clock?","note":"With u the usual form is kunt, also in a question: the t only drops after jij/je, so never kun u. Kunnen is plural."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Je ___ niet te komen, want de uitslag is goed.","options":["moet","hoeft","mag"],"correct":1,"en":"You don't have to come, because the result is good.","note":"'Don't have to' is niet hoeven te. Mag niet would mean you are not allowed, and neither moet nor mag takes te."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Met deze pillen ___ je geen alcohol drinken.","options":["hoeft","mag","wilt"],"correct":1,"en":"You must not drink alcohol with these pills.","note":"Not allowed is mogen with geen or niet. Hoeft would need te, and after the verb wilt loses its t: wil je."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ we morgen samen naar de apotheek gaan?","options":["Zal","Zullen","Zult"],"correct":1,"en":"Shall we go to the pharmacy together tomorrow?","note":"A proposal with we: Zullen we …? We is plural, so the plural form zullen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn buik doet pijn. Ik ___ (willen) een afspraak met de huisarts.","answers":["wil"],"en":"My stomach hurts. I want an appointment with the GP.","note":"The ik-form of willen is wil. The modal can stand without an infinitive when the rest is clear."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (kunnen) je morgen terugkomen?","answers":["Kun","Kan"],"en":"Can you come back tomorrow?","note":"With je after the verb both kun je and kan je are correct. The separable verb terugkomen stays together at the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"U ___ (mogen) twee pillen per dag nemen.","answers":["mag"],"en":"You (formal) may take two pills a day.","note":"Mogen is mag for ik, jij, u, hij and zij. There is no t form: never magt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Je hoeft morgen niet ___ werken.","answers":["te"],"en":"You don't have to work tomorrow.","note":"Hoeven needs te before the infinitive. The five modals do not: Je mag morgen niet werken."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["moet","de pillen","na het eten","innemen"],"answers":["Ik moet de pillen na het eten innemen.","Ik moet na het eten de pillen innemen."],"en":"I have to take the pills after the meal.","note":"Moet takes the second place and the infinitive innemen closes the sentence, in one piece."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["mag","ik","weer","sporten"],"answers":["Morgen mag ik weer sporten."],"en":"Tomorrow I am allowed to do sport again.","note":"After Morgen comes the modal mag, then the subject ik. The infinitive sporten goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Kunt","words":["u","naar mijn rug","kijken"],"answers":["Kunt u naar mijn rug kijken?"],"en":"Can you (formal) look at my back?","note":"A yes/no question starts with the modal. Everything else stands between the subject and the infinitive kijken."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Add moeten: I have to …","from":"Ik neem elke ochtend een pil.","answers":["Ik moet elke ochtend een pil nemen."],"en":"I have to take a pill every morning.","note":"Moet becomes the conjugated verb in second place and neem moves to the end as the infinitive nemen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say that it is not necessary.","from":"Je moet een afspraak maken.","answers":["Je hoeft geen afspraak te maken."],"en":"You don't need to make an appointment.","note":"'Not necessary' is hoeven with geen (before een afspraak) and te before the infinitive."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Zij wilt niet naar de huisarts.","answers":["Zij wil niet naar de huisarts."],"en":"She doesn't want to go to the GP.","note":"The hij/zij-form of willen has no t: zij wil. Wilt is only for jij and u."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Je hoeft niet naar de apotheek gaan.","answers":["Je hoeft niet naar de apotheek te gaan.","Je hoeft niet naar de apotheek."],"en":"You don't have to go to the pharmacy.","note":"Hoeven needs te before the infinitive: hoeft niet … te gaan. With a direction gaan can also be left out."}]},
  {"id":"a2-12","level":"A2","icon":"🎉","title":"Making plans","grammar":"Separable verbs","summary":"A separable verb has a stressed prefix (opbellen). In a main clause and the imperative the prefix goes to the end: Ik bel je op. As an infinitive it stays whole: Ik wil je opbellen. be-, ver-, ont- never split.","explain":"A separable verb (*scheidbaar werkwoord*) is a verb with a small word in front, such as `op`, `mee`, `uit` or `af`. In the dictionary it is one word: `opbellen`, `meenemen`, `uitnodigen`. In a normal sentence the two parts often stand far apart. The prefix also changes the meaning: `gaan` is \"to go\", `uitgaan` is \"to go out\", `meegaan` is \"to come along\".\n\n### The rule in a main clause\n\nIn a statement or a question the conjugated part stands where the verb always stands (second in a statement, first in a yes/no question), and the prefix goes to the **end** of the sentence. The conjugated part follows the normal rules of the present tense.\n\n| | opbellen | meenemen |\n| ik | bel … op | neem … mee |\n| jij / je, u, hij, zij | belt … op | neemt … mee |\n| wij, jullie, zij | bellen … op | nemen … mee |\n\n> Ik bel je morgen op. — I will call you tomorrow.\n> Mijn zus neemt een taart mee. — My sister is bringing a cake.\n> Neem je iets te drinken mee? — Are you bringing something to drink?\n\nJij and je directly after the verb lose the t, as always: `Neem je … mee?`, `Ga je mee?`\n\nEverything else comes between the two parts: the object, the time, `niet`. `Niet` stands just before the prefix.\n\n> Het feest gaat zaterdag niet door. — The party is not going ahead on Saturday.\n\nA phrase with a preposition may also come after the prefix, and in speech that is very common: `Ik nodig je uit voor mijn feest` and `Ik nodig je voor mijn feest uit` are both correct.\n\nThe simple past splits in the same way: `Ik belde je op` (unit A2 · 15).\n\n### When it stays in one piece\n\nWhen the verb is an **infinitive** at the end, it is written as one word. That happens after a modal verb, after `gaan` and after `zullen`.\n\n> Ik wil je graag uitnodigen. — I would like to invite you.\n> Kun je me morgen ophalen? — Can you pick me up tomorrow?\n\n| form | example |\n| present | Ik kom vanavond langs. |\n| question | Kom je vanavond langs? |\n| imperative | Kom vanavond langs! |\n| with a modal | Ik kan vanavond langskomen. |\n| perfect | Ik ben langsgekomen. |\n\nThe imperative splits too: `Bel me op!`, `Neem een jas mee!` In the perfect the `ge` goes in the middle: `opgebeld`, `meegenomen`, `uitgenodigd` (units A2 · 13 and A2 · 14). In a sub-clause the two parts come together again at the end; that is unit B1 · 17.\n\n### Separable or not? Listen to the stress\n\nA separable prefix is **stressed**: OPbellen, MEEnemen, UITnodigen, AFspreken. Most of these prefixes are also words of their own: `op, af, uit, mee, aan, in, weg, terug, thuis, langs`.\n\nThe prefixes **be-, ge-, her-, ont-, ver-, er-** are never stressed and **never split**: `betalen, gebruiken, herhalen, ontmoeten, vertellen, ervaren`. The stress is on the verb itself: beTAlen, verTELlen.\n\n> Wie betaalt de drankjes? — Who is paying for the drinks?\n> Ik ontmoet haar bij het station. — I am meeting her at the station.\n\nA few prefixes, such as `door, over, onder, voor` and `om`, can be either, and then the stress decides. `VOORstellen` (to introduce, to propose) splits: `Ik stel je voor aan mijn vrienden`. But `overLEGgen` (to discuss) does not: `We overleggen morgen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The prefix does not stay in front: *Ik opbel je → `Ik bel je op`.\n- Do not forget the prefix: `Hij komt langs` (he drops by) is not `Hij komt` (he is coming). Without `mee`, `Ik neem een fles wijn` means \"I take a bottle of wine\", not \"I bring one\".\n- After a modal verb nothing splits: *Ik wil je nodigen uit → `Ik wil je uitnodigen`.\n- be-, ver-, ont- never split: *Ik taal de drankjes be → `Ik betaal de drankjes`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian also changes a verb's meaning with a prefix (`йти`, `прийти`, `піти`), but the prefix is always glued to the verb. In Dutch it travels to the end: `Я запрошую тебе` is `Ik nodig je uit`. So read a Dutch sentence to the end before you decide what the verb means.","words":[{"nl":"feest","art":"het","pl":"feesten","en":"party","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitnodiging","art":"de","pl":"uitnodigingen","en":"invitation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bioscoop","art":"de","pl":"bioscopen","en":"cinema","pos":"n"},{"nl":"borrel","art":"de","pl":"borrels","en":"drinks (an informal gathering)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kroeg","art":"de","pl":"kroegen","en":"pub / bar","pos":"n"},{"nl":"concert","art":"het","pl":"concerten","en":"concert","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitnodigen","past":"nodigde uit","perf":"heeft uitgenodigd","en":"to invite","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"afspreken","past":"sprak af","perf":"heeft afgesproken","en":"to arrange (to meet) / to agree","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"meenemen","past":"nam mee","perf":"heeft meegenomen","en":"to bring (along) / to take (along)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"meegaan","past":"ging mee","perf":"is meegegaan","en":"to come along / to go along","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"uitgaan","past":"ging uit","perf":"is uitgegaan","en":"to go out (for fun)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"langskomen","past":"kwam langs","perf":"is langsgekomen","en":"to drop by","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"afzeggen","past":"zei af / zegde af","perf":"heeft afgezegd","en":"to cancel / to call off","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"voorstellen","past":"stelde voor","perf":"heeft voorgesteld","en":"to introduce (a person) / to propose","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"doorgaan","past":"ging door","perf":"is doorgegaan","en":"to go ahead / to take place","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"terugbellen","past":"belde terug","perf":"heeft teruggebeld","en":"to call back","pos":"v","tag":"separable"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik nodig mijn buren uit voor mijn feest.","en":"I am inviting my neighbours to my party.","hint":["uitnodigen","buur","feest"],"note":"nodig stands in second place and uit goes towards the end. The phrase voor mijn feest may come after uit or before it.","alt":["Ik nodig mijn buren voor mijn feest uit."]},{"nl":"Neem jij een fles wijn mee?","en":"Are you bringing a bottle of wine?","hint":["meenemen","fles","wijn"],"note":"In a yes/no question neem comes first and mee goes to the very end. Jij follows the verb, so there is no t.","alt":["Neem je een fles wijn mee?"]},{"nl":"Hoe laat spreken we af?","en":"What time shall we meet?","hint":["hoe laat","afspreken"],"note":"After the question word comes spreken, and af closes the question. With zullen the verb would stay whole: Hoe laat zullen we afspreken?","alt":["Hoe laat zullen we afspreken?"]},{"nl":"Mijn zus komt morgenavond langs.","en":"My sister is dropping by tomorrow evening.","hint":["zus","langskomen","morgenavond"],"note":"komt is the conjugated part (stem + t for mijn zus); langs goes to the end. Without langs it would only mean \"is coming\".","alt":["Morgenavond komt mijn zus langs."]},{"nl":"Ga je vanavond mee naar de kroeg?","en":"Are you coming along to the pub tonight?","hint":["meegaan","vanavond","kroeg"],"note":"Ga comes first and mee goes to the end, but the phrase naar de kroeg may follow mee, which is what people usually say.","alt":["Ga jij vanavond mee naar de kroeg?","Ga je vanavond naar de kroeg mee?","Ga jij vanavond naar de kroeg mee?"]},{"nl":"Ik wil je aan mijn vrienden voorstellen.","en":"I want to introduce you to my friends.","hint":["willen","vriend","voorstellen"],"note":"After the modal verb wil, voorstellen is an infinitive at the end and stays in one piece.","alt":["Ik wil jou aan mijn vrienden voorstellen.","Ik wil je voorstellen aan mijn vrienden.","Ik wil jou voorstellen aan mijn vrienden."]},{"nl":"Waarom zeg je de afspraak af?","en":"Why are you cancelling the appointment?","hint":["waarom","afzeggen","afspraak"],"note":"zeg comes straight after the question word, without t because je follows it; af goes to the end, after the object.","alt":["Waarom zeg jij de afspraak af?"]},{"nl":"Het feest gaat zaterdag niet door.","en":"The party is not going ahead on Saturday.","hint":["feest","doorgaan","zaterdag","niet"],"note":"gaat is in second place and door at the end. Niet stands just before the prefix.","alt":["Zaterdag gaat het feest niet door."]},{"nl":"Neem een warme jas mee!","en":"Bring a warm coat!","hint":["meenemen","warm","jas"],"note":"The imperative is the stem, and the separable verb splits here too: Neem … mee."},{"nl":"Op een feest ontmoet je altijd nieuwe mensen.","en":"At a party you always meet new people.","hint":["feest","ontmoeten","altijd","nieuw"],"note":"ontmoeten has the unstressed prefix ont-, so it never splits: ontmoet je, never \"moet je … ont\".","alt":["Je ontmoet op een feest altijd nieuwe mensen.","Je ontmoet altijd nieuwe mensen op een feest."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ je na de bioscoop op.","options":["bel","opbel","belt"],"correct":0,"en":"I will call you after the cinema.","note":"The prefix op is already at the end, so only the conjugated part goes in second place: bel. Ik takes the bare stem, without t."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik moet morgen vroeg ___.","options":["sta op","opstaan","op staan"],"correct":1,"en":"I have to get up early tomorrow.","note":"After the modal moet the separable verb is an infinitive at the end, written as one word: opstaan."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn vriendin ___ elke zaterdag uit.","options":["gaan","uitgaat","gaat"],"correct":2,"en":"My friend goes out every Saturday.","note":"uit is already at the end, so the gap needs only the conjugated part. Mijn vriendin is a hij/zij-subject: stem + t, gaat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wat ___ (meenemen) jij mee naar het feest?","answers":["neem"],"en":"What are you bringing to the party?","note":"Only neem goes in second place; mee is already in the sentence. Jij follows the verb, so there is no t."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn broer ___ (langskomen) vanavond langs.","answers":["komt"],"en":"My brother is dropping by this evening.","note":"The conjugated part of langskomen is komen: mijn broer takes stem + t, komt. The prefix langs is at the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hoe laat ___ (opstaan) je na een feest op?","answers":["sta"],"en":"What time do you get up after a party?","note":"sta is the conjugated part, and je directly after the verb means no t: sta je … op."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Waarom ___ (afzeggen) hij het concert af?","answers":["zegt"],"en":"Why is he cancelling the concert?","note":"The conjugated part zeggen becomes zegt for hij; af stays at the end of the question."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["bel","je","morgen","op"],"answers":["Ik bel je morgen op."],"en":"I will call you tomorrow.","note":"bel is the verb in second place, the pronoun je comes right after it, and op closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Neem","words":["jij","vanavond","je gitaar","mee"],"answers":["Neem jij vanavond je gitaar mee?","Neem jij je gitaar vanavond mee?"],"en":"Are you bringing your guitar tonight?","note":"A yes/no question starts with the verb, then the subject. Mee goes to the very end; vanavond may stand before or after je gitaar."},{"type":"order","lead":"We","words":["willen","jou","voor onze borrel","uitnodigen"],"answers":["We willen jou voor onze borrel uitnodigen.","We willen jou uitnodigen voor onze borrel."],"en":"We want to invite you to our drinks.","note":"After willen the infinitive uitnodigen stays in one piece at the end. The phrase voor onze borrel may also follow it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start the sentence with Morgen.","from":"Ik kom bij je langs.","answers":["Morgen kom ik bij je langs.","Morgen kom ik langs bij je."],"en":"Tomorrow I will drop by at your place.","note":"Morgen takes the first place, kom stays second and ik follows it. The prefix langs stays at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Add the modal verb wil and use the infinitive uitgaan.","from":"Ik ga vanavond uit.","answers":["Ik wil vanavond uitgaan.","Vanavond wil ik uitgaan."],"en":"I want to go out tonight.","note":"wil takes second place, and uitgaan goes to the end as one word."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn it into an instruction (the imperative) to one friend.","from":"Jij neemt de uitnodiging mee.","answers":["Neem de uitnodiging mee."],"en":"Bring the invitation.","note":"The imperative is the stem, neem, at the start. The separable verb splits as in any main clause: mee goes to the end."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik terugbel je vanavond.","answers":["Ik bel je vanavond terug."],"en":"I will call you back this evening.","note":"In a main clause the prefix goes to the end: bel … terug. Terugbellen stays whole only as an infinitive."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik wil al mijn vrienden nodigen uit.","answers":["Ik wil al mijn vrienden uitnodigen."],"en":"I want to invite all my friends.","note":"After a modal verb the separable verb is an infinitive and stays in one piece: uitnodigen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik taal de drankjes be.","answers":["Ik betaal de drankjes."],"en":"I am paying for the drinks.","note":"be- is an unstressed prefix and never splits: betalen, ik betaal. The stress is on -taal."}]},
  {"id":"a2-13","level":"A2","icon":"🧺","title":"Last weekend","grammar":"The perfect tense: regular verbs","summary":"hebben in second place + participle at the end. Participle: ge + stem + t after the letters of 't kofschip, else + d (geleefd: look at the v). No ge- after be-, ver-, ont-; separable: opgeruimd.","explain":"The perfect tense (*de voltooide tijd*) is the normal way to talk about something that is finished, above all in conversation. It covers both English \"I cleaned\" and \"I have cleaned\": `Ik heb de keuken schoongemaakt`. It is made of two parts: a form of `hebben` and a past participle (*het voltooid deelwoord*).\n\n### hebben + participle\n\n| | hebben |\n| ik | heb |\n| jij / je | hebt (heb je?) |\n| u | hebt / heeft |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | heeft |\n| wij, jullie, zij / ze | hebben |\n\n> Ik heb de hele ochtend gewerkt. — I worked all morning.\n> Mijn zus heeft haar kamer geverfd. — My sister has painted her room.\n\n### Making the participle: ge + stem + t or d\n\nTake the stem (the ik-form of the present: `werken → werk`), put `ge` in front and `t` or `d` behind it. Which one? Look at the **last letter of the stem**. If it is one of the consonants of **'t kofschip** (t, k, f, s, ch, p), add `t`. After any other letter, add `d`.\n\n| infinitive | stem | participle |\n| werken | werk | gewerkt |\n| poetsen | poets | gepoetst |\n| koken | kook | gekookt |\n| spelen | speel | gespeeld |\n| dweilen | dweil | gedweild |\n| repareren | repareer | gerepareerd |\n\n**Careful with v and z.** The rule looks at the stem as the infinitive spells it, **before** v becomes f and z becomes s. The stems of `leven`, `verven` and `reizen` end in v or z, which are not in 't kofschip, so you add d: `geleefd`, `geverfd`, `gereisd`. The same goes for `verhuizen`: `verhuisd`. (Reizen and verhuizen often take zijn: unit A2 · 14.)\n\n> We hebben de schuur geverfd. — We painted the shed.\n\n**A stem in t or d gets no extra letter.** A participle never ends in tt or dd: `praten → gepraat`, `wachten → gewacht`, `antwoorden → geantwoord`.\n\n> We hebben de hele avond gepraat. — We talked all evening.\n\n### No ge-\n\nVerbs that start with the unstressed prefixes **be-, ge-, her-, ont-, ver-** do not get an extra `ge`: `betalen → betaald`, `gebruiken → gebruikt`, `herhalen → herhaald`, `ontmoeten → ontmoet`, `vertellen → verteld`. The t or d rule still applies.\n\nSeparable verbs put `ge` **between** the prefix and the verb: `opruimen → opgeruimd`, `schoonmaken → schoongemaakt`, `invullen → ingevuld`.\n\n> Heb jij je kamer al opgeruimd? — Have you tidied your room yet?\n\n### Word order\n\n`hebben` is the conjugated verb, so it stands second in a statement and first in a yes/no question. The participle goes to the **end**. When the sentence starts with a time word, the subject comes after `heb`. `Niet` comes before the participle.\n\n> Zaterdag heb ik de vloer gedweild. — On Saturday I mopped the floor.\n> Ik heb de ramen niet gepoetst. — I did not clean the windows.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- t or d by 't kofschip: `gewerkt`, not *gewerkd; `gespeeld`, not *gespeelt.\n- v and z count as v and z: `geleefd`, not *geleeft; `geverfd`, not *geverft.\n- Never a double letter at the end: `gepraat`, not *gepraatt; `geantwoord`, not *geantwoordt.\n- No ge- after be-, ver-, ont-: `verteld`, not *geverteld. But `verven` has no prefix ver-, so `geverfd`.\n- Separable: `opgeruimd`, not *geopruimd.\n- The participle goes last: `Ik heb mijn fiets gepoetst`, not *Ik heb gepoetst mijn fiets.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has one past tense with no helping verb: `Я прибрала кімнату`. Dutch needs both parts, `Ik heb mijn kamer opgeruimd`, and `heb` can never be left out. Dutch also has no aspect: `прибирала` and `прибрала` can both be `heb opgeruimd` when the action is over. Many common verbs have an irregular participle (`gedaan`, `gegeten`); they come in unit A2 · 14.","words":[{"nl":"klus","art":"de","pl":"klussen","en":"job (around the house) / chore","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schuur","art":"de","pl":"schuren","en":"shed","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stofzuiger","art":"de","pl":"stofzuigers","en":"vacuum cleaner","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gras","art":"het","pl":"","en":"grass","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"prullenbak","art":"de","pl":"prullenbakken","en":"bin / wastepaper basket","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schoonmaken","past":"maakte schoon","perf":"heeft schoongemaakt","en":"to clean","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"opruimen","past":"ruimde op","perf":"heeft opgeruimd","en":"to tidy up","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"stofzuigen","past":"stofzuigde","perf":"heeft gestofzuigd","en":"to vacuum","pos":"v","tag":"not separable"},{"nl":"dweilen","past":"dweilde","perf":"heeft gedweild","en":"to mop","pos":"v"},{"nl":"maaien","past":"maaide","perf":"heeft gemaaid","en":"to mow","pos":"v"},{"nl":"poetsen","past":"poetste","perf":"heeft gepoetst","en":"to clean / to polish","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verven","past":"verfde","perf":"heeft geverfd","en":"to paint (walls, a door)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"tuinieren","past":"tuinierde","perf":"heeft getuinierd","en":"to garden","pos":"v"},{"nl":"repareren","past":"repareerde","perf":"heeft gerepareerd","en":"to repair","pos":"v"},{"nl":"uitrusten","past":"rustte uit","perf":"heeft uitgerust","en":"to rest","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"afgelopen","en":"last (afgelopen weekend) / past","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Afgelopen weekend heb ik de hele keuken schoongemaakt.","en":"Last weekend I cleaned the whole kitchen.","hint":["afgelopen","weekend","heel","keuken","schoonmaken"],"note":"Afgelopen weekend comes first, so heb comes second and ik after it. Schoonmaken is separable: ge goes in the middle, schoongemaakt.","alt":["Ik heb afgelopen weekend de hele keuken schoongemaakt."]},{"nl":"Heb jij de vloer al gedweild?","en":"Have you mopped the floor yet?","hint":["hebben","vloer","al","dweilen"],"note":"In a yes/no question heb comes first, without t because jij follows it. Dweil ends in l, which is not in 't kofschip: gedweild.","alt":["Heb je de vloer al gedweild?"]},{"nl":"Mijn man heeft zaterdag de schuur geverfd.","en":"My husband painted the shed on Saturday.","hint":["man","hebben","zaterdag","schuur","verven"],"note":"verven: the stem ends in v before it becomes f, and v is not in 't kofschip, so the participle ends in d: geverfd.","alt":["Zaterdag heeft mijn man de schuur geverfd.","Mijn man heeft de schuur zaterdag geverfd."]},{"nl":"We hebben de hele middag getuinierd.","en":"We gardened all afternoon.","hint":["hebben","heel","middag","tuinieren"],"note":"English uses the simple past here, Dutch the perfect. Tuinier ends in r, so ge + stem + d.","alt":["De hele middag hebben we getuinierd."]},{"nl":"Wie heeft de stofzuiger gerepareerd?","en":"Who repaired the vacuum cleaner?","hint":["wie","hebben","stofzuiger","repareren"],"note":"Wie takes heeft, like hij. The stem repareer ends in r, so the participle ends in d."},{"nl":"Jullie hebben de prullenbak niet geleegd.","en":"You (plural) have not emptied the bin.","hint":["hebben","prullenbak","niet","legen"],"note":"legen → stem leeg; g is not in 't kofschip, so geleegd. Niet stands just before the participle."},{"nl":"Zondag heb ik eindelijk mijn kamer opgeruimd.","en":"On Sunday I finally tidied my room.","hint":["zondag","hebben","eindelijk","kamer","opruimen"],"note":"opruimen is separable, so ge comes between op and ruimd: opgeruimd. Zondag stands first, so the subject follows heb.","alt":["Ik heb zondag eindelijk mijn kamer opgeruimd.","Ik heb eindelijk zondag mijn kamer opgeruimd.","Zondag heb ik mijn kamer eindelijk opgeruimd.","Ik heb zondag mijn kamer eindelijk opgeruimd."]},{"nl":"Hij heeft de hele ochtend gestofzuigd.","en":"He vacuumed all morning.","hint":["hebben","heel","ochtend","stofzuigen"],"note":"stofzuigen is not separable (ik stofzuig), so ge goes in front: gestofzuigd. Stofzuig ends in g, so d.","alt":["De hele ochtend heeft hij gestofzuigd."]},{"nl":"Na alle klussen hebben we even uitgerust.","en":"After all the jobs we rested for a while.","hint":["na","alle","klus","even","uitrusten"],"note":"The stem rust already ends in t, so nothing is added: uitgerust. Ge goes after the prefix uit.","alt":["We hebben na alle klussen even uitgerust."]},{"nl":"Ik heb mijn vriendin over mijn nieuwe hobby verteld.","en":"I told my friend about my new hobby.","hint":["hebben","vriendin","nieuw","hobby","vertellen"],"note":"vertellen starts with the unstressed prefix ver-, so there is no ge: verteld.","alt":["Ik heb mijn vriendin verteld over mijn nieuwe hobby."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ het gras al gemaaid.","options":["heb","heeft","hebt"],"correct":0,"en":"I have already mowed the grass.","note":"Ik takes heb. Heeft is for hij, zij and het; hebt for jij and u."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb gisteren de ramen ___.","options":["gepoetsd","gepoetst","poetst"],"correct":1,"en":"I cleaned the windows yesterday.","note":"The stem poets ends in s, one of the letters of 't kofschip, so the participle ends in t: gepoetst."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We hebben de deur groen ___.","options":["geverfd","geverft","verfd"],"correct":0,"en":"We painted the door green.","note":"Look at the infinitive verven: the stem ends in v, not f. V is not in 't kofschip, so d: geverfd."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Heb jij de rekening al ___?","options":["gebetaald","betaalt","betaald"],"correct":2,"en":"Have you paid the bill yet?","note":"betalen starts with the unstressed prefix be-, so there is no ge. The stem betaal ends in l: d. Betaalt is the present tense."},{"type":"gap","nl":"We hebben lang over onze hobby's ___ (praten).","answers":["gepraat"],"en":"We talked about our hobbies for a long time.","note":"The stem praat already ends in t, and a participle never ends in tt: gepraat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zij heeft haar fiets zelf ___ (repareren).","answers":["gerepareerd"],"en":"She repaired her bike herself.","note":"ge + repareer + d: the stem ends in r, not one of 't kofschip. Write the long e double, as in the stem."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hebben jullie de keuken al ___ (opruimen)?","answers":["opgeruimd"],"en":"Have you (plural) tidied the kitchen yet?","note":"opruimen is separable, so ge goes after op: opgeruimd. The stem ruim ends in m, so d."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Heb jij de woonkamer al ___ (stofzuigen)?","answers":["gestofzuigd"],"en":"Have you vacuumed the living room yet?","note":"stofzuigen does not split (ik stofzuig), so ge goes in front. The stem ends in g: gestofzuigd."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn opa heeft altijd gezond ___ (leven).","answers":["geleefd"],"en":"My grandfather has always lived healthily.","note":"The stem of leven ends in v in the infinitive. V is not in 't kofschip, so d: geleefd, although you write f."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["heb","ik","mijn fiets","gepoetst"],"answers":["Gisteren heb ik mijn fiets gepoetst."],"en":"Yesterday I cleaned my bike.","note":"Gisteren takes the first place, heb the second, then the subject. The participle closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","de vloer","niet","gedweild"],"answers":["Ik heb de vloer niet gedweild."],"en":"I have not mopped the floor.","note":"Niet comes after the object de vloer and just before the participle at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Heb","words":["jij","de stofzuiger","al","gerepareerd"],"answers":["Heb jij de stofzuiger al gerepareerd?","Heb jij al de stofzuiger gerepareerd?"],"en":"Have you repaired the vacuum cleaner yet?","note":"A yes/no question starts with heb, then the subject. Al usually follows the object de stofzuiger (before it is possible too), and the participle goes last."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect tense.","from":"Ik maak de badkamer schoon.","answers":["Ik heb de badkamer schoongemaakt."],"en":"I have cleaned the bathroom.","note":"heb takes the place of maak, and the participle schoongemaakt, with ge in the middle, goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect tense.","from":"Zaterdag tuinieren we de hele dag.","answers":["Zaterdag hebben we de hele dag getuinierd."],"en":"On Saturday we gardened all day.","note":"hebben takes the second place after Zaterdag, we follows it, and getuinierd goes to the end."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Wij hebben de muur geverft.","answers":["Wij hebben de muur geverfd."],"en":"We painted the wall.","note":"verven: the stem ends in v in the infinitive, and v is not in 't kofschip, so the participle ends in d."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb mijn oma over het feest geverteld.","answers":["Ik heb mijn oma over het feest verteld.","Ik heb mijn oma verteld over het feest."],"en":"I told my grandmother about the party.","note":"vertellen has the unstressed prefix ver-, so no ge is added: verteld."}]},
  {"id":"a2-14","level":"A2","icon":"✈️","title":"On holiday","grammar":"The perfect tense: irregular verbs, hebben or zijn","summary":"Strong verbs: ge + stem + en, often with a new vowel (gelezen, gedronken). zijn for movement to a place and for a change of state (ben gegaan, is begonnen, ben gebleven, ben geweest); hebben for the rest.","explain":"Unit A2 · 13 built the perfect tense with `hebben` and a regular participle (`gewerkt`, `gespeeld`). Two things are still missing: many of the most common verbs have an **irregular participle**, and some verbs take **zijn** instead of `hebben`.\n\n### Strong verbs: ge + stem + en\n\nStrong verbs (*sterke werkwoorden*) end their participle in **-en**, and the vowel often changes. There are patterns, but in the end you learn each verb. More: `blijven → gebleven`, `krijgen → gekregen`, `zwemmen → gezwommen`, `eten → gegeten`, `zien → gezien`.\n\n| pattern | infinitive | participle |\n| ij → e | schrijven, rijden | geschreven, gereden |\n| i → o | drinken, vinden | gedronken, gevonden |\n| e → o | nemen, spreken | genomen, gesproken |\n| ie → o | vliegen | gevlogen |\n| no change | lezen, slapen | gelezen, geslapen |\n\nSome verbs are irregular in other ways: `doen → gedaan`, `hebben → gehad`, `zijn → geweest`, `gaan → gegaan`, `komen → gekomen`. And a few end in **-cht**: `brengen → gebracht`, `kopen → gekocht`, `denken → gedacht`, `zoeken → gezocht`.\n\nThe prefixes work as with regular verbs: `meenemen → meegenomen`, `aankomen → aangekomen`, but `vertrekken → vertrokken` and `beginnen → begonnen`, without ge. The app has a separate irregular-verbs course for learning these forms by heart.\n\n> We hebben veel Spaans gesproken. — We spoke a lot of Spanish.\n> Ik heb op het strand een boek gelezen. — I read a book on the beach.\n\n### zijn instead of hebben\n\nMost verbs take `hebben`. A verb takes `zijn` (ik ben, jij bent, hij is, wij zijn) when it means **movement to somewhere** or a **change of state**:\n\n- `gaan, komen, vertrekken, aankomen, terugkomen, vallen`\n- `worden, beginnen, stoppen, gebeuren, opstaan, groeien, sterven, verhuizen, slagen, trouwen`\n- and two that you simply learn: `blijven` (ben gebleven) and `zijn` itself (ben geweest).\n\n`stoppen`, `verhuizen` and `trouwen` take `hebben` when they have an object, because then someone does something to something: `Ik ben gestopt met roken`, but `De politie heeft de auto gestopt`; `Wij zijn verhuisd`, but `Wie heeft je meubels verhuisd?`\n\n> De trein is om zes uur vertrokken. — The train left at six o'clock.\n> Wij zijn in een mooi hotel gebleven. — We stayed in a nice hotel.\n\n### Movement verbs: where to, or the activity?\n\n`lopen, fietsen, rijden, vliegen, zwemmen, reizen` can take either. Use **zijn** when a destination or direction is given (`naar`, `naar huis`, `naar de overkant`). Use **hebben** when it is about the activity itself: how long, how much, how often.\n\n> Ik ben naar Parijs gevlogen. — I flew to Paris.\n> Ik heb nog nooit gevlogen. — I have never flown.\n> We hebben de hele ochtend gefietst. — We cycled all morning.\n\n### vergeten: both\n\n`vergeten` takes `zijn` or `hebben`, and both are correct: `Ik ben mijn paspoort vergeten`, `Ik heb mijn paspoort vergeten`. With `zijn` it is more common.\n\n### Word order\n\nNothing changes: `ben` or `heb` stands second (first in a yes/no question), the participle goes to the end, and `niet` stands before the participle.\n\n> Ben je wel eens in Italië geweest? — Have you ever been to Italy?\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **I have been** is `Ik ben geweest`, not *Ik heb geweest. English \"have been\" pulls the wrong way here.\n- Going somewhere takes zijn: `Wij zijn vertrokken`, not *Wij hebben vertrokken.\n- No regular ending on a strong verb: `gedronken`, not *gedrinkt; `gekocht`, not *gekoopt; `gebracht`, not *gebrengd.\n- With a destination, a movement verb takes zijn: `Ik ben naar huis gelopen`, but `Ik heb veel gelopen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has no helping verb in the past at all, and English always uses \"have\". Dutch makes you choose, and the choice depends on the meaning of the verb, not on the person. `Я поїхала до Львова` becomes `Ik ben naar Lviv gegaan`, with `ben`.","words":[{"nl":"koffer","art":"de","pl":"koffers","en":"suitcase","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vliegveld","art":"het","pl":"vliegvelden","en":"airport","pos":"n"},{"nl":"camping","art":"de","pl":"campings","en":"campsite","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tent","art":"de","pl":"tenten","en":"tent","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bestemming","art":"de","pl":"bestemmingen","en":"destination","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitzicht","art":"het","pl":"uitzichten","en":"view","pos":"n"},{"nl":"taxi","art":"de","pl":"taxi's","en":"taxi","pos":"n"},{"nl":"paspoort","art":"het","pl":"paspoorten","en":"passport","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rugzak","art":"de","pl":"rugzakken","en":"rucksack / backpack","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vertrekken","past":"vertrok","perf":"is vertrokken","en":"to leave / to depart","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aankomen","past":"kwam aan","perf":"is aangekomen","en":"to arrive","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"vliegen","past":"vloog","perf":"heeft/is gevlogen","en":"to fly","pos":"v"},{"nl":"terugkomen","past":"kwam terug","perf":"is teruggekomen","en":"to come back","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"huren","past":"huurde","perf":"heeft gehuurd","en":"to rent / to hire","pos":"v"},{"nl":"genieten","past":"genoot","perf":"heeft genoten","en":"to enjoy","pos":"v","tag":"genieten van"},{"nl":"onderweg","en":"on the way","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Vorig jaar zijn we naar Portugal gevlogen.","en":"Last year we flew to Portugal.","hint":["vorig","jaar","zijn","vliegen"],"note":"naar Portugal gives a destination, so vliegen takes zijn here. The participle gevlogen is strong: ie becomes o.","alt":["We zijn vorig jaar naar Portugal gevlogen."]},{"nl":"Ik heb nog nooit in een tent geslapen.","en":"I have never slept in a tent.","hint":["hebben","nog nooit","tent","slapen"],"note":"slapen is a strong verb whose vowel stays the same: geslapen, with -en. Sleeping is not a movement, so hebben."},{"nl":"Hoe laat is de trein vertrokken?","en":"What time did the train leave?","hint":["hoe laat","trein","vertrekken"],"note":"vertrekken means going away from a place, so it takes zijn. The prefix ver- means there is no ge: vertrokken."},{"nl":"Na tien uur zijn we eindelijk op onze bestemming aangekomen.","en":"After ten hours we finally arrived at our destination.","hint":["na","uur","eindelijk","bestemming","aankomen"],"note":"aankomen takes zijn (movement to a place). It is separable, so ge goes in the middle: aangekomen.","alt":["We zijn na tien uur eindelijk op onze bestemming aangekomen."]},{"nl":"Op vakantie hebben we elke dag gezwommen.","en":"On holiday we swam every day.","hint":["vakantie","hebben","elke dag","zwemmen"],"note":"No destination, just the activity, so hebben. Zwemmen is strong: i becomes o, gezwommen.","alt":["We hebben op vakantie elke dag gezwommen."]},{"nl":"Ik ben mijn paspoort vergeten!","en":"I have forgotten my passport!","hint":["zijn","paspoort","vergeten"],"note":"vergeten can take zijn or hebben; ben is the more common choice. The participle looks like the infinitive: vergeten.","alt":["Ik heb mijn paspoort vergeten!"]},{"nl":"Wat heb je in je koffer meegenomen?","en":"What did you bring in your suitcase?","hint":["wat","hebben","koffer","meenemen"],"note":"nemen → genomen (e becomes o), and the separable prefix mee goes in front: meegenomen. Taking something is not moving to a place: hebben.","alt":["Wat heb jij in je koffer meegenomen?","Wat heb je meegenomen in je koffer?","Wat heb jij meegenomen in je koffer?"]},{"nl":"Ben je wel eens in het buitenland geweest?","en":"Have you ever been abroad?","hint":["zijn","wel eens","buitenland"],"note":"zijn takes zijn: ben … geweest. English \"have been\" tempts you to say heb, but that is wrong.","alt":["Ben jij wel eens in het buitenland geweest?","Ben je weleens in het buitenland geweest?","Ben jij weleens in het buitenland geweest?","Ben je ooit in het buitenland geweest?","Ben jij ooit in het buitenland geweest?"]},{"nl":"In Kroatië hebben we een auto gehuurd.","en":"In Croatia we rented a car.","hint":["Kroatië","hebben","auto","huren"],"note":"huren is a regular verb with hebben: ge + huur + d. Not every travel verb takes zijn, only movement to somewhere.","alt":["We hebben in Kroatië een auto gehuurd."]},{"nl":"Ze zijn gisteren uit Spanje teruggekomen.","en":"They came back from Spain yesterday.","hint":["zijn","gisteren","Spanje","terugkomen"],"note":"terugkomen is movement, so zijn. Komen keeps its vowel (gekomen), and ge goes after the prefix terug.","alt":["Gisteren zijn ze uit Spanje teruggekomen.","Ze zijn gisteren teruggekomen uit Spanje."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Wij ___ naar Spanje gereden.","options":["hebben","zijn","worden"],"correct":1,"en":"We drove to Spain.","note":"naar Spanje is a destination, so rijden takes zijn. Without a destination it would be hebben: We hebben veel gereden."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb vandaag zes uur ___.","options":["gerijd","gereden","gerijden"],"correct":1,"en":"I drove for six hours today.","note":"rijden is a strong verb: ij becomes e and the participle ends in -en, gereden. It is about the activity, so hebben."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Heb je op vakantie veel boeken ___?","options":["geleesd","gelezen","gelezd"],"correct":1,"en":"Did you read a lot of books on holiday?","note":"lezen is strong: the participle is ge + lez + en, gelezen. The regular d or t ending does not exist for it."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De bus ___ om acht uur vertrokken.","options":["heeft","hebben","is"],"correct":2,"en":"The bus left at eight o'clock.","note":"vertrekken is movement away from a place, so it takes zijn. De bus is singular: is."},{"type":"gap","nl":"We hebben een taxi naar het vliegveld ___ (nemen).","answers":["genomen"],"en":"We took a taxi to the airport.","note":"nemen is strong: e becomes o, genomen. The destination belongs to the taxi ride; the verb itself is nemen, which always takes hebben."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik ___ (zijn) twee keer in Rome geweest.","answers":["ben"],"en":"I have been to Rome twice.","note":"zijn makes its perfect with zijn: ik ben geweest. Never heb geweest."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zij zijn een week langer ___ (blijven).","answers":["gebleven"],"en":"They stayed a week longer.","note":"blijven is strong: ij becomes e, gebleven. It is one of the verbs that take zijn although nothing moves."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hebben jullie onderweg een goed hotel ___ (vinden)?","answers":["gevonden"],"en":"Did you (plural) find a good hotel on the way?","note":"vinden is strong: i becomes o, gevonden. Not gevind or gevindt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"We hebben erg van het uitzicht ___ (genieten).","answers":["genoten"],"en":"We really enjoyed the view.","note":"genieten starts with the prefix ge-, so no second ge is added. It is strong: ie becomes o, genoten."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["heb","ik","mijn rugzak","ingepakt"],"answers":["Gisteren heb ik mijn rugzak ingepakt."],"en":"Yesterday I packed my rucksack.","note":"heb stands second after Gisteren, the subject follows, and the participle ingepakt goes last."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hoe laat","words":["zijn","jullie","in het hotel","aangekomen"],"answers":["Hoe laat zijn jullie in het hotel aangekomen?"],"en":"What time did you (plural) arrive at the hotel?","note":"After the question words comes the auxiliary zijn (aankomen takes zijn), then the subject. The participle closes the question."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect tense.","from":"We gaan met de trein naar Berlijn.","answers":["We zijn met de trein naar Berlijn gegaan."],"en":"We went to Berlin by train.","note":"gaan takes zijn and has the participle gegaan. The participle goes to the very end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect tense.","from":"Ik zwem elke ochtend in het meer.","answers":["Ik heb elke ochtend in het meer gezwommen."],"en":"I swam in the lake every morning.","note":"in het meer says where, not where to: it is about the activity, so hebben. Zwemmen → gezwommen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect tense.","from":"Wij fietsen naar de camping.","answers":["Wij zijn naar de camping gefietst."],"en":"We cycled to the campsite.","note":"naar de camping is a destination, so zijn. Fietsen itself is regular: the stem fiets ends in s, so gefietst."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb vorig jaar in Griekenland geweest.","answers":["Ik ben vorig jaar in Griekenland geweest."],"en":"I was in Greece last year.","note":"The perfect of zijn takes zijn: ik ben geweest. \"Heb geweest\" is a very common mistake, because English says \"have been\"."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ze heeft een nieuwe tent gekoopt.","answers":["Ze heeft een nieuwe tent gekocht."],"en":"She bought a new tent.","note":"kopen is irregular: gekocht, with -cht. The regular form gekoopt does not exist."}]},
  {"id":"a2-15","level":"A2","icon":"🧒","title":"When I was a child","grammar":"The simple past: regular verbs","summary":"Stem + te after the letters of 't kofschip, else + de; plural + n: werkte(n), speelde(n). leven → leefde. Stems in t or d double: praatte, antwoordde. Irregular: was/waren, had/hadden.","explain":"The simple past (*de onvoltooid verleden tijd*, often just *de o.v.t.*) is the tense for **what things were like** in the past, for **habits** and things that happened again and again, and for **telling a story**. It is one word: `Als kind speelde ik veel buiten`. It often goes with `vroeger` (in the past, \"used to\"), `toen` (then, at that time), `als kind`, `altijd` and `elke zomer`.\n\n### The form: stem + te or de\n\nTake the stem, as in the present tense. Then use the same rule as for the participle (unit A2 · 13): after a letter of **'t kofschip** (t, k, f, s, ch, p) add `te`, after any other letter add `de`. In the plural add an extra `n`.\n\n| | werken | spelen |\n| ik, jij / je, u, hij, zij | werkte | speelde |\n| wij, jullie, zij / ze | werkten | speelden |\n\nThere are only two forms: one for the singular and one for the plural. Jij gets no extra t: `Werkte jij?`, `jij werkte`.\n\n> Mijn vader werkte in een fabriek. — My father worked in a factory.\n> Wij speelden elke middag voetbal. — We played football every afternoon.\n> Woonde jij vroeger ook in de stad? — Did you also use to live in the city?\n\nThe spelling of the stem is the same as in the present: `maken → maakte`, `horen → hoorde`, `stoppen → stopte`.\n\n### v and z: look at the infinitive\n\nAs with the participle, the rule looks at the stem **before** v becomes f and z becomes s. V and z are not in 't kofschip, so you add `de`: `leven → leefde`, `reizen → reisde`, `verhuizen → verhuisde`.\n\n> In 2010 verhuisden we naar Utrecht. — In 2010 we moved to Utrecht.\n\n### Stems in t or d: a double letter\n\nHere, unlike the participle, nothing is dropped. A stem in t gets `te`, a stem in d gets `de`, and you write the letter twice: `praten → praatte`, `wachten → wachtte`, `antwoorden → antwoordde`, `landen → landde`.\n\n> De juf praatte veel over haar kat. — The teacher talked a lot about her cat.\n\nCareful: `wij praatten` (past) sounds exactly like `wij praten` (present). Only the spelling and the context tell them apart.\n\n### Word order and separable verbs\n\nThe past form is the conjugated verb, so it stands in second place, or first in a yes/no question. A separable verb splits, as in the present: `Ik belde hem op`, `Ik groeide op in Odesa`.\n\n> Vroeger haalde mijn oma mij van school op. — My grandmother used to pick me up from school.\n\n### was and had\n\nTwo irregular verbs you need from the start:\n\n| | zijn | hebben |\n| ik, jij / je, u, hij, zij | was | had |\n| wij, jullie, zij / ze | waren | hadden |\n\n> Mijn eerste juf was erg streng. — My first teacher was very strict.\n> We hadden vroeger een hond. — We used to have a dog.\n\nStrong verbs, such as `ging`, `kwam` and `zag`, have their own past forms (unit A2 · 16). Do not give them a regular ending: *ik loopte is wrong.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- 't kofschip: `werkte`, not *werkde; `speelde`, not *speelte.\n- v and z: `leefde`, not *leefte; `verhuisde`, not *verhuiste.\n- Stem in t or d: `praatte`, `wachtte`, `antwoordde`, not *praate or *antwoorde.\n- The plural needs its n: `wij speelden`, not *wij speelde.\n- A separable verb splits: `Ik belde haar op`, not *Ik opbelde haar.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nThe Ukrainian past changes for gender (`я грав`, `я грала`). The Dutch past changes only for number: `ik speelde`, `wij speelden`, for a man and a woman alike. A Ukrainian imperfective past for a habit, `щоліта я їздила до бабусі`, is very often a Dutch simple past with a time word like `elke zomer`.","words":[{"nl":"jeugd","art":"de","pl":"","en":"childhood / youth","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"juf","art":"de","pl":"juffen","en":"teacher (woman, primary school)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"meester","art":"de","pl":"meesters","en":"teacher (man, primary school)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"klasgenoot","art":"de","pl":"klasgenoten","en":"classmate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kleuterschool","art":"de","pl":"kleuterscholen","en":"nursery school / infant school","pos":"n"},{"nl":"speelgoed","art":"het","pl":"","en":"toys","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"schoolplein","art":"het","pl":"schoolpleinen","en":"school playground","pos":"n"},{"nl":"opgroeien","past":"groeide op","perf":"is opgegroeid","en":"to grow up","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verhuizen","past":"verhuisde","perf":"is verhuisd","en":"to move (house)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"knutselen","past":"knutselde","perf":"heeft geknutseld","en":"to do crafts / to make things","pos":"v"},{"nl":"plagen","past":"plaagde","perf":"heeft geplaagd","en":"to tease","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verstoppen","past":"verstopte","perf":"heeft verstopt","en":"to hide","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verzamelen","past":"verzamelde","perf":"heeft verzameld","en":"to collect","pos":"v"},{"nl":"straffen","past":"strafte","perf":"heeft gestraft","en":"to punish","pos":"v"},{"nl":"huilen","past":"huilde","perf":"heeft gehuild","en":"to cry","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vroeger","en":"in the past / used to","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Als kind speelde ik elke dag buiten.","en":"As a child I played outside every day.","hint":["als","kind","spelen","elke dag","buiten"],"note":"The stem speel ends in l, not a letter of 't kofschip, so + de: speelde. Als kind comes first, so the verb comes before ik.","alt":["Ik speelde als kind elke dag buiten."]},{"nl":"Vroeger woonden we in een klein dorp.","en":"We used to live in a small village.","hint":["vroeger","wonen","klein","dorp"],"note":"English \"used to\" is the Dutch simple past, often with vroeger. Woon + de, and + n for the plural: woonden.","alt":["We woonden vroeger in een klein dorp."]},{"nl":"Mijn oudere broer plaagde mij altijd.","en":"My older brother always teased me.","hint":["oud","broer","plagen","altijd"],"note":"plagen → stem plaag, ending in g: + de, plaagde. A habit in the past is a typical use of the simple past.","alt":["Mijn oudere broer plaagde me altijd."]},{"nl":"Op het schoolplein wachtten we op de juf.","en":"We waited for the teacher in the school playground.","hint":["schoolplein","wachten","juf"],"note":"The stem wacht ends in t, so + te gives a double t, and the plural adds n: wachtten. It sounds like the present wachten.","alt":["We wachtten op het schoolplein op de juf.","We wachtten op de juf op het schoolplein."]},{"nl":"Toen verhuisden we naar een andere stad.","en":"Then we moved to another town.","hint":["toen","verhuizen","ander","stad"],"note":"The stem of verhuizen ends in z in the infinitive, so + de: verhuisde, plural verhuisden. Ver- is part of the verb and stays.","alt":["We verhuisden toen naar een andere stad."]},{"nl":"Verzamelde jij vroeger ook stickers?","en":"Did you also use to collect stickers?","hint":["verzamelen","vroeger","ook","sticker"],"note":"The singular form verzamelde is the same for jij, with nothing added or dropped when jij follows it.","alt":["Verzamelde je vroeger ook stickers?"]},{"nl":"In mijn jeugd had ik weinig speelgoed.","en":"In my childhood I had few toys.","hint":["jeugd","hebben","weinig","speelgoed"],"note":"hebben is irregular in the past: ik had, wij hadden. In mijn jeugd stands first, so had comes before ik.","alt":["Ik had in mijn jeugd weinig speelgoed."]},{"nl":"Elke zomer logeerde ik bij mijn tante.","en":"Every summer I stayed with my aunt.","hint":["elke","zomer","logeren","tante"],"note":"logeren → stem logeer (the long e is written double), ending in r: + de. Elke zomer marks a repeated action.","alt":["Ik logeerde elke zomer bij mijn tante."]},{"nl":"Mijn klasgenoten waren altijd heel aardig.","en":"My classmates were always very nice.","hint":["klasgenoot","zijn","altijd","heel","aardig"],"note":"zijn is irregular: was in the singular, waren in the plural. Mijn klasgenoten is plural: waren."},{"nl":"Ik groeide op in een groot gezin.","en":"I grew up in a big family.","hint":["opgroeien","groot","gezin"],"note":"opgroeien is separable and splits in the simple past too: groeide … op. The phrase in een groot gezin may follow op or come before it.","alt":["Ik groeide in een groot gezin op."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De meester ___ ons nooit.","options":["strafde","strafte","straften"],"correct":1,"en":"The teacher never punished us.","note":"The stem straf ends in f, a letter of 't kofschip, so + te. De meester is singular: strafte, not straften."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Als kinderen ___ we veel buiten.","options":["speelde","speelten","speelden"],"correct":2,"en":"As children we played outside a lot.","note":"The stem speel ends in l, so + de, and we needs the plural n: speelden. Speelten mixes up 't kofschip."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn opa ___ heel gezond.","options":["leefte","leefde","leefden"],"correct":1,"en":"My grandfather lived very healthily.","note":"Look at the infinitive leven: the stem ends in v, not f. V is not in 't kofschip, so + de: leefde."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Als kind ___ ik elke ochtend op de bus.","options":["wachte","wachtte","wachtten"],"correct":1,"en":"As a child I waited for the bus every morning.","note":"The stem wacht already ends in t, and + te makes the t double: wachtte. Wachtten is the plural."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De juf ___ (praten) veel over haar hond.","answers":["praatte"],"en":"The teacher talked a lot about her dog.","note":"praten → stem praat, + te: praatte, with a double t. Praate does not exist."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik ___ (verstoppen) me altijd achter de deur.","answers":["verstopte"],"en":"I always hid behind the door.","note":"The stem verstop ends in p, a letter of 't kofschip: + te. Ver- is part of the verb, and the stem has one p."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn ouders ___ (verhuizen) vaak.","answers":["verhuisden"],"en":"My parents often moved house.","note":"The z of verhuizen counts, so + de, and mijn ouders is plural: verhuisden. Not verhuisten."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij ___ (hebben) vroeger een hond.","answers":["hadden"],"en":"We used to have a dog.","note":"hebben is irregular in the past: had in the singular, hadden in the plural."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn eerste juf ___ (zijn) erg lief.","answers":["was"],"en":"My first teacher was very kind.","note":"The past of zijn is was in the singular and waren in the plural. Mijn eerste juf is singular."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Onze meester ___ (antwoorden) altijd heel rustig.","answers":["antwoordde"],"en":"Our teacher always answered very calmly.","note":"The stem antwoord ends in d, so + de gives a double d: antwoordde. Every letter is written."},{"type":"order","lead":"Vroeger","words":["verzamelde","ik","altijd","oude munten"],"answers":["Vroeger verzamelde ik altijd oude munten."],"en":"I always used to collect old coins.","note":"Vroeger takes the first place, so the verb comes second and ik follows it."},{"type":"order","lead":"Mijn moeder","words":["haalde","mij","elke dag","van school","op"],"answers":["Mijn moeder haalde mij elke dag van school op.","Mijn moeder haalde mij elke dag op van school."],"en":"My mother picked me up from school every day.","note":"ophalen splits in the simple past: haalde stands second, op goes towards the end. Van school may come before or after op."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the simple past.","from":"Op woensdagmiddag knutselen we met de juf.","answers":["Op woensdagmiddag knutselden we met de juf."],"en":"On Wednesday afternoons we did crafts with the teacher.","note":"The stem knutsel ends in l, not a letter of 't kofschip, so + de, and we needs the plural n: knutselden."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the simple past.","from":"Hij belt zijn moeder elke avond op.","answers":["Hij belde zijn moeder elke avond op."],"en":"He called his mother every evening.","note":"opbellen splits in the past as in the present: belde … op. The stem bel ends in l, so + de."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Wij speelde vaak op het schoolplein.","answers":["Wij speelden vaak op het schoolplein."],"en":"We often played in the school playground.","note":"Wij is plural, so the past form needs an n: speelden."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Op de kleuterschool huilte ik elke ochtend.","answers":["Op de kleuterschool huilde ik elke ochtend."],"en":"At nursery school I cried every morning.","note":"The stem huil ends in l, which is not in 't kofschip, so + de: huilde."}]},
  {"id":"a2-16","level":"A2","icon":"🌧️","title":"A day when everything went wrong","grammar":"The simple past of strong verbs; perfect or simple past?","summary":"Strong verbs change their vowel and take no ending in the singular (ik viel, hij kwam); the plural adds -en (vielen, kwamen). Perfect: what happened. Simple past: how it went, step by step.","explain":"Dutch has two past tenses. The perfect (*de voltooide tijd*: `ik ben gevallen`) reports that something happened; the simple past (*de onvoltooid verleden tijd*: `ik viel`) tells the story. Regular verbs make the simple past with -te or -de (unit A2 · 15). But most of the verbs you use every day are **strong**: they change their vowel instead. This unit gives you their forms, and shows when Dutch uses which past.\n\n### Strong verbs: a new vowel, no ending\n\nA strong verb gets **no -te or -de**. In the singular (ik, jij, u, hij, zij) the form is the new stem, the same for every person and **without a t**. In the plural (wij, jullie, zij) you add -en.\n\n| | vallen | komen |\n| ik, jij, u, hij, zij | viel | kwam |\n| wij, jullie, zij | vielen | kwamen |\n\n> Ik viel van de trap. — I fell down the stairs.\n> Jij kwam ook te laat. — You were late too.\n\nThe new vowel cannot be worked out from the infinitive, so learn each verb as a set of three: `vallen – viel – gevallen`. The most common ones:\n\n| infinitive | singular | plural |\n| gaan | ging | gingen |\n| zien | zag | zagen |\n| doen | deed | deden |\n| nemen | nam | namen |\n| geven | gaf | gaven |\n| vinden | vond | vonden |\n| lopen | liep | liepen |\n| krijgen | kreeg | kregen |\n| blijven | bleef | bleven |\n| zitten | zat | zaten |\n| staan | stond | stonden |\n| beginnen | begon | begonnen |\n\nMore of the same kind: `spreken – sprak`, `schrijven – schreef`, `lezen – las`, `eten – at`, `drinken – dronk`, `vertrekken – vertrok`, `weten – wist`, `rijden – reed`, `roepen – riep`, `helpen – hielp`, `vergeten – vergat`, `breken – brak`, `verliezen – verloor`.\n\n### Spelling the plural\n\nThe plural follows the spelling rules of unit A2 · 1, and in some verbs the vowel also becomes **long**:\n\n- **Short in the singular, long in the plural**: `kwam – kwamen`, `nam – namen`, `zat – zaten`, `at – aten`, `sprak – spraken`, `vergat – vergaten`, `gaf – gaven`, `las – lazen`. No double consonant, and f and s become v and z.\n- **Short in both**: the consonant is doubled to keep the vowel short: `begon – begonnen`, `vertrok – vertrokken`.\n- **A double vowel** becomes single in the open syllable: `bleef – bleven`, `kreeg – kregen`, `reed – reden`.\n\n> We kwamen een uur te laat. — We were an hour late.\n> De problemen begonnen al vroeg. — The problems started early.\n\n### Mixed and irregular verbs\n\n`zeggen – zei, zeiden`. These change their vowel and end in -cht: `denken – dacht`, `brengen – bracht`, `kopen – kocht`, `zoeken – zocht`. The modal verbs: `kunnen – kon, konden`, `moeten – moest`, `mogen – mocht`, `willen – wilde` (in speech also `wou`), `zullen – zou, zouden`. And `zijn – was, waren`, `hebben – had, hadden`.\n\n> Ik zocht overal, maar mijn sleutels waren kwijt. — I looked everywhere, but my keys were gone.\n\n### Perfect or simple past?\n\nOften both are correct. What decides is what you are doing:\n\n- The **perfect** reports **that** something happened: a fact, a result. It is the normal past tense in conversation, and the way to start telling something.\n- The **simple past** tells **how it went**: a chain of events, the background (the weather, what was going on), and what you used to do. After `toen` (then) in a story it is the usual tense.\n\nSo a story typically opens in the perfect and goes on in the simple past:\n\n> Ik ben gisteren gevallen. — I had a fall yesterday.\n> Ik liep naar de bus en het regende. — I was walking to the bus and it was raining.\n> Toen gleed ik uit. — Then I slipped.\n\n`zijn`, `hebben` and the modal verbs are nearly always in the simple past, in conversation too: `Ik was moe`, `Ik had pech`, `Ik moest lopen`. `Ik ben moe geweest` is not wrong, but far less usual.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No -te or -de on a strong verb: `ik viel`, never *ik valde; `ik vergat`, never *ik vergatte.\n- No t for jij, u or hij: `hij kwam`, never *hij kwamt.\n- Spell the plural: `kwamen`, not *kwammen; `begonnen`, not *begonen.\n- The past of zeggen is usually `zei`, plural `zeiden`. (`zegde`, `zegden` is also correct, but in the Netherlands far less usual.)\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nThe choice is not about aspect. `Я впав` can be `ik ben gevallen` or `ik viel`: it depends on whether you report the fact or tell the story (more in unit B1 · 22).","words":[{"nl":"pech","art":"de","pl":"","en":"bad luck / breakdown","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"ongeluk","art":"het","pl":"ongelukken","en":"accident","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vertraging","art":"de","pl":"vertragingen","en":"delay","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vlek","art":"de","pl":"vlekken","en":"stain","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gat","art":"het","pl":"gaten","en":"hole","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pleister","art":"de","pl":"pleisters","en":"plaster (for a cut)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"buil","art":"de","pl":"builen","en":"bump (on the head)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vallen","past":"viel","perf":"is gevallen","en":"to fall","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verliezen","past":"verloor","perf":"heeft verloren","en":"to lose","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vergeten","past":"vergat","perf":"heeft/is vergeten","en":"to forget","pos":"v"},{"nl":"breken","past":"brak","perf":"heeft/is gebroken","en":"to break","pos":"v"},{"nl":"uitglijden","past":"gleed uit","perf":"is uitgegleden","en":"to slip","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"morsen","past":"morste","perf":"heeft gemorst","en":"to spill","pos":"v"},{"nl":"struikelen","past":"struikelde","perf":"is gestruikeld","en":"to trip / to stumble","pos":"v"},{"nl":"per ongeluk","en":"by accident","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"gelukkig","en":"luckily / fortunately","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik heb vanochtend mijn sleutels verloren.","en":"I lost my keys this morning. (perfect)","hint":["vanochtend","sleutel","verliezen"],"note":"The perfect reports what happened, so it is how you start telling someone. Verliezen takes hebben: heb … verloren.","alt":["Vanochtend heb ik mijn sleutels verloren.","Ik heb mijn sleutels vanochtend verloren."]},{"nl":"Ik zocht overal, maar mijn sleutels waren kwijt.","en":"I looked everywhere, but my keys were gone. (simple past)","hint":["zoeken","overal","sleutel","kwijt"],"note":"The story goes on in the simple past. Zoeken is mixed: zocht. Mijn sleutels is plural, so zijn becomes waren, not was."},{"nl":"Toen gleed ik uit op de natte stoep.","en":"Then I slipped on the wet pavement. (simple past)","hint":["toen","uitglijden","nat","stoep"],"note":"After toen in a story the simple past is usual. Uitglijden is strong (glijden → gleed) and separable: uit goes to the end, or before the last phrase.","alt":["Toen gleed ik op de natte stoep uit.","Ik gleed toen uit op de natte stoep.","Ik gleed toen op de natte stoep uit."]},{"nl":"Het regende en de trein had een uur vertraging.","en":"It was raining and the train had an hour's delay. (simple past)","hint":["regenen","trein","uur","vertraging"],"note":"Background: the simple past. Hebben is almost always in the simple past: had, plural hadden."},{"nl":"We kwamen allebei te laat op ons werk.","en":"We were both late for work. (simple past)","hint":["komen","allebei","te laat","werk"],"note":"Kwam has a short a; in the plural the a becomes long, so it is kwamen with one m.","alt":["We kwamen allebei te laat op het werk.","We kwamen allebei te laat op kantoor.","We waren allebei te laat op ons werk.","We waren allebei te laat op het werk."]},{"nl":"Wat zei je baas toen?","en":"What did your boss say then? (simple past)","hint":["wat","zeggen","baas","toen"],"note":"The past of zeggen is zei, plural zeiden. It never gets a t: hij zei, jij zei.","alt":["Wat zei jouw baas toen?"]},{"nl":"Mijn collega struikelde, viel van de trap en brak haar pols.","en":"My colleague tripped, fell down the stairs and broke her wrist. (simple past)","hint":["collega","struikelen","vallen","trap","breken","pols"],"note":"A chain of events, so the simple past. Struikelen is regular (struikelde); vallen and breken are strong: viel, brak, with no ending."},{"nl":"Ik moest een pleister op mijn knie plakken.","en":"I had to put a plaster on my knee. (simple past)","hint":["moeten","pleister","knie","plakken"],"note":"Modal verbs are nearly always in the simple past: moest, plural moesten. The infinitive plakken stays at the end.","alt":["Ik moest een pleister op mijn knie doen."]},{"nl":"Thuis zag ik een grote vlek op mijn broek.","en":"At home I saw a big stain on my trousers. (simple past)","hint":["thuis","zien","groot","vlek","broek"],"note":"Zien → zag, plural zagen with a long a. Thuis stands first, so the verb comes before ik.","alt":["Ik zag thuis een grote vlek op mijn broek."]},{"nl":"Vroeger vergat ik altijd mijn paraplu.","en":"I always used to forget my umbrella.","hint":["vroeger","vergeten","altijd","paraplu"],"note":"A habit in the past: the simple past. Vergeten → vergat, with one t and no -te; the plural is vergaten.","alt":["Ik vergat vroeger altijd mijn paraplu."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn buurman ___ gisteren van zijn fiets.","options":["valde","viel","vielt"],"correct":1,"en":"My neighbour fell off his bike yesterday.","note":"Vallen is strong: viel. A strong verb never gets -de, and in the singular it has no t, so not valde or vielt."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wij ___ door de vertraging een uur te laat.","options":["kwam","kwammen","kwamen","komden"],"correct":2,"en":"Because of the delay we were an hour late.","note":"Wij needs the plural: kwam + en. The a becomes long in the open syllable, so it is written with one m: kwamen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Gisteren ___ jij je jas in de trein.","options":["vergaten","vergatte","vergat"],"correct":2,"en":"Yesterday you forgot your coat on the train.","note":"Vergeten is strong: vergat, the same for ik, jij and hij, with no ending. Vergatte mixes in the regular -te; vergaten is the plural."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na het ongeluk ___ ik naar huis lopen.","options":["moette","moest","moesten"],"correct":1,"en":"After the accident I had to walk home.","note":"The past of moeten is moest for ik, jij and hij, and moesten in the plural. Moette does not exist."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ gisteren echt pech.","options":["had","hadden","hebte"],"correct":0,"en":"I was really unlucky yesterday.","note":"Hebben is irregular: had, plural hadden. With hebben the simple past is the normal choice, also in conversation."},{"type":"gap","nl":"In het donker ___ (zien) ik het gat in de weg niet.","answers":["zag"],"en":"In the dark I did not see the hole in the road.","note":"Zien → zag, without an ending. In het donker stands first, so the verb comes before ik."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Gelukkig ___ (doen) de buil op mijn hoofd niet veel pijn.","answers":["deed"],"en":"Luckily the bump on my head did not hurt much.","note":"Doen → deed, plural deden. De buil is singular, so deed, with no ending and no t."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn ouders ___ (vertrekken) veel te laat van huis.","answers":["vertrokken"],"en":"My parents left home far too late.","note":"Vertrekken → vertrok. In the plural the o stays short, so the k is doubled: vertrokken."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Gelukkig ___ (helpen) twee mannen me met mijn fiets.","answers":["hielpen"],"en":"Luckily two men helped me with my bike.","note":"Helpen → hielp, plural hielpen. The subject is twee mannen, so the plural, even though it comes after the verb."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["verloor","ik","mijn portemonnee","op het station"],"answers":["Gisteren verloor ik mijn portemonnee op het station.","Gisteren verloor ik op het station mijn portemonnee."],"en":"Yesterday I lost my wallet at the station.","note":"Verliezen → verloor. Gisteren takes the first place, so the verb comes second and ik follows it."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["gevallen","van de trap","ben","gisteren"],"answers":["Ik ben gisteren van de trap gevallen.","Ik ben gisteren gevallen van de trap."],"en":"I fell down the stairs yesterday.","note":"The perfect reports what happened. Vallen takes zijn: ben … gevallen, with the participle at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the simple past.","from":"Hij morst per ongeluk thee en krijgt een vlek op zijn broek.","answers":["Hij morste per ongeluk thee en kreeg een vlek op zijn broek."],"en":"He spilt tea by accident and got a stain on his trousers.","note":"Morsen is regular: morste. Krijgen is strong: kreeg, with no ending."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect, the way you report it in conversation.","from":"Ik brak gisteren mijn bril.","answers":["Ik heb gisteren mijn bril gebroken.","Ik heb mijn bril gisteren gebroken."],"en":"I broke my glasses yesterday.","note":"Breken with an object (you break something) takes hebben: heb … gebroken. Something that breaks by itself takes zijn: de bril is gebroken."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it the usual way, with zijn in the simple past.","from":"Ik ben gisteren erg moe geweest.","answers":["Ik was gisteren erg moe.","Gisteren was ik erg moe."],"en":"I was very tired yesterday.","note":"With zijn, hebben and the modal verbs Dutch nearly always uses the simple past: was, waren."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Gisteren zeiden ik sorry tegen de buurvrouw.","answers":["Gisteren zei ik sorry tegen de buurvrouw.","Gisteren zegde ik sorry tegen de buurvrouw."],"en":"Yesterday I said sorry to the neighbour.","note":"Ik takes the singular: zei. Zeiden is the plural (wij, jullie, zij). Zegde also exists, but zei is far more usual in the Netherlands."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De problemen begonen al om zeven uur.","answers":["De problemen begonnen al om zeven uur."],"en":"The problems already started at seven o'clock.","note":"Begon keeps its short o in the plural, so the n is doubled: begonnen. With one n you would read a long o."}]},
  {"id":"a2-17","level":"A2","icon":"🌦️","title":"Plans and the weather","grammar":"Talking about the future","summary":"The future is usually the present tense with a time word (Morgen werk ik). gaan + infinitive: a plan, or something about to happen. zullen + infinitive: a promise, a guess (zal wel), a forecast. The infinitive goes last.","explain":"Dutch grammar books call `zullen` + infinitive the future tense (*de toekomende tijd*), but in real life that is not the usual way to talk about later. There are three ways, and the most common one is the simplest: the present tense.\n\n### 1. The present tense with a time word\n\nWhen a time word or the situation makes clear that it is about later, the present tense is enough. This is what people say most of the time.\n\n> Morgen regent het de hele dag. — Tomorrow it will rain all day.\n> Vanavond blijf ik thuis. — I am staying at home this evening.\n> Over twee dagen begint de vakantie. — The holiday starts in two days.\n\nTime words you will need: `vanavond` (this evening), `straks` (later today), `zo meteen` or just `zo` (in a moment), `morgen`, `overmorgen` (the day after tomorrow), `binnenkort` (soon), `volgende week`, `over twee dagen` (in two days: `over`, not `in`), `later`.\n\n### 2. gaan + infinitive\n\n`gaan` (ik ga, jij gaat, hij gaat, wij gaan) with an infinitive is the Dutch \"going to\". Use it for a **plan**, and for something that is **about to happen**: you can see it coming.\n\n> Ik ga volgend jaar Spaans leren. — I am going to learn Spanish next year.\n> Gaan jullie in de zomer weer kamperen? — Are you going camping again in the summer?\n> Kijk naar die wolken: het gaat regenen. — Look at those clouds: it is going to rain.\n\nWhere it does not fit:\n\n- Not with `gaan` itself: `Ik ga morgen naar de markt`, never *Ik ga morgen naar de markt gaan.\n- With `komen` the present tense is normal: `Ik kom morgen`, rather than `Ik ga morgen komen`.\n- With `zijn`, `hebben` and the modal verbs it is unusual. Use the present tense: `Morgen ben ik thuis`, `Ik heb morgen tijd`, `Ik moet morgen werken`.\n\n### 3. zullen + infinitive\n\n| | zullen |\n| ik | zal |\n| jij / je, u | zult / zal |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | zal |\n| wij / we, jullie, zij / ze | zullen |\n\n`zullen` is not a neutral future. It always adds something:\n\n- **a promise**: you give your word.\n- **a proposal**, in a question with ik or we (unit A2 · 11).\n- **a guess**, usually with `wel`: \"probably\".\n- **a forecast** in formal or written Dutch, such as the weather report.\n\n> Ik zal je morgen bellen. — I will call you tomorrow.\n> Zullen we zondag gaan fietsen? — Shall we go cycling on Sunday?\n> Het zal wel goed komen. — It will probably be all right.\n> Morgen zal het in het hele land regenen. — Tomorrow it will rain in the whole country.\n\nSo `Ik zal morgen werken` is correct, but it sounds like a promise. For a plain fact about tomorrow say `Ik werk morgen`.\n\n### Word order\n\n`ga` or `zal` is the conjugated verb, so it stands where the verb always stands: second in a statement, first in a yes/no question. The infinitive goes to the **end**, as with the modal verbs. When a time word comes first, the subject follows the verb. A separable verb stays in one piece at the end.\n\n> Morgen ga ik met mijn zus wandelen. — Tomorrow I am going for a walk with my sister.\n> Ik zal een paraplu voor je meenemen. — I will bring an umbrella for you.\n\nYou will also hear a phrase with a preposition after the infinitive (`Ik ga wandelen in het bos`). For now, put the infinitive last.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **No te.** English says \"going *to* rain\", Dutch does not: `Het gaat regenen`, never *Het gaat te regenen.\n- **willen is not \"will\".** `Ik wil komen` means \"I want to come\". \"I will come\" is `Ik kom` or `Ik zal komen`.\n- **Not gaan twice**: `Ik ga naar huis`, never *Ik ga naar huis gaan.\n- **The infinitive goes last**: `Ik ga morgen een paraplu kopen`, not *Ik ga kopen morgen een paraplu.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian builds a future with `буду` + infinitive: `Я буду працювати`. Do not turn every `буду` into `zal`. The normal Dutch is the present tense, `Ik werk morgen`, just as Ukrainian can say `Завтра я працюю`. In Dutch that is the rule, not the exception.","words":[{"nl":"weerbericht","art":"het","pl":"weerberichten","en":"weather forecast","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bui","art":"de","pl":"buien","en":"shower (of rain)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wolk","art":"de","pl":"wolken","en":"cloud","pos":"n"},{"nl":"temperatuur","art":"de","pl":"temperaturen","en":"temperature","pos":"n"},{"nl":"graad","art":"de","pl":"graden","en":"degree","pos":"n"},{"nl":"seizoen","art":"het","pl":"seizoenen","en":"season","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lente","art":"de","pl":"lentes","en":"spring (season)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"herfst","art":"de","pl":"herfsten","en":"autumn","pos":"n"},{"nl":"regenen","past":"regende","perf":"heeft geregend","en":"to rain","pos":"v"},{"nl":"waaien","past":"waaide","perf":"heeft gewaaid","en":"to blow (wind) / to be windy","pos":"v"},{"nl":"schijnen","past":"scheen","perf":"heeft geschenen","en":"to shine","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vriezen","past":"vroor","perf":"heeft gevroren","en":"to freeze","pos":"v"},{"nl":"bewolkt","en":"cloudy","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"droog","en":"dry","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"binnenkort","en":"soon","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"overmorgen","en":"the day after tomorrow","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Overmorgen gaan we naar het strand.","en":"The day after tomorrow we are going to the beach.","hint":["overmorgen","gaan","strand"],"note":"The present tense with a time word is the normal future. Gaan is the main verb here (to go). Overmorgen stands first, so the verb comes before we.","alt":["Overmorgen gaan wij naar het strand.","We gaan overmorgen naar het strand."]},{"nl":"Mijn ouders komen in de herfst naar Nederland.","en":"My parents are coming to the Netherlands in the autumn.","hint":["ouders","komen","herfst","Nederland"],"note":"With komen Dutch uses the present tense for the future, rather than gaan + komen. In de herfst shows that it is about later.","alt":["In de herfst komen mijn ouders naar Nederland."]},{"nl":"Straks schijnt de zon weer.","en":"Later today the sun will shine again. (present tense)","hint":["straks","schijnen","zon","weer"],"note":"Straks already says that it is about later, so the present tense is enough: schijnt. Straks stands first, so the subject de zon follows the verb.","alt":["De zon schijnt straks weer."]},{"nl":"Mijn zus gaat in de lente trouwen.","en":"My sister is going to get married in the spring.","hint":["zus","gaan","lente","trouwen"],"note":"gaan + infinitive for a plan. Gaat stands in second place and the infinitive trouwen goes to the end of the sentence.","alt":["In de lente gaat mijn zus trouwen.","Mijn zus gaat trouwen in de lente."]},{"nl":"Wat ga je dit weekend doen?","en":"What are you going to do this weekend?","hint":["wat","gaan","weekend","doen"],"note":"gaan + infinitive asks about a plan. It is jij gaat, but je directly after the verb loses the t: ga je. Doen stands at the end.","alt":["Wat ga jij dit weekend doen?"]},{"nl":"Het gaat zo meteen regenen.","en":"It is going to rain in a moment.","hint":["gaan","zo meteen","regenen"],"note":"gaan + infinitive for something that is about to happen: you can see the clouds. There is no te before regenen.","alt":["Het gaat zo regenen.","Zo meteen gaat het regenen."]},{"nl":"Volgens het weerbericht gaat het morgen niet vriezen.","en":"According to the weather forecast it is not going to freeze tomorrow.","hint":["volgens","weerbericht","gaan","morgen","vriezen"],"note":"Volgens het weerbericht is the first element, so gaat comes next and then the subject het. Niet stands before the infinitive at the end."},{"nl":"Ik zal je morgen helpen.","en":"I will help you tomorrow. (a promise)","hint":["zullen","morgen","helpen"],"note":"zullen for a promise: ik zal, without t. Not ik wil, which would mean 'I want to help you'. The infinitive helpen goes to the end.","alt":["Ik zal jou morgen helpen.","Morgen zal ik je helpen.","Morgen zal ik jou helpen."]},{"nl":"Zullen we morgen naar het bos gaan?","en":"Shall we go to the forest tomorrow?","hint":["zullen","morgen","bos","gaan"],"note":"Zullen we …? is a proposal. Gaan is the infinitive here and stands at the end. With zullen that is fine; only gaan + gaan is impossible.","alt":["Zullen wij morgen naar het bos gaan?"]},{"nl":"Het zal morgen wel bewolkt zijn.","en":"It will probably be cloudy tomorrow. (with zal … wel)","hint":["zullen","morgen","wel","bewolkt","zijn"],"note":"zal + wel is a guess: probably. With zijn Dutch uses zullen or the present tense (Het is morgen bewolkt) rather than gaan.","alt":["Morgen zal het wel bewolkt zijn."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Zie je die donkere wolken? We ___ nat worden.","options":["ga","gaat","gaan"],"correct":2,"en":"Do you see those dark clouds? We are going to get wet.","note":"We is plural, so gaan. This is gaan + infinitive for something you can see coming; the infinitive worden stands at the end."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We gaan morgen ___ zwemmen.","options":["te","–","om"],"correct":1,"en":"We are going swimming tomorrow.","note":"After gaan the infinitive comes without te. English 'going to' has a 'to', but Dutch gaan takes the bare infinitive: gaan zwemmen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De temperatuur ___ morgen stijgen tot twintig graden.","options":["zult","wil","zal","zullen"],"correct":2,"en":"Tomorrow the temperature will rise to twenty degrees.","note":"A forecast in a weather report uses zullen, and de temperatuur is a third-person singular subject: zal. Wil means 'wants to', and zult goes with jij and u."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ morgen graag komen, maar ik kan niet.","options":["zal","ga","wil"],"correct":2,"en":"I would like to come tomorrow, but I cannot.","note":"willen says what you want, not what will happen. Zal would be a promise to come, and that does not go together with 'maar ik kan niet'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wat ___ (gaan) je in de vakantie doen?","answers":["ga"],"en":"What are you going to do in the holidays?","note":"It is jij gaat, but when je follows the verb the t disappears: ga je. The plan itself, doen, stands at the end as an infinitive."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij ___ (gaan) binnenkort op vakantie.","answers":["gaan"],"en":"We are going on holiday soon.","note":"Here gaan is the main verb (to go) in the present tense, and binnenkort makes it future. A second gaan at the end is impossible: never 'wij gaan … gaan'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De bui ___ (zullen) zo wel voorbij zijn.","answers":["zal"],"en":"The shower will probably be over in a moment.","note":"Hij, zij and het take zal, without t. Zal together with wel is a guess: probably."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Over twee weken ___ (beginnen) het nieuwe seizoen.","answers":["begint"],"en":"The new season starts in two weeks.","note":"The present tense is enough, because over twee weken already places it in the future. Het nieuwe seizoen is the subject: stem + t, begint."},{"type":"order","lead":"Straks","words":["ga","ik","een paraplu","kopen"],"answers":["Straks ga ik een paraplu kopen."],"en":"Later today I am going to buy an umbrella.","note":"Straks takes the first place, so ga comes second and ik follows it. The object stands before the infinitive, and the infinitive closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zal","words":["ik","je","morgen","ophalen"],"answers":["Zal ik je morgen ophalen?"],"en":"Shall I pick you up tomorrow?","note":"An offer with Zal ik …? starts with the verb. The short pronoun je comes right after the subject, and the separable verb ophalen stays in one piece at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["gaat","het","niet","sneeuwen"],"answers":["Morgen gaat het niet sneeuwen."],"en":"Tomorrow it is not going to snow.","note":"After Morgen comes the verb gaat, then the subject het. Niet stands before the infinitive at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with gaan + infinitive.","from":"Het waait morgen hard.","answers":["Het gaat morgen hard waaien.","Morgen gaat het hard waaien."],"en":"It is going to be very windy tomorrow.","note":"Gaat takes the place of the conjugated verb, and waaien moves to the end as an infinitive, without te."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Write it the way a formal weather report does: with zullen.","from":"Morgen blijft het droog.","answers":["Morgen zal het droog blijven.","Het zal morgen droog blijven."],"en":"Tomorrow it will stay dry.","note":"Zal takes the second place and blijven goes to the end as an infinitive. In conversation the present tense, Morgen blijft het droog, is more usual."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it the everyday way: in the present tense, without zullen.","from":"Wij zullen volgende week niet thuis zijn.","answers":["Wij zijn volgende week niet thuis.","We zijn volgende week niet thuis."],"en":"We are not at home next week.","note":"Volgende week already shows that it is about the future, so the present tense of zijn is enough. With zijn this is the normal choice."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik ga morgen naar de markt gaan.","answers":["Ik ga morgen naar de markt.","Ik zal morgen naar de markt gaan."],"en":"I am going to the market tomorrow.","note":"gaan + infinitive is not used with gaan itself. The present tense of gaan together with morgen is enough. (With zal it would be a promise.)"},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Morgen wil het de hele dag regenen.","answers":["Morgen zal het de hele dag regenen.","Morgen gaat het de hele dag regenen.","Morgen regent het de hele dag."],"en":"Tomorrow it will rain all day.","note":"willen means 'to want', and the weather does not want anything. English 'will' is zullen, gaan or simply the present tense."}]},
  {"id":"a2-18","level":"A2","icon":"🛍️","title":"In the shop","grammar":"Deze, die, dit, dat","summary":"Near: deze (de-words and all plurals), dit (het-words in the singular). Far: die and dat in the same way. Dit is / dat zijn + noun never change. Die and dat also stand in for hij, zij, het, usually in first place.","explain":"`Deze`, `die`, `dit` and `dat` are the pointing words (*aanwijzende voornaamwoorden*): \"this\", \"that\", \"these\", \"those\". Which one you need depends on two things: is the thing near or far, and is the noun a de-word or a het-word? This is one of the reasons to learn every noun with its article.\n\n### The four forms\n\n| | near: this, these | far: that, those |\n| de-word (de jas) | deze jas | die jas |\n| het-word (het horloge) | dit horloge | dat horloge |\n| every plural | deze jassen, deze horloges | die jassen, die horloges |\n\nEvery plural is a de-word, so the plural always takes `deze` or `die`: `dit horloge`, but `deze horloges`. A way to remember it: the forms that end in **t** go with he**t**.\n\n> Deze trui is te groot. — This jumper is too big.\n> Dat overhemd staat je goed. — That shirt suits you.\n> Die laarzen zijn te duur. — Those boots are too expensive.\n\n\"Far\" does not have to be far away. `Die` and `dat` are also used for something that was just mentioned, and in speech they are the most common choice. `Deze` and `dit` really point: this one here.\n\nAn adjective after these words always takes `-e` (unit A2 · 10): `dit mooie horloge`, `deze nieuwe schoenen`.\n\n### On their own\n\nAll four can be used without a noun when it is clear what you mean. The form still follows the noun you have in mind. English adds \"one\", Dutch adds nothing.\n\n> Ik neem deze. — I will take this one.\n> Dit overhemd is te groot, maar dat past goed. — This shirt is too big, but that one fits well.\n\n### Dit is …, dat zijn …\n\nTo introduce or identify a person or a thing, Dutch says `dit` or `dat` + `zijn` + noun. Here **dit and dat never change**: not for a de-word, not for a plural. Only the verb follows the noun: `is` for one, `zijn` for more.\n\n> Dit is mijn broer. — This is my brother.\n> Dat is de paskamer. — That is the fitting room.\n> Dit zijn de nieuwe modellen. — These are the new models.\n> Dat zijn mijn ouders. — Those are my parents.\n\nCompare `Die schoenen zijn duur` (die + noun) with `Dat zijn dure schoenen` (dat + zijn + noun).\n\n### Die and dat instead of hij, zij, het\n\nIn everyday speech Dutch very often says `die` or `dat` where English has \"he\", \"she\", \"it\", \"they\", \"him\" or \"them\", for people and things that were just mentioned. They usually stand first; when `die` or `dat` is not the subject, the subject comes after the verb.\n\n> Ken je die verkoper? Nee, die ken ik niet. — Do you know that shop assistant? No, I do not know him.\n> Waar zijn de sleutels? Die liggen op tafel. — Where are the keys? They are on the table.\n> Hoe laat gaat de winkel dicht? Dat weet ik niet. — What time does the shop close? I do not know.\n\n`Die` for de-words and plurals, `dat` for het-words. `Dat` also refers to a whole idea or sentence: `Dat weet ik niet`, `Dat is waar`, `Dat vind ik mooi`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- A het-word takes `dit` and `dat` only in the singular: `dit product`, but `deze producten`, never *dit producten.\n- To say who or what it is, always `dit` or `dat`: `Dit is mijn collega` (not *Deze is mijn collega), `Dat zijn mijn kinderen` (not *Die zijn mijn kinderen, and not *Dat is mijn kinderen).\n- Without a noun after it, the pointing word follows the noun you mean: `Deze jas is duur en die is goedkoop`.\n- \"This one, that one\": `Ik neem deze`, never *deze een.\n\n`Die` and `dat` have two more jobs, which come later: `de man die daar staat` (unit B1 · 3) and `Ik weet dat hij komt` (unit A2 · 23).\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian chooses between `цей, ця, це, ці` and `той, та, те, ті` by gender, number and case. Dutch only looks at de or het and at near or far, and nothing changes after a preposition: `в цьому магазині` is simply `in deze winkel`. And `Це мій брат`, `Це мої батьки` work like `Dit is mijn broer`, `Dit zijn mijn ouders`: the pointing word stays the same. But Dutch must have the verb, and the verb shows the plural.","words":[{"nl":"klant","art":"de","pl":"klanten","en":"customer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verkoper","art":"de","pl":"verkopers","en":"shop assistant","pos":"n"},{"nl":"prijs","art":"de","pl":"prijzen","en":"price","pos":"n"},{"nl":"korting","art":"de","pl":"kortingen","en":"discount","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanbieding","art":"de","pl":"aanbiedingen","en":"special offer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bon","art":"de","pl":"bonnen","en":"receipt","pos":"n"},{"nl":"maat","art":"de","pl":"maten","en":"size","pos":"n"},{"nl":"paskamer","art":"de","pl":"paskamers","en":"fitting room","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rek","art":"het","pl":"rekken","en":"rack","pos":"n"},{"nl":"product","art":"het","pl":"producten","en":"product","pos":"n"},{"nl":"horloge","art":"het","pl":"horloges","en":"watch","pos":"n"},{"nl":"merk","art":"het","pl":"merken","en":"brand","pos":"n"},{"nl":"passen","past":"paste","perf":"heeft gepast","en":"to try on / to fit","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ruilen","past":"ruilde","perf":"heeft geruild","en":"to exchange","pos":"v"},{"nl":"terugbrengen","past":"bracht terug","perf":"heeft teruggebracht","en":"to take back / to return","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"pinnen","past":"pinde","perf":"heeft gepind","en":"to pay by card","pos":"v"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Deze jas is te klein voor mij.","en":"This coat is too small for me.","hint":["jas","klein","voor"],"note":"De jas is a de-word and the coat is right here, so deze. With a het-word it would be dit: dit overhemd.","alt":["Deze jas is te klein voor me."]},{"nl":"Hoeveel kost dit horloge met korting?","en":"How much does this watch cost with the discount?","hint":["hoeveel","kosten","horloge","korting"],"note":"Het horloge is a het-word in the singular and you have it in your hand: dit.","alt":["Wat kost dit horloge met korting?"]},{"nl":"Deze horloges zijn in de aanbieding.","en":"These watches are on offer.","hint":["horloge","zijn","aanbieding"],"note":"The plural always takes deze (or die), also for a het-word: dit horloge, but deze horloges."},{"nl":"Ik wil die zwarte schoenen graag passen.","en":"I would like to try on those black shoes.","hint":["willen","zwart","schoen","graag","passen"],"note":"Schoenen is plural and the shoes are further away: die. The adjective after die takes -e: zwarte.","alt":["Ik wil graag die zwarte schoenen passen."]},{"nl":"Wij verkopen dat product niet meer.","en":"We no longer sell that product.","hint":["verkopen","product","niet meer"],"note":"Het product is a het-word in the singular, and it is not here in front of us: dat. In the plural it would be die producten.","alt":["We verkopen dat product niet meer.","Dat product verkopen wij niet meer.","Dat product verkopen we niet meer."]},{"nl":"Welke maat wilt u, deze of die?","en":"Which size would you (formal) like, this one or that one?","hint":["welke","maat","willen","deze","die"],"note":"Deze and die stand on their own here: the noun maat is not repeated. De maat is a de-word, so deze and die, not dit and dat. Dutch has no word for 'one'.","alt":["Welke maat wil u, deze of die?"]},{"nl":"Dit is de nieuwe verkoper.","en":"This is the new shop assistant.","hint":["dit","zijn","nieuw","verkoper"],"note":"To introduce someone Dutch uses dit is, also for a de-word like de verkoper. Deze is … would be wrong here."},{"nl":"Dat zijn de prijzen van vorig jaar.","en":"Those are last year's prices.","hint":["dat","zijn","prijs","vorig","jaar"],"note":"Dat stays dat, also before a plural. Only the verb changes: zijn, because de prijzen is plural."},{"nl":"Waar is de bon? Die heb ik niet meer.","en":"Where is the receipt? I do not have it any more. (it: die, in first place)","hint":["waar","bon","die","hebben","niet meer"],"note":"Die stands for de bon, a de-word, and comes first, so the verb heb follows and then the subject ik. With hem it would be: Ik heb hem niet meer."},{"nl":"Dat vind ik een goede prijs.","en":"I think that is a good price.","hint":["dat","vinden","goed","prijs"],"note":"Dat refers to what was just said or shown, not to a noun. It stands first, so the verb vind comes before ik.","alt":["Ik vind dat een goede prijs."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ korting geldt alleen vandaag.","options":["Dit","Deze","Dat"],"correct":1,"en":"This discount is only valid today.","note":"Korting is a de-word, like all words in -ing, so deze. Dit and dat only go with het-words in the singular."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wat vind je van ___ merk?","options":["deze","die","dit"],"correct":2,"en":"What do you think of this brand?","note":"Het merk is a het-word in the singular: dit. Deze and die go with de-words and with plurals."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ zijn mijn nieuwe schoenen.","options":["Deze","Dit","Die"],"correct":1,"en":"These are my new shoes.","note":"To say what something is, Dutch uses dit or dat before zijn, also when the noun is plural. Only the verb shows the plural: dit zijn."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Deze jas kost honderd euro en ___ kost vijftig euro.","options":["die","dit","dat"],"correct":0,"en":"This coat costs a hundred euros and that one costs fifty euros.","note":"Die stands on its own here and still means a jas, a de-word: die. Nothing is added for English 'one'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Waar is de klant? ___ staat bij de kassa.","options":["Dat","Dit","Die"],"correct":2,"en":"Where is the customer? She is standing at the checkout.","note":"In speech die often replaces hij or zij. De klant is a de-word, so die. Dat would be for a het-word."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik wil ___ (this) overhemd graag ruilen.","answers":["dit"],"en":"I would like to exchange this shirt.","note":"Het overhemd is a het-word in the singular, and you are holding it: dit."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Is ___ (that) paskamer vrij?","answers":["die"],"en":"Is that fitting room free?","note":"De paskamer is a de-word, and it is over there: die."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit ___ (zijn) onze nieuwe producten.","answers":["zijn"],"en":"These are our new products.","note":"Dit does not change, but the verb follows the noun after it. Producten is plural, so zijn."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Kan ik hier pinnen? Ja, ___ (that) kan.","answers":["dat"],"en":"Can I pay by card here? Yes, you can.","note":"Dat refers to the whole idea of paying by card, not to a noun. For an idea or a whole sentence it is always dat."},{"type":"order","lead":"Die schoenen","words":["breng","ik","morgen","terug"],"answers":["Die schoenen breng ik morgen terug."],"en":"I am taking those shoes back tomorrow.","note":"Die schoenen is the object and stands first, so the verb comes second and ik after it. The prefix of terugbrengen goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the noun plural.","from":"Dit horloge is duur.","answers":["Deze horloges zijn duur."],"en":"These watches are expensive.","note":"Every plural takes deze, also the plural of a het-word. The verb becomes plural too: zijn."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the noun singular.","from":"Deze rekken zijn nieuw.","answers":["Dit rek is nieuw."],"en":"This rack is new.","note":"Rek is a het-word, so in the singular deze becomes dit. The verb becomes singular too: is."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Point at things further away: change this to that, twice.","from":"Deze klant wil dit horloge ruilen.","answers":["Die klant wil dat horloge ruilen."],"en":"That customer wants to exchange that watch.","note":"Deze becomes die (de klant) and dit becomes dat (het horloge). The pairs are deze / die and dit / dat."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the same with Dat zijn …","from":"Die schoenen zijn duur.","answers":["Dat zijn dure schoenen."],"en":"Those are expensive shoes.","note":"Before zijn + noun it is dat, also for a plural. The adjective now stands before the noun and takes -e: dure."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Dit schoenen zijn te klein.","answers":["Deze schoenen zijn te klein.","Die schoenen zijn te klein."],"en":"These shoes are too small.","note":"Schoenen is plural, and a plural never takes dit or dat. Near is deze. Die would also be a correct sentence, for shoes that are further away."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Deze is mijn nieuwe collega.","answers":["Dit is mijn nieuwe collega.","Dat is mijn nieuwe collega."],"en":"This is my new colleague.","note":"To introduce a person Dutch uses dit is (or dat is), whether the noun is a de-word or a het-word. Deze is … is only right without a noun after it: Deze is van mij."}]},
  {"id":"a2-19","level":"A2","icon":"🏙️","title":"City or village?","grammar":"Comparing: groter, het grootst, even groot als","summary":"Comparative: adjective + er (groter, duurder), followed by dan. Superlative: + st (het grootst, de grootste stad). Irregular: goed, beter, best; veel, meer, meest. Equal: even groot als. No meer + adjective.","explain":"To compare two things Dutch uses the comparative (*de vergrotende trap*): `groter`. To say that something beats all the others it uses the superlative (*de overtreffende trap*): `het grootst`. Both are made with an ending on the adjective itself. Dutch normally does not use an extra word like English \"more\" and \"most\", not even for long adjectives.\n\n### The comparative: + er\n\n| | comparative | what happens |\n| klein | kleiner | + er |\n| groot | groter | long vowel: one letter |\n| dik | dikker | short vowel: double consonant |\n| lief | liever | f becomes v |\n| vies | viezer | s becomes z |\n| duur | duurder | after r: + der |\n\nThe spelling changes are the same as for the `-e` of the adjective (unit A2 · 10): `grote → groter`, `dikke → dikker`, `lieve → liever`, `vieze → viezer`. New is the **d after r**: `duur → duurder`, `ver → verder`, `lekker → lekkerder`, `zwaar → zwaarder`.\n\nAfter a comparative comes `dan`. You will hear `als` too, but `dan` is the standard, and it is what you should write.\n\n> Utrecht is kleiner dan Rotterdam. — Utrecht is smaller than Rotterdam.\n> Een huis is duurder dan een flat. — A house is more expensive than a flat.\n\n### The superlative: + st\n\n`groot → grootst`, `mooi → mooist`, `duur → duurst` (no d here). After an s only a t is added: `vies → viest`.\n\n- Before a noun: `de` or `het` + superlative + `e`: `de grootste stad`, `het mooiste dorp`.\n- On its own: `het` + superlative, with or without `-e`: `Dit huis is het duurst` or `het duurste`.\n\n> Amsterdam is de grootste stad van Nederland. — Amsterdam is the biggest city in the Netherlands.\n> In de zomer is het hier het drukst. — It is busiest here in the summer.\n\n### Irregular forms\n\n| | comparative | superlative |\n| goed | beter | het best |\n| veel | meer | het meest |\n| weinig | minder | het minst |\n| graag | liever | het liefst |\n\nAlso: `dichtbij → dichterbij` (closer).\n\n> Ik woon graag in de stad, maar mijn man woont liever in een dorp. — I like living in the city, but my husband prefers to live in a village.\n> Een dorp heeft minder inwoners dan een stad. — A village has fewer inhabitants than a city.\n\n### Just as, not as\n\n| Dutch | English |\n| even groot als | just as big as |\n| net zo groot als | just as big as |\n| niet zo groot als | not as big as |\n\nHere the adjective keeps its basic form, and the word after it is `als`, not `dan`.\n\n> Mijn kamer is even groot als de keuken. — My room is just as big as the kitchen.\n> Utrecht is niet zo groot als Amsterdam. — Utrecht is not as big as Amsterdam.\n\n### Before a noun\n\nA comparative before a noun follows the ordinary `-e` rule (unit A2 · 10): it takes `-e`, except with a het-word after `een`, `geen` or no article.\n\n> Zij willen een grotere keuken. — They want a bigger kitchen.\n> Zij willen een groter balkon. — They want a bigger balcony.\n\nLong comparatives sometimes drop that `-e` (`een veiliger buurt`). Following the rule is always right.\n\n### Small words before a comparative\n\n`veel groter` (much bigger), `nog groter` (even bigger), `steeds groter` (bigger and bigger).\n\n> Het verkeer wordt steeds drukker. — The traffic is getting busier and busier.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No `meer` + adjective: `interessanter`, `belangrijker`, `gezelliger`, not *meer interessant. `Meer` is the comparative of `veel`: `meer ruimte`, `meer mensen`.\n- `dan` after a comparative, `als` after `even` and `zo`: `groter dan`, but `even groot als`.\n- `duurder` has a d, `duurst` has none: never *duurdst.\n- `beter` and `best`, never *goeder and *goedst.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has a short comparative (`більший`, `дешевший`) and a long one with `більш` (`більш цікавий`). Dutch normally has only the short one: `interessanter`. `Ніж` or `за` after a comparative is always `dan`. And `найбільший` is `de grootste` or `het grootst`: an ending, not a prefix.","words":[{"nl":"dorp","art":"het","pl":"dorpen","en":"village","pos":"n"},{"nl":"platteland","art":"het","pl":"","en":"countryside","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"inwoner","art":"de","pl":"inwoners","en":"inhabitant","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lawaai","art":"het","pl":"","en":"noise","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"ruimte","art":"de","pl":"ruimtes","en":"space","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lucht","art":"de","pl":"luchten","en":"air / sky","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voordeel","art":"het","pl":"voordelen","en":"advantage","pos":"n"},{"nl":"nadeel","art":"het","pl":"nadelen","en":"disadvantage","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verschil","art":"het","pl":"verschillen","en":"difference","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rustig","en":"quiet / calm","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"veilig","en":"safe","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"schoon","en":"clean","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"ruim","en":"spacious","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"saai","en":"boring","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"stil","en":"silent (no sound)","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"dichtbij","en":"nearby / close","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Een dorp is rustiger dan een stad.","en":"A village is quieter than a city.","hint":["dorp","rustig","dan","stad"],"note":"rustig + er = rustiger. After a comparative comes dan, not als."},{"nl":"Wat is het grootste verschil tussen een dorp en een stad?","en":"What is the biggest difference between a village and a city?","hint":["groot","verschil","tussen","dorp","stad"],"note":"groot + st = grootst, and before a noun the superlative always takes -e: het grootste verschil."},{"nl":"In het centrum zijn de huizen het duurst.","en":"In the centre the houses are the most expensive.","hint":["centrum","huis","duur"],"note":"duur + st = duurst, without d: only the comparative duurder has one. On its own the superlative takes het: het duurst (het duurste is also right).","alt":["In het centrum zijn de huizen het duurste.","De huizen zijn in het centrum het duurst.","De huizen zijn in het centrum het duurste."]},{"nl":"Wij zoeken een groter huis met een tuin.","en":"We are looking for a bigger house with a garden.","hint":["zoeken","groot","huis","tuin"],"note":"Before a noun the comparative follows the -e rule. Huis is a het-word after een, so no -e: een groter huis. With a de-word: een grotere tuin.","alt":["We zoeken een groter huis met een tuin."]},{"nl":"Mijn zus woont liever in een klein dorp.","en":"My sister prefers to live in a small village.","hint":["zus","wonen","graag","klein","dorp"],"note":"liever is the comparative of graag: woont graag (likes living), woont liever (prefers to live). Never *meer graag."},{"nl":"Is jullie nieuwe huis even groot als het oude?","en":"Is your (plural) new house just as big as the old one?","hint":["nieuw","huis","even","groot","oud"],"note":"even … als says that two things are equal. The adjective keeps its basic form, groot, and the word after it is als, not dan.","alt":["Is jullie nieuwe huis net zo groot als het oude?"]},{"nl":"De lucht is hier niet zo schoon als in ons dorp.","en":"The air here is not as clean as in our village.","hint":["lucht","hier","schoon","dorp"],"note":"niet zo … als: the adjective keeps its basic form, schoon, and als follows. The same idea with a comparative: In ons dorp is de lucht schoner dan hier.","alt":["De lucht hier is niet zo schoon als in ons dorp.","Hier is de lucht niet zo schoon als in ons dorp."]},{"nl":"De huizen in de stad worden steeds duurder.","en":"The houses in the city are getting more and more expensive.","hint":["huis","stad","worden","steeds","duur"],"note":"steeds + comparative means 'more and more'. Duur ends in r, so the comparative gets a d: duurder."},{"nl":"Het belangrijkste nadeel van de stad is het lawaai.","en":"The most important disadvantage of the city is the noise.","hint":["belangrijk","nadeel","stad","lawaai"],"note":"Long adjectives take the ending too: belangrijk + st, and -e before the noun. Not *het meest belangrijke nadeel."},{"nl":"Op het platteland heb je meer ruimte en minder verkeer.","en":"In the countryside you have more space and less traffic.","hint":["platteland","hebben","veel","ruimte","weinig","verkeer"],"note":"meer and minder are the irregular comparatives of veel and weinig. Before a noun they do not change: meer ruimte, minder verkeer.","alt":["Je hebt op het platteland meer ruimte en minder verkeer."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het openbaar vervoer is in de stad ___ dan op het platteland.","options":["goeder","beter","meer goed"],"correct":1,"en":"Public transport is better in the city than in the countryside.","note":"goed is irregular: goed, beter, best. There is no *goeder, and meer goed is not Dutch either."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ons dorp is kleiner ___ jullie stad.","options":["als","dat","dan"],"correct":2,"en":"Our village is smaller than your (plural) city.","note":"After a comparative the standard is dan. You will hear als in speech, but in writing and in exams it counts as a mistake."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze hebben nu een ___ tuin.","options":["groter","grotere","meer grote"],"correct":1,"en":"They now have a bigger garden.","note":"Before a noun the comparative follows the -e rule. Tuin is a de-word, so -e: een grotere tuin. Without -e only for a het-word after een: een groter huis."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het ___ voordeel van een dorp is de rust.","options":["grootst","meest grote","grootste"],"correct":2,"en":"The biggest advantage of a village is the peace and quiet.","note":"Before a noun the superlative always takes -e: het grootste voordeel. Dutch does not build it with meest."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik woon ___ in een dorp dan in een stad.","options":["liever","graager","meer graag"],"correct":0,"en":"I prefer living in a village to living in a city.","note":"graag is irregular: graag, liever, het liefst. Liever … dan … says what you prefer."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De lucht in de stad is ___ (vies) dan in ons dorp.","answers":["viezer"],"en":"The air in the city is dirtier than in our village.","note":"vies + er. The s of vies becomes z when an ending is added, as in vieze: viezer."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Welke stad heeft de ___ (veel) inwoners?","answers":["meeste"],"en":"Which city has the most inhabitants?","note":"veel, meer, meest. Before a plural noun it is de meeste, with de and with -e: de meeste inwoners."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn werk is nu ___ (dichtbij) dan vroeger.","answers":["dichterbij"],"en":"My work is closer now than it used to be.","note":"In dichtbij the ending goes on the first part: dichterbij. The dan after it shows that a comparative is needed."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het leven in een dorp is niet zo ___ (saai) als je denkt.","answers":["saai"],"en":"Life in a village is not as boring as you think.","note":"After zo (and after even) the adjective keeps its basic form: niet zo saai als. The comparative saaier would need dan."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Van alle steden is Amsterdam het ___ (duur).","answers":["duurst","duurste"],"en":"Of all cities Amsterdam is the most expensive.","note":"The superlative is duur + st, without the d of duurder. After het both duurst and duurste are right."},{"type":"gap","nl":"'s Nachts is het hier nog ___ (stil) dan overdag.","answers":["stiller"],"en":"At night it is even quieter here than during the day.","note":"stil has a short vowel, so the l is doubled before -er: stiller. Nog before a comparative means 'even'."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ons dorp","words":["is","niet zo","groot","als","jullie stad"],"answers":["Ons dorp is niet zo groot als jullie stad."],"en":"Our village is not as big as your (plural) city.","note":"niet zo + adjective + als: the adjective stands between zo and als, in its basic form."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the same with a comparative. Start with Een huis.","from":"Een flat is niet zo ruim als een huis.","answers":["Een huis is ruimer dan een flat."],"en":"A house is more spacious than a flat.","note":"niet zo ruim als turns round into ruimer dan: ruim + er, and dan after the comparative."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say that it is getting busier and busier: use steeds.","from":"Het centrum wordt druk.","answers":["Het centrum wordt steeds drukker."],"en":"The centre is getting busier and busier.","note":"steeds + comparative. Druk has a short vowel, so the k is doubled: drukker."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Deze buurt is meer veilig dan het centrum.","answers":["Deze buurt is veiliger dan het centrum."],"en":"This neighbourhood is safer than the centre.","note":"Dutch puts the ending on the adjective itself: veilig + er. Meer veilig is English, not Dutch."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ons huis is even groot dan jullie huis.","answers":["Ons huis is even groot als jullie huis."],"en":"Our house is just as big as your (plural) house.","note":"After even + adjective comes als. Dan only follows a comparative: groter dan."}]},
  {"id":"a2-20","level":"A2","icon":"🧭","title":"Finding the way","grammar":"Prepositions of place and direction","summary":"Where: in, op, aan, bij … Where to: naar. Each place has its own preposition and the pairs are fixed: op school, op het station, in de trein, aan tafel, bij de dokter. Thuis is being at home, naar huis is going home.","explain":"A preposition (*het voorzetsel*) says where something is or where it is going. The words themselves are easy. The hard part is that every language divides places differently: Dutch `op` is not always English \"on\", and `in`, `op`, `aan` and `bij` each have places that belong to them. Those combinations are fixed, so learn them as they are.\n\n### Where something is\n\n| preposition | meaning | example |\n| in | in, inside | in de tas |\n| op | on, on top of | op de tafel |\n| aan | on, at (a side or edge) | aan de muur |\n| bij | at, near | bij de ingang |\n| naast | next to | naast de bank |\n| tussen | between | tussen de bank en de kerk |\n| tegenover | opposite | tegenover het park |\n| voor, achter | in front of, behind | achter het station |\n| boven, onder | above, under | onder de brug |\n\n> De bakker is naast de bank. — The baker's is next to the bank.\n> De fietsen staan achter het station. — The bicycles are behind the station.\n\n### in, op, aan, bij: the fixed places\n\n- **op**: `op het station`, `op school`, `op kantoor`, `op het werk`, `op straat`, `op de markt`, `op de eerste verdieping`\n- **in**: `in de stad`, `in de trein`, `in het ziekenhuis`, `in de buurt`\n- **aan**: `aan tafel`, `aan zee`, `aan de muur`, `aan de linkerkant`\n- **bij**: `bij de dokter`, `bij Anna`, `bij de bakker`, `bij het raam`\n\nRoughly: `op` for a surface, an open place and many places where you work or learn; `in` for a space you are inside, and for a town or an area; `aan` for a side or an edge; `bij` for a person, their shop or practice, and for \"near\".\n\n> Ik sta op het station. — I am at the station.\n> We zitten al in de trein. — We are already on the train.\n> Mijn dochter is bij de dokter. — My daughter is at the doctor's.\n\n### Where something is going\n\n| preposition | meaning | example |\n| naar | to | naar het station |\n| uit | out of, from | uit de trein |\n| van … naar | from … to | van Utrecht naar Den Haag |\n| tot, tot aan | as far as | tot de rotonde |\n| door | through | door het park |\n| langs | along, past | langs het water |\n| over | over, across | over de brug |\n| om | round | om de hoek |\n| richting | towards | richting het centrum |\n\nFor the way to a place Dutch changes the preposition: `Ik ben op het station`, but `Ik ga naar het station`; `Ik ben bij de dokter`, but `Ik ga naar de dokter`.\n\n### thuis and naar huis\n\n`thuis` is where you are, `naar huis` is where you go, both without an article. The same pair exists for `boven` (upstairs), `buiten` (outside), `binnen` (inside), `links` and `rechts`: `Ik ben boven`, but `Ik ga naar boven`.\n\n> Ik blijf thuis. — I am staying at home.\n> Om vijf uur gaan we naar huis. — We go home at five o'clock.\n\n### Giving directions\n\nDirections are given with `je gaat` or `u gaat`, or with the imperative `ga` (unit A2 · 24).\n\n> U gaat hier rechtdoor. — You go straight ahead here.\n> Bij het kruispunt gaat u rechtsaf. — At the crossroads you turn right.\n> Neem de eerste straat links. — Take the first street on the left.\n> Steek bij het stoplicht de straat over. — Cross the street at the traffic lights.\n\n`rechtdoor`, `linksaf` and `rechtsaf` are written as one word. `oversteken` takes no preposition: `de straat oversteken`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- English \"at\" is not one word in Dutch: `op school`, `op het werk`, `op het station`, but `bij de dokter` and `aan tafel`.\n- `in de trein`, `in de bus`: English says \"on the train\".\n- `op straat`: English says \"in the street\".\n- The way to a place is `naar`: `Ik ga naar de stad` (not *Ik ga in de stad), `Ik ga naar het werk` (not *Ik ga op het werk).\n- `naar huis`, never *naar thuis.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian keeps the preposition and changes the case: `на роботі` (where?) against `на роботу` (where to?). Dutch has no cases and changes the preposition: `op het werk` against `naar het werk`. Which preposition a place takes is fixed in both languages. Often they match (`на вокзалі` = `op het station`, `у лікаря` = `bij de dokter`, `вдома` = `thuis`, `додому` = `naar huis`), but not always: `в школі` is `op school`.","words":[{"nl":"kruispunt","art":"het","pl":"kruispunten","en":"crossroads","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rotonde","art":"de","pl":"rotondes","en":"roundabout","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stoplicht","art":"het","pl":"stoplichten","en":"traffic light","pos":"n"},{"nl":"brug","art":"de","pl":"bruggen","en":"bridge","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hoek","art":"de","pl":"hoeken","en":"corner","pos":"n"},{"nl":"halte","art":"de","pl":"haltes","en":"stop (bus or tram)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"plein","art":"het","pl":"pleinen","en":"square","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ingang","art":"de","pl":"ingangen","en":"entrance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"oversteken","past":"stak over","perf":"is overgestoken","en":"to cross (the street)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"tegenover","en":"opposite","pos":"prep"},{"nl":"langs","en":"along / past","pos":"prep"},{"nl":"tussen","en":"between","pos":"prep"},{"nl":"naast","en":"next to","pos":"prep"},{"nl":"richting","en":"towards","pos":"prep"},{"nl":"rechtdoor","en":"straight ahead","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"linksaf","en":"left (turning)","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De halte is tegenover het station.","en":"The stop is opposite the station.","hint":["halte","tegenover","station"],"note":"tegenover is one word and stands before the noun, where Dutch prepositions normally stand. The noun does not change after it: Dutch has no cases."},{"nl":"De ingang is niet aan de voorkant, maar om de hoek.","en":"The entrance is not at the front, but round the corner.","hint":["ingang","voorkant","hoek"],"note":"A side takes aan: aan de voorkant, aan de linkerkant. Om de hoek is 'round the corner'. Niet stands before the phrase with the preposition."},{"nl":"Je gaat rechtdoor tot de rotonde.","en":"You go straight ahead as far as the roundabout.","hint":["gaan","rechtdoor","rotonde"],"note":"tot gives the point where you stop: as far as. Rechtdoor is one word.","alt":["Je gaat rechtdoor tot aan de rotonde."]},{"nl":"Bij het stoplicht ga je linksaf.","en":"At the traffic lights you turn left.","hint":["stoplicht","gaan","linksaf"],"note":"bij for a point on the way: bij het stoplicht. The phrase stands first, so the verb comes before je, and je after the verb means no t: ga je.","alt":["Bij het stoplicht ga jij linksaf.","Bij de stoplichten ga je linksaf.","Bij het stoplicht sla je linksaf.","Je gaat bij het stoplicht linksaf."]},{"nl":"De apotheek is tussen de bank en de bakker.","en":"The pharmacy is between the bank and the baker's.","hint":["apotheek","tussen","bank","bakker"],"note":"tussen … en …: between two things. De bakker is both the person and the shop."},{"nl":"De kinderen zijn nog op school.","en":"The children are still at school.","hint":["kind","nog","school"],"note":"op school is a fixed combination: op, and no article. English 'at' has no single Dutch translation."},{"nl":"U loopt langs het water tot de brug.","en":"You (formal) walk along the water as far as the bridge.","hint":["lopen","langs","water","brug"],"note":"langs follows the side of something: along the water. Tot de brug gives the point where you stop.","alt":["U loopt langs het water tot aan de brug."]},{"nl":"Moet ik hier de straat oversteken?","en":"Do I have to cross the street here?","hint":["moeten","hier","straat","oversteken"],"note":"oversteken has its object without a preposition: de straat oversteken. After moeten the separable verb stays in one piece at the end.","alt":["Moet ik de straat hier oversteken?"]},{"nl":"De tram richting het centrum stopt op het plein.","en":"The tram towards the centre stops in the square.","hint":["tram","richting","centrum","stoppen","plein"],"note":"richting works as a preposition: towards. A square is an open place, so op het plein, where English says 'in' or 'on'."},{"nl":"Na het werk ga ik meteen naar huis.","en":"After work I go straight home.","hint":["werk","gaan","meteen","huis"],"note":"naar huis is where you go; thuis is where you are. Neither has an article, and *naar thuis does not exist.","alt":["Na het werk ga ik direct naar huis.","Na mijn werk ga ik meteen naar huis."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn collega is vandaag niet ___ kantoor.","options":["in","op","aan"],"correct":1,"en":"My colleague is not at the office today.","note":"op kantoor is a fixed combination, without an article. In needs an article and then means inside the building: in het kantoor."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik zit nu ___ de trein naar Utrecht.","options":["op","aan","in"],"correct":2,"en":"I am on the train to Utrecht now.","note":"You are inside the train, so in de trein. English says 'on the train', but in the Netherlands it is in."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het museum is ___ de linkerkant.","options":["aan","op","in"],"correct":0,"en":"The museum is on the left-hand side.","note":"A side takes aan: aan de linkerkant, aan de rechterkant, aan de voorkant."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb een afspraak ___ de tandarts.","options":["aan","bij","op"],"correct":1,"en":"I have an appointment at the dentist's.","note":"bij + a person means at that person's place or practice: bij de tandarts, bij de dokter, bij Anna."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik blijf vandaag ___.","options":["naar huis","thuis","huis"],"correct":1,"en":"I am staying at home today.","note":"thuis says where you are, and blijven is about staying where you are. Naar huis is for going there."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De kinderen spelen ___ straat.","options":["in","aan","op"],"correct":2,"en":"The children are playing in the street.","note":"op straat is fixed, and it has no article. English 'in the street' does not help here."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik woon ___ (opposite) het park.","answers":["tegenover"],"en":"I live opposite the park.","note":"tegenover is written as one word and comes before the noun."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het café is ___ (next to) de bioscoop.","answers":["naast"],"en":"The café is next to the cinema.","note":"naast is one word for English 'next to'. No second preposition is added: naast de bioscoop."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Je fietst ___ (along) het kanaal tot het kruispunt.","answers":["langs"],"en":"You cycle along the canal as far as the crossroads.","note":"langs follows the side of something long: a canal, a river, a road. Tot gives the point where you stop."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De bus rijdt ___ (from) het station naar het ziekenhuis.","answers":["van","vanaf"],"en":"The bus goes from the station to the hospital.","note":"van … naar gives the start and the end of a route. Vanaf is also right for the starting point."},{"type":"order","lead":"Bij het stoplicht","words":["steekt","u","de straat","over"],"answers":["Bij het stoplicht steekt u de straat over."],"en":"At the traffic lights you (formal) cross the street.","note":"Bij het stoplicht stands first, so the verb comes second and u after it. The prefix of oversteken goes to the end, and de straat has no preposition."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"They are not there yet, they are on their way: use gaan.","from":"De kinderen zijn buiten.","answers":["De kinderen gaan naar buiten."],"en":"The children are going outside.","note":"Where you are: buiten. Where you go: naar buiten. For the way to a place Dutch adds naar."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"He is not there yet, he is on his way: use gaan.","from":"Mijn man is bij de dokter.","answers":["Mijn man gaat naar de dokter."],"en":"My husband is going to the doctor.","note":"bij de dokter is where you are, naar de dokter is where you go. Ukrainian changes the case here, Dutch changes the preposition."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"They have arrived: say where they are, with zijn.","from":"Wij gaan naar huis.","answers":["Wij zijn thuis.","We zijn thuis."],"en":"We are at home.","note":"naar huis is the way there, thuis is being there. 'At home' is one word in Dutch, without a preposition."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik ga na het werk naar thuis.","answers":["Ik ga na het werk naar huis."],"en":"I go home after work.","note":"After naar it is huis: naar huis. Thuis already means 'at home' and never follows naar."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Om acht uur ga ik op het werk.","answers":["Om acht uur ga ik naar het werk."],"en":"At eight o'clock I go to work.","note":"op het werk says where you are. Going there needs naar: naar het werk."}]},
  {"id":"a2-21","level":"A2","icon":"⏰","title":"Times and dates","grammar":"Prepositions of time and time expressions","summary":"om + clock time, op + day or date, in + month, season, year. over een uur = from now; twee jaar geleden = ago. sinds and al take the present for what is still going on. half drie is 2.30.","explain":"English and Dutch both use small words to place things in time, but they do not always choose the same ones: \"in an hour\" is `over een uur`, \"three years ago\" is `drie jaar geleden`. Most of them are fixed pairs that you learn once and then use every day: for opening hours, appointments and dates.\n\n### om, op, in\n\n| | use it for | example |\n| om | a clock time | om drie uur |\n| op | a day, a date | op maandag, op 5 mei |\n| in | a month, season, year | in mei, in de zomer, in 2020 |\n\n> De bibliotheek gaat om half negen open. — The library opens at half past eight.\n> Op feestdagen sluit de winkel al om vier uur. — On public holidays the shop already closes at four.\n> In de zomer zijn de openingstijden anders. — In the summer the opening hours are different.\n\nAlso with in: `in het weekend`, `in de ochtend`. For parts of the day the short forms are more usual: `'s morgens` or `'s ochtends`, `'s middags`, `'s avonds`, `'s nachts`. A day without op (`Ik werk maandag`) means this coming or this past Monday; `op maandag` can also mean every Monday.\n\n### No preposition\n\nWith `vandaag`, `morgen`, `gisteren`, `vorige week`, `volgend jaar`, `deze week`, `dit jaar`, `elke dag`, `afgelopen zaterdag` there is no preposition: `Ik heb volgende week vrij`, never *in volgende week.\n\n### From now, ago, how long\n\n- `over` = from now: `over een uur`, `over twee weken`.\n- `geleden` = ago, and it comes **after** the time: `twee jaar geleden`.\n- `binnen` = within: `binnen een week`.\n- `voor` and `na` = before and after: `voor het werk`, `na de les`.\n- `tot` = until; `van … tot` = from … to (on signs: `tot en met`, up to and including); `tussen … en` = between; `vanaf` = from … on; `tijdens` = during.\n- `rond` = about, around: `rond tien uur`; `tegen` = towards, shortly before: `tegen vijf uur`.\n\n> De vergadering begint over een kwartier. — The meeting starts in a quarter of an hour.\n> Ik heb de afspraak twee weken geleden verzet. — I moved the appointment two weeks ago.\n\n### sinds and al: the present tense\n\nFor something that started in the past and is **still going on**, Dutch uses the **present** tense: `sinds` + a starting point, or `al` + a length of time.\n\n> Ik woon sinds 2021 in Nederland. — I have been living in the Netherlands since 2021.\n> Hij werkt hier al drie jaar. — He has been working here for three years.\n\nThe perfect `Hij heeft hier drie jaar gewerkt` means that he no longer works here.\n\n### pas and al\n\n`al` = already, earlier than expected; `pas` = not until, later than expected.\n\n> De bus komt pas over twintig minuten. — The bus is not coming for another twenty minutes.\n\n### The clock and dates\n\nDutch counts the half hour towards the **next** hour: `half drie` is 2.30. Around it: `kwart over twee` (2.15), `tien voor half drie` (2.20), `vijf over half drie` (2.35), `kwart voor drie` (2.45). For 2.20 and 2.40 you also hear `twintig over twee` and `twintig voor drie`.\n\nDates: `op 1 mei`, spoken `op één mei` or `op de eerste mei`. The ordinal ends in **-ste** for 1, 8 and from 20 on (`eerste`, `achtste`, `twintigste`), and in **-de** for the others (`tweede`, `derde`, `negende`, `twaalfde`). The date question is `De hoeveelste is het vandaag?`\n\n> De hoeveelste is het vandaag? — What is the date today?\n\n### Watch out\n\n- From now is over, not in: `over een uur`, not *in een uur.\n- Still going on: `Ik woon hier sinds 2021`, not *Ik heb hier sinds 2021 gewoond.\n- A date takes op: `op 15 mei`, not *in 15 mei.\n- `half drie` is 2.30, not 3.30.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nTwo things work as in Ukrainian: `пів на третю` is `half drie`, and `тому` comes after the time just like `geleden`: `два роки тому`, `twee jaar geleden`. And Ukrainian also uses the present for \"since\": `Я живу тут з 2021 року` is `Ik woon hier sinds 2021`.","words":[{"nl":"agenda","art":"de","pl":"agenda's","en":"diary / calendar (for appointments)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tijdstip","art":"het","pl":"tijdstippen","en":"time (point in time)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"openingstijd","art":"de","pl":"openingstijden","en":"opening time (mostly plural: opening hours)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kwartier","art":"het","pl":"kwartieren","en":"quarter of an hour","pos":"n"},{"nl":"datum","art":"de","pl":"datums","en":"date (day of the month)","pos":"n","tag":"plural also data"},{"nl":"feestdag","art":"de","pl":"feestdagen","en":"public holiday","pos":"n"},{"nl":"werkdag","art":"de","pl":"werkdagen","en":"working day","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verzetten","past":"verzette","perf":"heeft verzet","en":"to move (an appointment) / to reschedule","pos":"v"},{"nl":"annuleren","past":"annuleerde","perf":"heeft geannuleerd","en":"to cancel","pos":"v"},{"nl":"duren","past":"duurde","perf":"heeft geduurd","en":"to last / to take (time)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"sluiten","past":"sloot","perf":"heeft gesloten","en":"to close","pos":"v"},{"nl":"op tijd","en":"on time","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"tijdens","en":"during","pos":"prep"},{"nl":"sinds","en":"since","pos":"prep"},{"nl":"geleden","en":"ago","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"pas","en":"not until / only (later than expected)","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De bibliotheek gaat om half negen open.","en":"The library opens at half past eight.","hint":["bibliotheek","half","negen","opengaan"],"note":"om before a clock time. Half negen is half an hour before nine, so 8.30, not 9.30.","alt":["Om half negen gaat de bibliotheek open.","De bibliotheek opent om half negen.","Om half negen opent de bibliotheek."]},{"nl":"Op feestdagen sluit de winkel al om vier uur.","en":"On public holidays the shop already closes at four o'clock.","hint":["feestdag","sluiten","winkel","al","uur"],"note":"op before a day, om before the clock time. Al says it is earlier than you might expect.","alt":["De winkel sluit op feestdagen al om vier uur."]},{"nl":"In de zomer zijn de openingstijden anders.","en":"In the summer the opening hours are different.","hint":["zomer","openingstijd","anders"],"note":"in before a season, with the article: in de zomer. The same with months and years, without article: in juli, in 2025.","alt":["De openingstijden zijn in de zomer anders."]},{"nl":"De vergadering begint over een kwartier.","en":"The meeting starts in a quarter of an hour.","hint":["vergadering","beginnen","kwartier"],"note":"English 'in' for time from now is over in Dutch: over een kwartier. In een kwartier would mean 'within', how long something takes.","alt":["Over een kwartier begint de vergadering."]},{"nl":"Ik woon sinds 2021 in Nederland.","en":"I have been living in the Netherlands since 2021.","hint":["wonen","sinds","Nederland"],"note":"I still live here, so Dutch uses the present tense with sinds, where English uses 'have been living'.","alt":["Sinds 2021 woon ik in Nederland.","Ik woon in Nederland sinds 2021."]},{"nl":"Hij werkt hier al drie jaar.","en":"He has been working here for three years.","hint":["werken","al","jaar"],"note":"al + a length of time, with the present: he still works here. Hij heeft hier drie jaar gewerkt would mean he has left.","alt":["Hij werkt al drie jaar hier."]},{"nl":"Ik heb de afspraak twee weken geleden verzet.","en":"I moved the appointment two weeks ago.","hint":["afspraak","week","geleden","verzetten"],"note":"geleden comes after the length of time: twee weken geleden. Here the action is finished, so the perfect is right.","alt":["Twee weken geleden heb ik de afspraak verzet.","Ik heb twee weken geleden de afspraak verzet."]},{"nl":"Het concert duurde tot elf uur 's avonds.","en":"The concert lasted until eleven o'clock in the evening.","hint":["concert","duren","tot","uur","'s avonds"],"note":"tot = until. 's avonds is the usual way to say 'in the evening'; it comes after the clock time here.","alt":["Het concert duurde tot 's avonds elf uur."]},{"nl":"De hoeveelste is het vandaag?","en":"What is the date today?","hint":["hoeveelste","vandaag"],"note":"The fixed question for the date: de hoeveelste, from the ordinal numbers. Vandaag needs no preposition.","alt":["Welke datum is het vandaag?"]},{"nl":"De bus komt pas over twintig minuten.","en":"The bus is not coming for another twenty minutes.","hint":["bus","komen","pas","minuut"],"note":"pas = not until, later than you would like. Over twintig minuten counts from now."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Op welk tijdstip begint de les? ___ tien uur.","options":["Op","Om","In"],"correct":1,"en":"At what time does the lesson start? At ten o'clock.","note":"A clock time takes om. The word tijdstip itself takes op (op welk tijdstip, op dit tijdstip), but tien uur needs om."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Volgens mijn agenda is de afspraak ___ 3 juni.","options":["in","om","op"],"correct":2,"en":"According to my diary the appointment is on 3 June.","note":"A date takes op, like a day: op 3 juni, op maandag. In is only for the whole month: in juni."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Je bent op tijd: de film begint pas ___ tien minuten.","options":["in","over","om"],"correct":1,"en":"You are on time: the film does not start for another ten minutes.","note":"Counting from now is over: over tien minuten. In tien minuten is the English pattern, not Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze heeft de les twee dagen ___ geannuleerd.","options":["sinds","geleden","voor"],"correct":1,"en":"She cancelled the lesson two days ago.","note":"'Ago' is geleden, and it comes after the time: twee dagen geleden. Sinds means since, a starting point."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de lunchpauze is de balie gesloten.","options":["Om","Tijdens","Op"],"correct":1,"en":"During the lunch break the desk is closed.","note":"tijdens = during, for the whole period of something. Om only goes with a clock time."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het is 10.20: het is ___.","options":["tien over tien","tien voor half elf","tien over half elf"],"correct":1,"en":"It is 10.20: twenty past ten.","note":"Half elf is 10.30, and 10.20 is ten minutes before that: tien voor half elf. Tien over half elf is 10.40."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het is 7.30: het is half ___.","answers":["acht"],"en":"It is 7.30: half past seven.","note":"Dutch counts the half hour towards the next hour: 7.30 is half acht, half an hour before eight."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op werkdagen zijn we ___ negen tot vijf uur open.","answers":["van","vanaf"],"en":"On working days we are open from nine to five.","note":"From … to is van … tot; vanaf … tot is also correct. Tot without van would just mean until."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De vergadering is op de ___ (acht).","answers":["achtste"],"en":"The meeting is on the eighth.","note":"Acht is one of the numbers that take -ste, like eerste and twintigste. Most others take -de: tweede, negende."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De winkel gaat ___ (not until) om tien uur open, niet om negen.","answers":["pas"],"en":"The shop does not open until ten o'clock, not at nine.","note":"pas = not until, later than expected. Al would say the opposite: earlier than expected."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["al","hier","vijf jaar","woon"],"answers":["Ik woon hier al vijf jaar.","Ik woon al vijf jaar hier."],"en":"I have been living here for five years.","note":"The present tense with al + a length of time, because you still live here. English needs 'have been living'."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["om drie uur","ben","bij de tandarts","ik"],"answers":["Morgen ben ik om drie uur bij de tandarts.","Morgen ben ik bij de tandarts om drie uur."],"en":"Tomorrow I am at the dentist's at three o'clock.","note":"Morgen needs no preposition. In the middle of the sentence the time usually comes before the place: om drie uur bij de tandarts."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the time the way people speak, with half.","from":"De film begint om 20.30 uur.","answers":["De film begint om half negen.","De film begint om half negen 's avonds.","De film begint 's avonds om half negen."],"en":"The film starts at half past eight.","note":"20.30 is 8.30 in the evening, and Dutch counts towards the next hour: half negen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb hier sinds 2020 gewoond.","answers":["Ik woon hier sinds 2020.","Ik woon sinds 2020 hier.","Sinds 2020 woon ik hier."],"en":"I have been living here since 2020.","note":"You still live here, so Dutch uses the present tense with sinds. The perfect would mean that you have moved away."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn dochter is in 15 mei geboren.","answers":["Mijn dochter is op 15 mei geboren."],"en":"My daughter was born on 15 May.","note":"A date takes op, just like a day of the week. In is for the month alone: in mei."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De dokter belt je in een kwartier terug.","answers":["De dokter belt je over een kwartier terug.","De dokter belt je binnen een kwartier terug."],"en":"The doctor will call you back in a quarter of an hour.","note":"From now is over: over een kwartier. Binnen een kwartier (within) is also right. In een kwartier is copied from English."}]},
  {"id":"a2-22","level":"A2","icon":"🎨","title":"Free time","grammar":"Linking sentences: en, maar, of, want, dus","summary":"en, maar, of and want stand outside the next sentence: normal order after them. After dus both orders are right (dus ik blijf, dus blijf ik). A comma before maar, want and dus.","explain":"Five short words join two sentences into one: `en` (and), `maar` (but), `of` (or), `want` (because) and `dus` (so). For four of them the word order is easy: they stand **outside** the second sentence, and that sentence keeps the order it would have on its own. `dus` is different, because it can also be part of the sentence.\n\n### en, maar, of, want: nothing changes\n\nThe conjunction does not count as the first element of the second sentence (you will also see this called *position 0*). After it comes a complete sentence, with the verb in second place as usual.\n\n| first sentence | link | second sentence |\n| Ik ben moe | en | ik ga naar huis. |\n| Ik wil wel | maar | ik heb geen tijd. |\n| We gaan zwemmen | of | we gaan fietsen. |\n| Ik blijf thuis | want | ik ben ziek. |\n\n> Ik zing in een koor en mijn man speelt gitaar. — I sing in a choir and my husband plays the guitar.\n> Ik train graag, maar ik ben niet fanatiek. — I like training, but I am not a fanatic.\n\nWhen the second sentence starts with something other than the subject, it has inversion, exactly as it would on its own:\n\n> Ons team heeft gewonnen, maar morgen spelen we weer. — Our team has won, but tomorrow we play again.\n\n### want and omdat\n\nBoth give a reason. `want` joins two main clauses, so the order stays normal. `omdat` starts a sub-clause, and there the verb goes to the end (unit A2 · 23):\n\n> Ik kom niet, want ik heb training. — I am not coming, because I have training.\n> Ik kom niet, omdat ik training heb. — I am not coming, because I have training.\n\nThe part with `want` always comes after the part it explains, never before it: not *Want ik heb training, ik kom niet. (In writing you will sometimes see a new sentence start with `Want`; it then gives the reason for the sentence before it.)\n\n### dus: three right orders\n\n`dus` can be a conjunction, with normal order after it, or an adverb. As an adverb it can take the first place, and then the verb comes before the subject; or it can stand in the middle of the sentence. All of these are correct:\n\n> Het regent, dus ik blijf thuis. — It is raining, so I am staying at home.\n> Het regent, dus blijf ik thuis. — It is raining, so I am staying at home.\n> Het regent. Ik blijf dus thuis. — It is raining. So I am staying at home.\n\n### of: or\n\n`of` joins two words or two sentences: `Wil je thee of koffie?`, `Ga je mee, of blijf je thuis?` (Later you will also meet `of` meaning \"whether\"; that is a different use.)\n\n### Leaving out what is repeated\n\nAfter `en`, `maar` and `of` you can leave out a subject that is the same in both parts, when the first sentence starts with that subject:\n\n> Ik brei een trui en luister naar de radio. — I knit a jumper and listen to the radio.\n\nWith `ik` repeated (`en ik luister`) it is also correct. After `want` and `dus` the subject must stay: `…, dus ik blijf thuis`, never *dus blijf thuis.\n\n### Commas\n\nPut a comma before `maar`, `want` and `dus`. Before `en` and `of` there is normally no comma, except to make a long sentence easier to read.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No inversion after want: `want ik ben moe`, not *want ben ik moe.\n- Do not mix want and omdat: `want ik ben moe` or `omdat ik moe ben`, not *omdat ik ben moe.\n- Do not drop the subject after want or dus.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian `бо` works like `want`: it gives a reason and changes nothing in the order after it. The difference is that Dutch order is fixed: after these words comes a normal Dutch sentence, with its verb in second place.","words":[{"nl":"hobby","art":"de","pl":"hobby's","en":"hobby","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vereniging","art":"de","pl":"verenigingen","en":"club / association","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lid","art":"het","pl":"leden","en":"member","pos":"n"},{"nl":"training","art":"de","pl":"trainingen","en":"training session / practice","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wedstrijd","art":"de","pl":"wedstrijden","en":"match / competition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"klimmen","past":"klom","perf":"heeft/is geklommen","en":"to climb","pos":"v"},{"nl":"contributie","art":"de","pl":"contributies","en":"membership fee","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tekenen","past":"tekende","perf":"heeft getekend","en":"to draw","pos":"v"},{"nl":"schilderen","past":"schilderde","perf":"heeft geschilderd","en":"to paint","pos":"v"},{"nl":"breien","past":"breide","perf":"heeft gebreid","en":"to knit","pos":"v"},{"nl":"trainen","past":"trainde","perf":"heeft getraind","en":"to train","pos":"v"},{"nl":"winnen","past":"won","perf":"heeft gewonnen","en":"to win","pos":"v"},{"nl":"fotograferen","past":"fotografeerde","perf":"heeft gefotografeerd","en":"to take photographs","pos":"v"},{"nl":"sportief","en":"sporty / athletic","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"fanatiek","en":"keen / fanatical","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"liever","en":"rather (would rather)","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik zing in een koor en mijn man speelt gitaar.","en":"I sing in a choir and my husband plays the guitar.","hint":["zingen","koor","man","spelen","gitaar"],"note":"en does not count as the first element: after it comes a normal sentence, subject first, then the verb."},{"nl":"Ik schilder graag, maar ik heb weinig tijd.","en":"I like painting, but I have little time.","hint":["schilderen","graag","weinig","tijd"],"note":"A comma before maar, and after it the normal order: ik heb.","alt":["Ik schilder graag, maar heb weinig tijd."]},{"nl":"Mijn zoon is lid van een voetbalvereniging, want hij voetbalt graag.","en":"My son is a member of a football club, because he likes playing football.","hint":["zoon","lid","voetbalvereniging","voetballen","graag"],"note":"want gives a reason with normal order: hij voetbalt. With omdat the verb would go to the end: omdat hij graag voetbalt.","alt":["Mijn zoon is lid van een voetbalvereniging, omdat hij graag voetbalt."]},{"nl":"We hebben vanavond training, dus ik kan niet komen.","en":"We have training this evening, so I cannot come.","hint":["vanavond","training","kunnen","komen"],"note":"After dus both orders are right: dus ik kan, with dus as a conjunction, or dus kan ik, with dus in the first place.","alt":["We hebben vanavond training, dus kan ik niet komen.","Vanavond hebben we training, dus ik kan niet komen.","Vanavond hebben we training, dus kan ik niet komen."]},{"nl":"Wil je liever tennissen of zwemmen?","en":"Would you rather play tennis or go swimming?","hint":["willen","liever","tennissen","zwemmen"],"note":"of joins two words here, two infinitives at the end of the question. Liever is the comparative of graag.","alt":["Wil jij liever tennissen of zwemmen?"]},{"nl":"Ons team heeft gewonnen, maar morgen spelen we weer.","en":"Our team has won, but tomorrow we play again.","hint":["team","winnen","morgen","spelen","weer"],"note":"After maar the second sentence starts with morgen, so it has inversion, as it would on its own: morgen spelen we.","alt":["Ons team heeft gewonnen, maar we spelen morgen weer."]},{"nl":"Ik brei een trui en luister naar de radio.","en":"I knit a jumper and listen to the radio.","hint":["breien","trui","luisteren","radio"],"note":"Both parts have the same subject, and the sentence starts with it, so after en ik can be left out.","alt":["Ik brei een trui en ik luister naar de radio."]},{"nl":"Hij is heel sportief, want hij traint vijf keer per week.","en":"He is very sporty, because he trains five times a week.","hint":["sportief","trainen","keer","week"],"note":"want + normal order: hij traint. The subject cannot be left out after want.","alt":["Hij is erg sportief, want hij traint vijf keer per week.","Hij is heel sportief, omdat hij vijf keer per week traint.","Hij is erg sportief, omdat hij vijf keer per week traint."]},{"nl":"Ik ben niet fanatiek, dus ik doe niet mee aan wedstrijden.","en":"I am not a fanatic, so I do not take part in competitions.","hint":["fanatiek","meedoen","wedstrijd"],"note":"dus as a conjunction keeps the normal order. Dus doe ik niet mee is just as right.","alt":["Ik ben niet fanatiek, dus doe ik niet mee aan wedstrijden."]},{"nl":"De contributie is vrij hoog, maar de trainers zijn goed.","en":"The membership fee is quite high, but the coaches are good.","hint":["contributie","vrij","hoog","trainer","goed"],"note":"maar joins two complete sentences, each with its own subject and verb, and a comma in front of maar."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Tekenen is mijn hobby, ___ ik heb weinig tijd.","options":["want","maar","dus"],"correct":1,"en":"Drawing is my hobby, but I have little time.","note":"The second part goes against the first, so maar. Want would give a reason and dus a result, and neither makes sense here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ga vanavond niet naar het koor, ___ ik ben verkouden.","options":["maar","dus","want"],"correct":2,"en":"I am not going to the choir this evening, because I have a cold.","note":"Being ill is the reason, so want. After want the order stays normal: ik ben."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik kom niet naar de wedstrijd, want ___.","options":["ik heb training","heb ik training","ik training heb"],"correct":0,"en":"I am not coming to the match, because I have training.","note":"want is outside the second sentence, so subject and verb keep their normal order. The verb at the end belongs to omdat."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik kom niet naar de wedstrijd, omdat ___.","options":["heb ik training","ik training heb","ik heb training"],"correct":1,"en":"I am not coming to the match, because I have training.","note":"omdat starts a sub-clause, and there the verb goes to the end: omdat ik training heb. That is the difference from want."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik train elke dag, ___ (because) ik wil de wedstrijd winnen.","answers":["want"],"en":"I train every day, because I want to win the match.","note":"A reason with normal order after it (ik wil) is want. Omdat would need the verb at the end: omdat ik de wedstrijd wil winnen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij is ziek, ___ (so) hij kan niet meedoen aan de wedstrijd.","answers":["dus"],"en":"He is ill, so he cannot take part in the match.","note":"A result is dus. Here it is used as a conjunction, with normal order after it: hij kan."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Gaan we zaterdag fietsen, ___ blijven we thuis?","answers":["of"],"en":"Are we going cycling on Saturday, or are we staying at home?","note":"of = or. It joins two questions here; each keeps its own order with the verb first."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik kom niet,","words":["want","ik","heb","vanavond","training"],"answers":["Ik kom niet, want ik heb vanavond training.","Ik kom niet, want vanavond heb ik training.","Ik kom niet, want ik heb training vanavond."],"en":"I am not coming, because I have training this evening.","note":"After want the second sentence has its own normal order: subject and verb, or vanavond first and then the verb before ik."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het regent,","words":["dus","we","trainen","binnen"],"answers":["Het regent, dus we trainen binnen.","Het regent, dus trainen we binnen."],"en":"It is raining, so we are training indoors.","note":"dus allows both orders: as a conjunction with normal order (dus we trainen), or in the first place with the verb before the subject (dus trainen we)."},{"type":"order","lead":"Vandaag heb ik geen tijd,","words":["maar","morgen","kom","ik"],"answers":["Vandaag heb ik geen tijd, maar morgen kom ik.","Vandaag heb ik geen tijd, maar ik kom morgen."],"en":"Today I have no time, but tomorrow I will come.","note":"maar is outside the second sentence. If morgen comes first, the verb comes before ik; if ik comes first, the order is normal."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with want.","from":"Ik ga naar de training. Ik wil fitter worden.","answers":["Ik ga naar de training, want ik wil fitter worden."],"en":"I am going to training, because I want to get fitter.","note":"The two sentences stay as they are, with a comma and want in between. After want nothing in the order changes."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with dus.","from":"Mijn vriendin is fanatiek. Ze traint elke dag.","answers":["Mijn vriendin is fanatiek, dus ze traint elke dag.","Mijn vriendin is fanatiek, dus zij traint elke dag.","Mijn vriendin is fanatiek, dus traint ze elke dag.","Mijn vriendin is fanatiek, dus traint zij elke dag."],"en":"My friend is a fanatic, so she trains every day.","note":"Both orders are right after dus: dus ze traint (conjunction) and dus traint ze (dus in the first place)."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with omdat instead of want.","from":"Ik ben lid van een klimclub, want ik vind klimmen spannend.","answers":["Ik ben lid van een klimclub, omdat ik klimmen spannend vind."],"en":"I am a member of a climbing club, because I find climbing exciting.","note":"omdat sends the verb to the end of its clause: omdat ik klimmen spannend vind. With want the verb stays in second place."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Leave out the subject that is repeated.","from":"Mijn broer fotografeert graag en hij tekent ook.","answers":["Mijn broer fotografeert graag en tekent ook."],"en":"My brother likes taking photographs and draws too.","note":"After en the same subject can go when the first sentence starts with it. The verb tekent then follows en directly."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik ga niet mee, want ben ik moe.","answers":["Ik ga niet mee, want ik ben moe."],"en":"I am not coming along, because I am tired.","note":"want does not take a place in the sentence after it, so there is no inversion: want ik ben moe."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn zus is ziek, dus komt vanavond niet.","answers":["Mijn zus is ziek, dus ze komt vanavond niet.","Mijn zus is ziek, dus zij komt vanavond niet.","Mijn zus is ziek, dus komt ze vanavond niet.","Mijn zus is ziek, dus komt zij vanavond niet."],"en":"My sister is ill, so she is not coming this evening.","note":"After dus the subject cannot be left out (after en it could: en komt vanavond niet). Both dus ze komt and dus komt ze are right."}]},
  {"id":"a2-23","level":"A2","icon":"💼","title":"Work and study","grammar":"Sub-clauses: omdat, dat, als","summary":"In a sub-clause the conjugated verb goes to the end: omdat ik ziek ben. dat is never left out. A sub-clause that comes first is the first element, so the main clause starts with its verb.","explain":"A sub-clause (*de bijzin*) is a sentence inside a sentence. It cannot stand on its own and starts with a word like `omdat` (because), `dat` (that) or `als` (if, when). Dutch marks it clearly: in a sub-clause the conjugated verb goes to the **end**.\n\n### The verb goes to the end\n\n| main clause | sub-clause |\n| Ik ben ziek. | … omdat ik ziek ben. |\n| De cursus is nuttig. | … dat de cursus nuttig is. |\n| Je hebt een diploma. | Als je een diploma hebt, … |\n\nAfter the conjunction comes the subject, then everything else, and the verb closes the clause.\n\n> Ik blijf thuis omdat ik ziek ben. — I am staying at home because I am ill.\n> Mijn baas zegt dat de cursus erg nuttig is. — My boss says that the course is very useful.\n\n### Two verbs at the end\n\nWith a modal verb or the perfect, both verbs go to the end. Both orders are right. The order with the conjugated verb first is always correct, so make that your default:\n\n> Ik doe deze opleiding omdat ik verpleegkundige wil worden. — I am doing this course because I want to become a nurse.\n> Ik weet dat hij rechten heeft gestudeerd. — I know that he studied law.\n\nYou will also hear and read `omdat ik verpleegkundige worden wil` and `dat hij rechten gestudeerd heeft`. Those are right too.\n\n### Separable verbs\n\nAt the end of a sub-clause a separable verb is written as one word again: `Hij belt me morgen op` becomes `… dat hij me morgen opbelt`.\n\n> Ik denk dat ik volgend jaar afstudeer. — I think I am graduating next year.\n\n### niet\n\n`niet` comes before the verbs at the end: `… omdat ik niet kan komen`, `… dat ik het niet weet`.\n\n### dat is never left out\n\nEnglish often drops \"that\": *I think it is a good job*. Dutch never does. After `zeggen`, `denken`, `weten`, `hopen`, `vinden` and `geloven` the sub-clause starts with `dat`:\n\n> Ik hoop dat ik voor het examen slaag. — I hope I pass the exam.\n\n### als: if, and when\n\n`als` means \"if\", and also \"when\" for the present and the future. For one moment in the past Dutch uses a different word, `toen` (unit B1 · 1).\n\n### The sub-clause first\n\nA sub-clause can also come first. Then the whole sub-clause is the first element of the sentence, so the main clause starts with its **verb**, and the subject follows it. A comma separates the two.\n\n> Als je dit diploma hebt, verdien je meer. — If you have this diploma, you earn more.\n> Omdat het salaris laag was, zocht ik een andere baan. — Because the salary was low, I looked for another job.\n\n### omdat or want\n\nThey mean the same. `omdat` sends the verb to the end; `want` keeps the normal order (unit A2 · 22). An omdat-clause can come first; a want-clause cannot.\n\n- `Ik stop, omdat het werk te zwaar is.`\n- `Ik stop, want het werk is te zwaar.`\n\nOne more word works like this: `of` meaning \"whether\" (`Ik weet niet of hij komt`). More about it in unit B1 · 2.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The verb at the end: `omdat ik ziek ben`, not *omdat ik ben ziek.\n- Do not drop dat: `Ik denk dat het goed is`, not *Ik denk het is goed.\n- After a sub-clause, the verb first: `Als het regent, blijf ik thuis`, not *Als het regent, ik blijf thuis.\n- A separable verb in one piece: `dat hij me opbelt`, not *dat hij me belt op.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian keeps `що` too (`Я думаю, що це добре`), so `dat` should feel natural. What is new is the verb at the end: Ukrainian has nothing like it, so check it every time you write `omdat`, `dat` or `als`.","words":[{"nl":"opleiding","art":"de","pl":"opleidingen","en":"course of training / education","pos":"n"},{"nl":"cursus","art":"de","pl":"cursussen","en":"course (classes)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"diploma","art":"het","pl":"diploma's","en":"diploma / certificate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vak","art":"het","pl":"vakken","en":"profession / school subject","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ervaring","art":"de","pl":"ervaringen","en":"experience","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stage","art":"de","pl":"stages","en":"internship / work placement","pos":"n"},{"nl":"salaris","art":"het","pl":"salarissen","en":"salary","pos":"n"},{"nl":"baan","art":"de","pl":"banen","en":"job","pos":"n"},{"nl":"studeren","past":"studeerde","perf":"heeft gestudeerd","en":"to study (at college or university)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"slagen","past":"slaagde","perf":"is geslaagd","en":"to pass (an exam: slagen voor)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"kiezen","past":"koos","perf":"heeft gekozen","en":"to choose","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verdienen","past":"verdiende","perf":"heeft verdiend","en":"to earn","pos":"v"},{"nl":"afstuderen","past":"studeerde af","perf":"is afgestudeerd","en":"to graduate","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"beslissen","past":"besliste","perf":"heeft beslist","en":"to decide","pos":"v"},{"nl":"hopen","past":"hoopte","perf":"heeft gehoopt","en":"to hope","pos":"v"},{"nl":"nuttig","en":"useful","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik doe deze opleiding omdat ik verpleegkundige wil worden.","en":"I am doing this course because I want to become a nurse.","hint":["doen","opleiding","verpleegkundige","willen","worden"],"note":"After omdat both verbs go to the end, conjugated verb first: wil worden. Worden wil is also right.","alt":["Ik doe deze opleiding omdat ik verpleegkundige worden wil.","Ik doe deze opleiding, want ik wil verpleegkundige worden."]},{"nl":"Mijn baas zegt dat de cursus erg nuttig is.","en":"My boss says that the course is very useful.","hint":["baas","zeggen","cursus","erg","nuttig"],"note":"In the dat-clause the verb is goes to the very end, after nuttig.","alt":["Mijn baas zegt dat de cursus heel nuttig is."]},{"nl":"Ik denk dat ik volgend jaar afstudeer.","en":"I think I am graduating next year.","hint":["denken","volgend jaar","afstuderen"],"note":"English can drop 'that', Dutch cannot. At the end of the sub-clause the separable verb is one word again: afstudeer.","alt":["Ik denk dat ik volgend jaar ga afstuderen.","Ik denk dat ik volgend jaar afstuderen ga."]},{"nl":"Als je dit diploma hebt, verdien je meer.","en":"If you have this diploma, you earn more.","hint":["als","diploma","hebben","verdienen","meer"],"note":"The sub-clause is the first element, so the main clause starts with the verb: verdien je. Je after the verb loses the t.","alt":["Je verdient meer als je dit diploma hebt."]},{"nl":"Hij koos dit vak omdat hij graag met mensen werkt.","en":"He chose this profession because he likes working with people.","hint":["kiezen","vak","graag","mens","werken"],"note":"In the omdat-clause the verb werkt closes the clause. With want it would be: want hij werkt graag met mensen.","alt":["Hij heeft dit vak gekozen omdat hij graag met mensen werkt.","Hij koos dit vak, want hij werkt graag met mensen.","Hij heeft dit vak gekozen, want hij werkt graag met mensen."]},{"nl":"Ik hoop dat ik voor het examen slaag.","en":"I hope that I pass the exam.","hint":["hopen","examen","slagen"],"note":"After hopen comes dat, and the verb slaag goes to the end.","alt":["Ik hoop dat ik slaag voor het examen."]},{"nl":"Ik weet dat hij rechten heeft gestudeerd.","en":"I know that he studied law.","hint":["weten","rechten","studeren"],"note":"In the sub-clause the auxiliary and the participle both go to the end: heeft gestudeerd, or gestudeerd heeft.","alt":["Ik weet dat hij rechten gestudeerd heeft.","Ik weet dat hij rechten studeerde."]},{"nl":"Omdat het salaris laag was, zocht ik een andere baan.","en":"Because the salary was low, I looked for another job.","hint":["omdat","salaris","laag","zoeken","baan"],"note":"The omdat-clause comes first and counts as one element, so the main clause starts with its verb: zocht ik.","alt":["Omdat het salaris laag was, heb ik een andere baan gezocht.","Ik zocht een andere baan omdat het salaris laag was.","Ik heb een andere baan gezocht omdat het salaris laag was.","Ik zocht een andere baan, want het salaris was laag.","Ik heb een andere baan gezocht, want het salaris was laag."]},{"nl":"Ze zegt dat ze morgen niet naar de cursus kan komen.","en":"She says that she cannot come to the course tomorrow.","hint":["zeggen","morgen","cursus","kunnen","komen"],"note":"niet stands before the verbs at the end of the sub-clause: niet … kan komen.","alt":["Ze zegt dat zij morgen niet naar de cursus kan komen.","Ze zegt dat ze morgen niet naar de cursus komen kan.","Ze zegt dat zij morgen niet naar de cursus komen kan."]},{"nl":"Als ik klaar ben met mijn studie, wil ik in een ziekenhuis werken.","en":"When I have finished my studies, I want to work in a hospital.","hint":["als","klaar","studie","willen","ziekenhuis","werken"],"note":"als also means 'when' for the future. After the sub-clause the main clause starts with its verb: wil ik.","alt":["Als ik met mijn studie klaar ben, wil ik in een ziekenhuis werken.","Ik wil in een ziekenhuis werken als ik klaar ben met mijn studie.","Ik wil in een ziekenhuis werken als ik met mijn studie klaar ben."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik doe een stage, omdat ik ervaring ___.","options":["heb nodig","nodig heb","nodig hebben"],"correct":1,"en":"I am doing an internship because I need experience.","note":"In an omdat-clause the conjugated verb heb goes to the end, after nodig. Heb nodig is main-clause order."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn docent zegt ___ ik goed Nederlands spreek.","options":["–","of","dat"],"correct":2,"en":"My teacher says that I speak Dutch well.","note":"After zeggen the sub-clause starts with dat, and Dutch never leaves it out. Of means whether, which does not fit after zegt here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Als je dit diploma hebt, ___ meer.","options":["je verdient","verdien je","je verdien"],"correct":1,"en":"If you have this diploma, you earn more.","note":"The als-clause is the first element, so the verb comes next and then the subject. Je after the verb loses its t: verdien je."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik denk dat de school me morgen ___.","options":["belt op","opbelt","op belt"],"correct":1,"en":"I think that the school will call me tomorrow.","note":"At the end of a sub-clause a separable verb is one word again: opbelt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ze zegt dat ze de cursus heel nuttig ___ (vinden).","answers":["vindt"],"en":"She says that she finds the course very useful.","note":"The verb goes to the end of the dat-clause, and it still agrees with its subject: ze vindt, with d + t."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik hoop dat ik volgende maand ___ (afstuderen).","answers":["afstudeer"],"en":"I hope that I graduate next month.","note":"In a sub-clause the separable verb comes together at the end: afstudeer, one word, with the ik-form."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik kies deze opleiding ___ (because) ik graag met kinderen werk.","answers":["omdat","aangezien"],"en":"I am choosing this course because I like working with children.","note":"The verb werk stands at the end, so this is a sub-clause: omdat. Want would need the normal order: want ik werk graag met kinderen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik blijf vanavond thuis","words":["omdat","ik","moet","leren","voor mijn examen"],"answers":["Ik blijf vanavond thuis omdat ik voor mijn examen moet leren.","Ik blijf vanavond thuis omdat ik voor mijn examen leren moet.","Ik blijf vanavond thuis omdat ik moet leren voor mijn examen."],"en":"I am staying at home this evening because I have to study for my exam.","note":"Both verbs go to the end of the omdat-clause, best with the conjugated verb first: moet leren. A phrase like voor mijn examen may also follow the verbs."},{"type":"order","lead":"Als","words":["ik","de baan","krijg","verhuis","ik","naar Utrecht"],"answers":["Als ik de baan krijg, verhuis ik naar Utrecht."],"en":"If I get the job, I will move to Utrecht.","note":"In the als-clause krijg goes to the end. Then the main clause starts with its verb, verhuis, and ik follows."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ze vertelt","words":["dat","ze","haar diploma","heeft","gehaald"],"answers":["Ze vertelt dat ze haar diploma heeft gehaald.","Ze vertelt dat ze haar diploma gehaald heeft."],"en":"She says that she has got her diploma.","note":"In the perfect both verbs go to the end of the dat-clause. Heeft gehaald and gehaald heeft are both right."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with omdat.","from":"Ik wil een andere baan. Het salaris is te laag.","answers":["Ik wil een andere baan omdat het salaris te laag is.","Omdat het salaris te laag is, wil ik een andere baan."],"en":"I want another job because the salary is too low.","note":"omdat makes the second sentence a sub-clause, so is moves to the end: omdat het salaris te laag is."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with the sub-clause.","from":"Ik ga studeren als ik genoeg geld heb.","answers":["Als ik genoeg geld heb, ga ik studeren."],"en":"If I have enough money, I will go and study.","note":"The als-clause becomes the first element, so the main clause starts with its verb: ga ik studeren."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Ik denk dat …","from":"Ze heeft nog niet beslist.","answers":["Ik denk dat ze nog niet heeft beslist.","Ik denk dat zij nog niet heeft beslist.","Ik denk dat ze nog niet beslist heeft.","Ik denk dat zij nog niet beslist heeft."],"en":"I think she has not decided yet.","note":"In the dat-clause heeft moves to the end, next to beslist; nog niet stays in front of the verbs."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik denk het examen moeilijk is.","answers":["Ik denk dat het examen moeilijk is."],"en":"I think the exam is difficult.","note":"English can leave out 'that', Dutch cannot: Ik denk dat … The verb at the end only makes sense after dat."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik doe deze opleiding omdat het is een goed vak.","answers":["Ik doe deze opleiding omdat het een goed vak is.","Ik doe deze opleiding, want het is een goed vak."],"en":"I am doing this course because it is a good profession.","note":"After omdat the verb goes to the end: omdat het een goed vak is. If you keep the normal order, the word must be want."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het is jammer dat ik kan niet komen.","answers":["Het is jammer dat ik niet kan komen.","Het is jammer dat ik niet komen kan."],"en":"It is a pity that I cannot come.","note":"In a sub-clause the verbs go to the end, and niet stands in front of them: dat ik niet kan komen."}]},
  {"id":"a2-24","level":"A2","icon":"🧑‍🍳","title":"In the kitchen","grammar":"The imperative and polite requests","summary":"The imperative is the stem: Wacht even. Snijd de ui. One form for one person or more; zijn becomes wees. With u: stem + t + u (Komt u binnen). Ask with Kun je …? or Wil je …? Recipes and signs use the infinitive.","explain":"The imperative (*de gebiedende wijs*) is the form for telling someone what to do: an order, an instruction, advice or an invitation. In the kitchen you hear it all the time: `Roer de saus`, `Pak een kom`, `Kom maar eten`. Dutch also has softer ways to ask, and recipes often use the infinitive instead.\n\n### The form: the stem\n\nThe imperative is simply the **stem** of the verb, the same form as `ik` in the present tense (except for `zijn`, see below), with the same spelling rules (unit A2 · 01). There is no subject.\n\n| infinitive | imperative |\n| wachten | wacht |\n| bakken | bak |\n| lezen | lees |\n| snijden | snijd |\n| komen | kom |\n\n> Wacht even, de pan is nog niet heet. — Wait a moment, the pan is not hot yet.\n> Lees eerst het hele recept. — Read the whole recipe first.\n> Snijd de ui in ringen. — Cut the onion into rings.\n\nThe form is the same for one person and for several: `Kom binnen!` to one friend, and `Kom binnen, jongens!` to a group.\n\nTwo verbs to learn by heart: `zijn` becomes `wees`, and `hebben` becomes `heb`.\n\n> Wees voorzichtig met het mes. — Be careful with the knife.\n> Heb even geduld. — Be patient for a moment.\n\n### The formal form with u\n\nTo someone you say `u` to, use the u-form of the present tense and put `u` after it: stem + t + u. A stem that already ends in t gets no second t.\n\n> Komt u binnen. — Come in. (formal)\n> Gaat u zitten. — Sit down. (formal)\n> Wacht u even. — Wait a moment. (formal)\n\n### Word order\n\nThe imperative stands **first**. The rest of the sentence follows in the normal order:\n\n- A **separable verb** splits: the prefix goes to the end. `Zet de oven aan`, `Bel me op`.\n- A short pronoun comes straight after the verb: `Geef me het mes`.\n- **niet** goes after the object, before the prefix or the place: `Zet de oven niet aan`, `Doe de pan niet in de vaatwasser`. With a noun that has `een` or no article you use **geen**: `Doe geen suiker in mijn thee`.\n\n### Making it friendly: even, maar, eens\n\nA bare imperative can sound bossy. Dutch speakers add a small word:\n\n- `even`: only for a moment, it is no big deal. `Wacht even.`\n- `maar`: go ahead, you may. `Kom maar binnen.`\n- `eens`: just try, have a look. `Kijk eens.`\n\nThey stand after the verb and a short pronoun, before or after the object: `Geef me even het mes` and `Geef me het mes even` are both fine.\n\n### Asking instead of ordering\n\nMost polite of all is a question with a modal verb. The infinitive goes to the end.\n\n> Kun je de oven aanzetten? — Can you switch on the oven?\n> Wilt u even wachten? — Would you wait a moment, please? (formal)\n> Mag ik het zout? — May I have the salt?\n\n`Kan je` is just as correct; `Kun je` is a little more formal and more usual in writing. For \"let's\" Dutch says `Laten we` + infinitive: `Laten we beginnen` (more about `laten` in B1).\n\n### The infinitive in recipes and on signs\n\nRecipes, manuals and signs often give the infinitive instead of the imperative. Everything else comes first and the infinitive goes **last**, in one piece; often there is no article.\n\n> De appels schillen en in stukken snijden. — Peel the apples and cut them into pieces.\n> Niet roken. — No smoking.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **No t.** *Snijdt de ui is wrong: the stem is `snijd`, written with d and pronounced as a t. `Snijd de ui.` (A form with -t for several people, as in `Komt allen`, is old-fashioned and survives only in old texts and songs.)\n- **zijn is wees**: *Ben voorzichtig → `Wees voorzichtig`.\n- **The prefix goes last**: *Opbel me → `Bel me op`.\n- **niet does not come first**: *Niet raak de pan aan → `Raak de pan niet aan`. Only the infinitive takes `niet` in front: `Niet aanraken`.\n- **With u the t comes back**: *Kom u binnen → `Komt u binnen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has two forms, `сідай` and `сідайте`. Dutch has one form for everyone you say `je` or `jullie` to, `Ga zitten`, and only `u` has its own form: `Gaat u zitten`.","words":[{"nl":"pan","art":"de","pl":"pannen","en":"pan","pos":"n"},{"nl":"mes","art":"het","pl":"messen","en":"knife","pos":"n"},{"nl":"snijplank","art":"de","pl":"snijplanken","en":"chopping board","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kom","art":"de","pl":"kommen","en":"bowl","pos":"n"},{"nl":"oven","art":"de","pl":"ovens","en":"oven","pos":"n"},{"nl":"theelepel","art":"de","pl":"theelepels","en":"teaspoon","pos":"n"},{"nl":"snijden","past":"sneed","perf":"heeft gesneden","en":"to cut","pos":"v"},{"nl":"schillen","past":"schilde","perf":"heeft geschild","en":"to peel","pos":"v"},{"nl":"roeren","past":"roerde","perf":"heeft geroerd","en":"to stir","pos":"v"},{"nl":"bakken","past":"bakte","perf":"heeft gebakken","en":"to fry / to bake","pos":"v"},{"nl":"toevoegen","past":"voegde toe","perf":"heeft toegevoegd","en":"to add","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"afwassen","past":"waste af","perf":"heeft afgewassen","en":"to do the dishes / to wash up","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"aanzetten","past":"zette aan","perf":"heeft aangezet","en":"to switch on","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"voorzichtig","en":"careful","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"gaar","en":"done / cooked (through)","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Wees voorzichtig met dat mes.","en":"Be careful with that knife.","hint":["zijn","voorzichtig","mes"],"note":"The imperative of zijn is wees, a form of its own. Not ben, although ik ben is the ik-form."},{"nl":"Zet eerst de oven aan.","en":"Switch the oven on first.","hint":["eerst","oven","aanzetten"],"note":"aanzetten is separable: the imperative zet stands first and the prefix aan goes to the end.","alt":["Zet de oven eerst aan."]},{"nl":"Pak maar een grote kom uit de kast.","en":"Go ahead and take a big bowl from the cupboard.","hint":["pakken","maar","groot","kom","kast"],"note":"pakken → stem pak, one k. Maar makes it an invitation: go ahead, you may."},{"nl":"Doe de pan niet in de vaatwasser.","en":"Don't put the pan in the dishwasher.","hint":["doen","pan","niet","vaatwasser"],"note":"The imperative doe stands first. Niet comes after the object de pan and before the place in de vaatwasser."},{"nl":"Voeg geen zout toe aan de soep.","en":"Don't add any salt to the soup.","hint":["toevoegen","zout","soep"],"note":"Zout has no article here, so the negative is geen, not niet. Toevoegen splits: voeg … toe.","alt":["Voeg geen zout aan de soep toe."]},{"nl":"Gaat u zitten, het eten is bijna klaar.","en":"Sit down (formal), the food is almost ready.","hint":["gaan","zitten","eten","bijna","klaar"],"note":"The formal imperative is the u-form of the verb with u after it: gaat u. Zitten is an infinitive and goes to the end.","alt":["Gaat u maar zitten, het eten is bijna klaar."]},{"nl":"Kun je even de pannen afwassen?","en":"Can you quickly wash the pans?","hint":["kunnen","even","pan","afwassen"],"note":"A question with kunnen is friendlier than an order. Kun je: jij loses its t after the verb, and the infinitive afwassen stays in one piece at the end.","alt":["Kun je de pannen even afwassen?","Kun jij even de pannen afwassen?","Kun jij de pannen even afwassen?","Kan je even de pannen afwassen?","Kan je de pannen even afwassen?","Kan jij even de pannen afwassen?","Kan jij de pannen even afwassen?"]},{"nl":"Bel me even op als het eten klaar is.","en":"Just give me a call when the food is ready.","hint":["opbellen","even","eten","klaar"],"note":"opbellen splits in the imperative: bel … op. The pronoun me comes straight after bel, and even after the pronoun.","alt":["Bel mij even op als het eten klaar is.","Bel me even als het eten klaar is.","Bel mij even als het eten klaar is."]},{"nl":"Twee theelepels suiker toevoegen en goed roeren.","en":"Add two teaspoons of sugar and stir well. (recipe style, with the infinitive)","hint":["twee","theelepel","suiker","toevoegen","goed","roeren"],"note":"A recipe can use the infinitive instead of the imperative. The infinitive goes last, and toevoegen stays in one piece."},{"nl":"Laten we eerst de appels schillen.","en":"Let's peel the apples first.","hint":["laten","eerst","appel","schillen"],"note":"Laten we + infinitive is Dutch for 'let's'. Laten stands first, we follows, and schillen goes to the end.","alt":["Laten we de appels eerst schillen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de ui op de snijplank, niet op tafel.","options":["Snijt","Snijd","Snijden"],"correct":1,"en":"Cut the onion on the chopping board, not on the table.","note":"The imperative is the stem: snijden → snijd. It sounds like a t, but it is written with d, as in ik snijd."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ voorzichtig, de pan is heet!","options":["Ben","Bent","Wees","Zijn"],"correct":2,"en":"Be careful, the pan is hot!","note":"zijn has its own imperative: wees. Ben and bent are present-tense forms and cannot give an order."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Doe ___ suiker in mijn thee, alsjeblieft.","options":["niet","geen","niets"],"correct":1,"en":"Don't put any sugar in my tea, please.","note":"Suiker stands without de or het, so the negative is geen. Niet is for a verb or for a noun with de, het or a possessive."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ u binnen, de koffie is klaar.","options":["Kom","Komt","Komen"],"correct":1,"en":"Come in (formal), the coffee is ready.","note":"With u the imperative is the u-form of the present tense: u komt, so Komt u. The bare stem kom is for je and jullie."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (hebben) even geduld, de taart is bijna gaar.","answers":["Heb"],"en":"Be patient for a moment, the cake is almost done.","note":"The imperative of hebben is heb, the same as the ik-form. Not hebt or heeft."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (bakken) de uien vijf minuten in een beetje boter.","answers":["Bak"],"en":"Fry the onions for five minutes in a little butter.","note":"The imperative is the stem, and a stem never ends in a double consonant: bakken → bak."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (lezen) eerst het hele recept.","answers":["Lees"],"en":"Read the whole recipe first.","note":"lezen → stem lees: the long e is written double, and z becomes s at the end of a word."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zet","words":["de oven","nog","niet","aan"],"answers":["Zet de oven nog niet aan."],"en":"Don't switch the oven on yet.","note":"The imperative stands first and the prefix aan goes to the end. Nog niet comes after the object, before the prefix."},{"type":"order","lead":"Kun","words":["je","even","de kom","pakken"],"answers":["Kun je even de kom pakken?","Kun je de kom even pakken?"],"en":"Can you just get the bowl?","note":"A polite question: Kun je first, the infinitive pakken last. Even may stand before or after de kom."},{"type":"order","lead":"De appels","words":["schillen","en","in stukken","snijden"],"answers":["De appels schillen en in stukken snijden."],"en":"Peel the apples and cut them into pieces. (recipe style)","note":"In a recipe the infinitive comes last in each part: de appels schillen, then in stukken snijden."},{"type":"order","lead":"Laten","words":["we","samen","de pannen","afwassen"],"answers":["Laten we samen de pannen afwassen.","Laten we de pannen samen afwassen."],"en":"Let's wash the pans together.","note":"Laten we + infinitive for 'let's'. The infinitive afwassen goes last; samen may stand before or after de pannen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it formal, with u.","from":"Wacht even, de soep is nog niet klaar.","answers":["Wacht u even, de soep is nog niet klaar."],"en":"Wait a moment (formal), the soup is not ready yet.","note":"The formal form is stem + t + u. The stem wacht already ends in t, so it stays wacht: Wacht u even."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn the order into a polite question with Kunt u.","from":"Stuur me het recept.","answers":["Kunt u me het recept sturen?","Kunt u mij het recept sturen?"],"en":"Could you send me the recipe? (formal)","note":"Kunt u stands first, and sturen moves to the end as an infinitive. Me stays right after the subject u."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Write it as a recipe would: with the infinitive instead of the imperative.","from":"Bak de uien in de pan.","answers":["De uien in de pan bakken.","De uien bakken in de pan."],"en":"Fry the onions in the pan.","note":"With the infinitive, the object comes first and the verb goes to the end: de uien in de pan bakken."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Opbel me als het eten klaar is.","answers":["Bel me op als het eten klaar is.","Bel mij op als het eten klaar is.","Bel me als het eten klaar is."],"en":"Call me when the food is ready.","note":"opbellen is separable. In the imperative it splits: bel stands first, op goes to the end of the main clause."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Niet raak de pan aan, hij is heet.","answers":["Raak de pan niet aan, hij is heet."],"en":"Don't touch the pan, it is hot.","note":"The imperative always stands first; niet comes later, before the prefix aan. Niet at the start only goes with an infinitive: Niet aanraken."}]},
  {"id":"a2-25","level":"A2","icon":"🙂","title":"How do you feel?","grammar":"Reflexive verbs","summary":"A reflexive verb has a pronoun that points back to the subject: ik voel me, jij voelt je, hij voelt zich, wij voelen ons. It stands after the verb, or after the subject when that follows the verb. Perfect: with hebben.","explain":"A reflexive verb (*een wederkerend werkwoord*) has an extra pronoun that refers back to the subject: `Ik vergis me` (I am mistaken), `Hij voelt zich goed` (he feels well). The pronoun does not add a person; it is the subject itself. Many verbs about feelings and daily care work this way.\n\n### The reflexive pronouns\n\n| | zich voelen |\n| ik | voel me |\n| jij / je | voelt je |\n| u | voelt zich / voelt u |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | voelt zich |\n| wij / we | voelen ons |\n| jullie | voelen je |\n| zij / ze | voelen zich |\n\n- Only `zich` is a new word. The others look like object pronouns, but with **jij** and **jullie** it is always `je`, never jou or jullie: `Jij voelt je`, `Jullie voelen je`.\n- With `ik` you will also read `mij` (`Ik voel mij goed`); in speech `me` is normal.\n- With `u` both `zich` and `u` are correct: `U vergist zich` or `U vergist u`. `zich` is more usual.\n\n> Ik voel me vandaag niet zo goed. — I don't feel so well today.\n> Mijn oma voelt zich soms eenzaam. — My grandmother sometimes feels lonely.\n> Wij verheugen ons op het weekend. — We are looking forward to the weekend.\n\n### Always reflexive\n\nSome verbs always have the pronoun when they have the meaning given here. Learn them with `zich`:\n\n`zich vergissen` (to be mistaken), `zich schamen` (to be ashamed), `zich haasten` (to hurry), `zich herinneren` (to remember), `zich vervelen` (to be bored), `zich zorgen maken` (to worry), `zich verheugen op` (to look forward to), `zich ergeren aan` (to be annoyed by), `zich afvragen` (to wonder), `zich voorstellen` (to introduce yourself, to imagine).\n\n`zich voelen` belongs here when it is about how you feel: `Ik voel me moe`. Without the pronoun, `voelen` means to touch or notice something: `Ik voel de wind`.\n\n### Sometimes reflexive\n\nOther verbs can have an ordinary object or the reflexive pronoun. Use the pronoun when the action goes back to the subject: `wassen`, `aankleden`, `scheren`, `omdraaien`, `verstoppen`.\n\n> Ik was de baby. Daarna was ik me. — I wash the baby. Then I wash myself.\n> Hij kleedt zich snel aan. — He gets dressed quickly.\n\n`Hij wast hem` means that he washes someone else. For himself it must be `zich`.\n\n### Word order\n\nThe pronoun stands **directly after the conjugated verb**. When the subject comes after the verb, the pronoun comes after the subject. In a sub-clause it follows the subject.\n\n> Morgen voel je je vast beter. — You will surely feel better tomorrow.\n> Ik denk dat ze zich verveelt op school. — I think she is bored at school.\n\nWith a modal verb or in the perfect, the pronoun stays in the middle and the other verb goes to the end. The perfect of a reflexive verb is made with `hebben` (for every verb in this unit; a rare exception like `Ik ben me doodgeschrokken` comes much later):\n\n> Jullie moeten je haasten. — You have to hurry.\n> Ik heb me vergist in de datum. — I made a mistake about the date.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **Do not drop the pronoun**: *Ik herinner die dag → `Ik herinner me die dag`.\n- **jij and jullie take je**: *Jij voelt jou niet goed → `Jij voelt je niet goed`.\n- **Not hem or haar for himself or herself**: *Mijn vader scheert hem → `Mijn vader scheert zich`.\n- **hebben, not zijn**: *Ik ben me vergist → `Ik heb me vergist`.\n- **Pronoun after the subject** in inversion: *Morgen voel me ik beter → `Morgen voel ik me beter`.\n\n`elkaar` means \"each other\" and is not the same as `zich`: `Ze zien elkaar morgen` (they see each other), `Ze wassen zich` (they wash themselves).\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian -ся and Dutch `zich` often do not match. These Ukrainian verbs are **not** reflexive in Dutch: сміятися = `lachen`, вчитися = `leren`, повертатися = `terugkomen`, сподіватися = `hopen`. And the other way round: пам'ятати has no -ся, but Dutch says `zich herinneren`. Learn every Dutch verb with or without `zich`, and do not translate the -ся.","words":[{"nl":"zich voelen","past":"voelde me","perf":"heeft zich gevoeld","en":"to feel (well, tired …)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich vergissen","past":"vergiste me","perf":"heeft zich vergist","en":"to be mistaken / to make a mistake","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich schamen","past":"schaamde me","perf":"heeft zich geschaamd","en":"to be ashamed","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich haasten","past":"haastte me","perf":"heeft zich gehaast","en":"to hurry","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich herinneren","past":"herinnerde me","perf":"heeft zich herinnerd","en":"to remember","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich vervelen","past":"verveelde me","perf":"heeft zich verveeld","en":"to be bored","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich zorgen maken","past":"maakte me zorgen","perf":"heeft zich zorgen gemaakt","en":"to worry","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich ergeren aan","past":"ergerde me","perf":"heeft zich geërgerd","en":"to be annoyed by","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich verheugen op","past":"verheugde me","perf":"heeft zich verheugd","en":"to look forward to","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich ontspannen","past":"ontspande me","perf":"heeft zich ontspannen","en":"to relax","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich aankleden","past":"kleedde me aan","perf":"heeft zich aangekleed","en":"to get dressed","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"zich scheren","past":"schoor me","perf":"heeft zich geschoren","en":"to shave","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich afvragen","past":"vroeg me af","perf":"heeft zich afgevraagd","en":"to wonder","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"zich omdraaien","past":"draaide me om","perf":"heeft zich omgedraaid","en":"to turn round","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"zenuwachtig","en":"nervous","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"eenzaam","en":"lonely","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik voel me vandaag niet zo goed.","en":"I don't feel so well today.","hint":["zich voelen","vandaag","niet","goed"],"note":"With ik the reflexive pronoun is me. It stands directly after the verb voel.","alt":["Vandaag voel ik me niet zo goed.","Ik voel mij vandaag niet zo goed.","Vandaag voel ik mij niet zo goed."]},{"nl":"Mijn oma voelt zich soms eenzaam.","en":"My grandmother sometimes feels lonely.","hint":["oma","zich voelen","soms","eenzaam"],"note":"Mijn oma is a hij/zij-subject, so the pronoun is zich: the only reflexive pronoun that is a word of its own.","alt":["Soms voelt mijn oma zich eenzaam."]},{"nl":"Morgen voel je je vast beter.","en":"You will surely feel better tomorrow.","hint":["morgen","zich voelen","vast","beter"],"note":"The first je is the subject, the second the reflexive pronoun. Morgen comes first, so the subject follows the verb and the pronoun follows the subject.","alt":["Je voelt je morgen vast beter.","Morgen voel jij je vast beter.","Jij voelt je morgen vast beter."]},{"nl":"Mijn broer ergert zich aan het lawaai van de buren.","en":"My brother is annoyed by the neighbours' noise.","hint":["broer","zich ergeren aan","lawaai","buren"],"note":"zich ergeren aan (to be annoyed by) has both the pronoun and the preposition aan. Zich stands right after ergert."},{"nl":"Maak je geen zorgen, het komt goed.","en":"Don't worry, it will be all right.","hint":["zich zorgen maken","geen","goed"],"note":"An imperative of a reflexive verb: maak + the pronoun je for the person you speak to. Zorgen has no article, so the negative is geen.","alt":["Maak je geen zorgen, het komt wel goed."]},{"nl":"Herinnert u zich die dag nog?","en":"Do you (formal) still remember that day?","hint":["zich herinneren","dag","nog"],"note":"In the meaning 'to remember', herinneren always has the pronoun, unlike Ukrainian пам'ятати. With u the pronoun is zich (u is also correct).","alt":["Herinnert u u die dag nog?"]},{"nl":"Ik heb me vergist in de datum.","en":"I made a mistake about the date.","hint":["zich vergissen","datum"],"note":"The perfect of a reflexive verb is made with hebben. The pronoun me comes straight after heb, and the participle vergist goes to the end.","alt":["Ik heb me in de datum vergist.","Ik heb mij vergist in de datum.","Ik heb mij in de datum vergist."]},{"nl":"Ik denk dat ze zich verveelt op school.","en":"I think she is bored at school.","hint":["denken","zich vervelen","school"],"note":"In a sub-clause with dat the verb goes to the end, and the pronoun zich stays directly after the subject ze.","alt":["Ik denk dat zij zich verveelt op school.","Ik denk dat ze zich op school verveelt.","Ik denk dat zij zich op school verveelt."]},{"nl":"Jullie moeten je haasten, de trein vertrekt zo.","en":"You (plural) have to hurry, the train is leaving in a moment.","hint":["moeten","zich haasten","trein","vertrekken","zo"],"note":"With jullie the reflexive pronoun is je, not jullie. With a modal verb the pronoun stays after moeten and the infinitive haasten goes last."},{"nl":"Hij wast zich en kleedt zich snel aan.","en":"He has a wash and gets dressed quickly.","hint":["zich wassen","zich aankleden","snel"],"note":"wassen and aankleden can also take another object (hij wast de auto). Here the action goes back to him, so zich, never hem.","alt":["Hij wast zich en hij kleedt zich snel aan."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik voel ___ vandaag heel goed.","options":["me","zich","mijn","ik"],"correct":0,"en":"I feel very well today.","note":"The reflexive pronoun for ik is me (in writing also mij). Zich is only for hij, zij, het, u and plural zij."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn vader scheert ___ elke ochtend.","options":["hem","zich","zijn"],"correct":1,"en":"My father shaves every morning.","note":"He shaves himself, so the pronoun points back to the subject: zich. Hem would be another man."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wij verheugen ___ op het weekend.","options":["zich","onze","ons"],"correct":2,"en":"We are looking forward to the weekend.","note":"With wij the reflexive pronoun is ons. Onze is a possessive (our) and needs a noun after it."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ dat het morgen mooi weer wordt.","options":["hoop me","hoop","hoop mij"],"correct":1,"en":"I hope the weather will be nice tomorrow.","note":"hopen is not reflexive in Dutch, although Ukrainian says сподіватися with -ся. Do not translate the -ся."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ me in het weekend goed ontspannen.","options":["ben","heb","word"],"correct":1,"en":"I relaxed well at the weekend.","note":"Reflexive verbs form the perfect with hebben: ik heb me ontspannen. Ontspannen is also the participle."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Jij voelt ___ vandaag niet lekker, hè?","answers":["je"],"en":"You are not feeling well today, are you?","note":"The reflexive pronoun for jij is je. Jou is never used as a reflexive pronoun."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Jullie moeten ___ niet schamen.","answers":["je"],"en":"You (plural) should not be ashamed.","note":"The reflexive pronoun for jullie is je, the same as for jij. Not jullie."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Kunt u ___ dat nog herinneren?","answers":["zich","u"],"en":"Can you (formal) still remember that?","note":"With u the reflexive pronoun is zich or u; both are correct, zich is more usual."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Draai ___ even om, ik wil de achterkant van je jas zien.","answers":["je"],"en":"Turn round for a moment, I want to see the back of your coat.","note":"An imperative to someone you say jij to: draai + je. The prefix om goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Voor een examen","words":["voel","ik","me","altijd","zenuwachtig"],"answers":["Voor een examen voel ik me altijd zenuwachtig."],"en":"Before an exam I always feel nervous.","note":"Voor een examen takes the first place, so the verb comes next and then the subject. The pronoun me follows the subject ik."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik denk","words":["dat","hij","zich","vergist"],"answers":["Ik denk dat hij zich vergist."],"en":"I think he is mistaken.","note":"In the sub-clause the verb vergist goes to the end; zich stays directly after the subject hij."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["heb","ik","me","erg","geërgerd","aan de buren"],"answers":["Gisteren heb ik me erg geërgerd aan de buren.","Gisteren heb ik me erg aan de buren geërgerd."],"en":"Yesterday I was very annoyed by the neighbours.","note":"After Gisteren comes heb, then ik, then me. The phrase with aan may stand before or after the participle."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Ik vergis me.","answers":["Ik heb me vergist.","Ik heb mij vergist."],"en":"I made a mistake.","note":"The perfect of a reflexive verb is made with hebben. The pronoun stays after the conjugated verb heb."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"The action now goes back to the subject: replace de kinderen by the reflexive pronoun.","from":"Ik kleed de kinderen aan.","answers":["Ik kleed me aan.","Ik kleed mij aan."],"en":"I get dressed.","note":"aankleden can take an ordinary object or a reflexive pronoun. For ik the pronoun is me, and aan still goes to the end."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Dat heb ik ook afgevraagd.","answers":["Dat heb ik me ook afgevraagd.","Dat heb ik mij ook afgevraagd."],"en":"I wondered about that too.","note":"zich afvragen always needs the pronoun, also in the perfect with hebben. Dat comes first, so ik follows heb and me follows ik."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn zoon leert zich Nederlands op school.","answers":["Mijn zoon leert Nederlands op school.","Mijn zoon leert op school Nederlands."],"en":"My son is learning Dutch at school.","note":"leren is not reflexive, even though Ukrainian says вчитися. The pronoun zich has to go."}]},
  {"id":"a2-26","level":"A2","icon":"🏘️","title":"My neighbourhood","grammar":"Er: there is, there are","summary":"Er is + singular, er zijn + plural, for something not yet known (een, geen, veel): Er is een bakker in mijn straat. A known subject (de, het) has no er. Er also means 'there' for a place already named: Ik woon er al jaren.","explain":"To say that something exists or is somewhere, Dutch uses `er`: `Er is een bakker in mijn straat` (there is a baker's in my street). This `er` is a small word without stress, and the sentence sounds wrong or unfinished without it. `er` has a second job too: it can mean \"there\" for a place you have already mentioned.\n\n### er is, er zijn\n\nThe verb agrees with the noun that comes **after** it, not with `er`:\n\n| form | used with |\n| er is | + one thing: een bakker, veel verkeer |\n| er zijn | + more things: veel winkels, twee scholen |\n| is er …? | the yes/no question |\n| er is geen … | the negative |\n\n> Er is een bakker in mijn straat. — There is a baker's in my street.\n> Er zijn veel gezellige cafés in deze wijk. — There are lots of nice cafés in this district.\n> Is er een bibliotheek in de buurt? — Is there a library nearby?\n> Er is geen parkeerplaats voor de deur. — There is no parking space in front of the door.\n\nUse it with an **indefinite** subject: something new for the listener, with `een`, `geen`, `veel`, `weinig`, no article, or `iemand`, `iets`, `niemand`. Notice `Zijn er nog vragen?` (are there any more questions?) and `Er is niemand thuis` (nobody is at home).\n\n### Other verbs with er\n\nThe same `er` goes with other verbs, as long as the subject is indefinite. Dutch often uses a verb of position (`staan`, `liggen`, `zitten`) or `wonen`, `komen` where English just says \"there is\":\n\n> Er woont een kapper naast ons. — A hairdresser lives next door to us.\n> Er staat een glasbak naast de supermarkt. — There is a bottle bank next to the supermarket.\n> Volgend jaar komt er een nieuw zwembad. — A new swimming pool is coming next year.\n\n### A known subject: no er\n\nWhen the listener already knows which one you mean (`de`, `het`, `mijn`, a name), there is no `er`. The subject simply comes first:\n\n> De slager is op de hoek. — The butcher's is on the corner.\n\nSo *Er is de slager op de hoek is wrong.\n\n### Word order\n\n- `er` stands first, or directly after the conjugated verb: `Morgen is er een markt`, `Is er een kapper?` The indefinite subject follows.\n- When a **place** comes first, `er` may be left out: `In mijn straat is een bakker` and `In mijn straat is er een bakker` are both correct. After a time word like `morgen` or `volgend jaar`, keep it: without `er` such a sentence usually sounds wrong.\n\n### er = there, for a place already named\n\nWhen a place has just been mentioned, `er` refers back to it, like an unstressed \"there\". It stands after the conjugated verb, and after the subject when the subject follows the verb.\n\n> Ken je het buurthuis? Ik ben er elke week. — Do you know the community centre? I'm there every week.\n> Woon je er al lang? — Have you lived there long?\n\nWhen \"there\" is stressed, or stands at the start, use `daar`. `er` can never start a sentence in this meaning:\n\n> Deze wijk? Daar woon ik al tien jaar. — This district? I've lived there for ten years.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **The verb agrees with what follows**: *Er is veel winkels → `Er zijn veel winkels`.\n- **No er with de or het**: *Er is de bibliotheek naast de school → `De bibliotheek is naast de school`.\n- **er is not daar**: `Er is een bakker` says that a baker's exists; `Daar is de bakker` points at it: over there.\n- **er never starts a sentence as \"there\"**: *Er woon ik → `Daar woon ik` or `Ik woon er`.\n\n`er` has more uses (`Ik heb er twee`, `Ik denk erover`); they come in B1.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has no such word: `У моєму районі є пекарня` simply uses `є`. Dutch needs the place first (`In mijn buurt is een bakker`) or `er`: `Er is een bakker in mijn buurt`. A sentence that starts with the indefinite subject, *Een bakker is in mijn buurt, sounds wrong.","words":[{"nl":"buurt","art":"de","pl":"buurten","en":"neighbourhood / area","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wijk","art":"de","pl":"wijken","en":"district (of a town)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bakker","art":"de","pl":"bakkers","en":"baker / baker's","pos":"n"},{"nl":"slager","art":"de","pl":"slagers","en":"butcher / butcher's","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bibliotheek","art":"de","pl":"bibliotheken","en":"library","pos":"n"},{"nl":"buurthuis","art":"het","pl":"buurthuizen","en":"community centre","pos":"n"},{"nl":"speeltuin","art":"de","pl":"speeltuinen","en":"playground","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zwembad","art":"het","pl":"zwembaden","en":"swimming pool","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pakketpunt","art":"het","pl":"pakketpunten","en":"parcel collection point","pos":"n"},{"nl":"parkeerplaats","art":"de","pl":"parkeerplaatsen","en":"parking space / car park","pos":"n"},{"nl":"fietsenstalling","art":"de","pl":"fietsenstallingen","en":"bicycle parking","pos":"n"},{"nl":"glasbak","art":"de","pl":"glasbakken","en":"bottle bank","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voorziening","art":"de","pl":"voorzieningen","en":"facility / service","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gezellig","en":"cosy / pleasant (company, place)","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"overal","en":"everywhere","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Er is een bakker in mijn straat.","en":"There is a baker's in my street.","hint":["er","zijn","bakker","straat"],"note":"Een bakker is new for the listener, so the sentence starts with er. One baker: er is.","alt":["In mijn straat is er een bakker.","In mijn straat is een bakker."]},{"nl":"Er zijn veel gezellige cafés in deze wijk.","en":"There are lots of nice cafés in this district.","hint":["er","zijn","veel","gezellig","café","wijk"],"note":"The verb agrees with what follows: veel gezellige cafés is plural, so er zijn.","alt":["In deze wijk zijn er veel gezellige cafés.","In deze wijk zijn veel gezellige cafés."]},{"nl":"Is er een bibliotheek in de buurt?","en":"Is there a library nearby?","hint":["zijn","er","bibliotheek","buurt"],"note":"In a yes/no question the verb comes first and er follows directly: is er …?","alt":["Is er in de buurt een bibliotheek?","Is er een bibliotheek dichtbij?"]},{"nl":"Er is geen parkeerplaats voor de deur.","en":"There is no parking space in front of the door.","hint":["er","zijn","geen","parkeerplaats","deur"],"note":"The negative of er is een … is er is geen …, not er is niet een.","alt":["Voor de deur is er geen parkeerplaats.","Voor de deur is geen parkeerplaats."]},{"nl":"Er woont een kapper naast ons.","en":"A hairdresser lives next door to us.","hint":["er","wonen","kapper","naast"],"note":"er also goes with other verbs when the subject is indefinite. Dutch prefers this to starting with een kapper.","alt":["Naast ons woont een kapper.","Naast ons woont er een kapper."]},{"nl":"Er staat een glasbak naast de supermarkt.","en":"There is a bottle bank next to the supermarket.","hint":["er","staan","glasbak","naast","supermarkt"],"note":"For a thing that stands upright, Dutch often says er staat where English says 'there is'.","alt":["Naast de supermarkt staat een glasbak.","Naast de supermarkt staat er een glasbak.","Er is een glasbak naast de supermarkt.","Naast de supermarkt is een glasbak.","Naast de supermarkt is er een glasbak."]},{"nl":"Volgend jaar komt er een nieuw zwembad in onze wijk.","en":"Next year a new swimming pool is coming to our district.","hint":["volgend jaar","komen","er","nieuw","zwembad","wijk"],"note":"Volgend jaar stands first, so the verb comes next and er follows it, before the indefinite subject. After a time word, keep er.","alt":["Er komt volgend jaar een nieuw zwembad in onze wijk.","In onze wijk komt er volgend jaar een nieuw zwembad.","In onze wijk komt volgend jaar een nieuw zwembad."]},{"nl":"De slager is op de hoek.","en":"The butcher's is on the corner.","hint":["slager","zijn","hoek"],"note":"De slager is a known, definite subject, so there is no er. The subject simply comes first.","alt":["De slager zit op de hoek."]},{"nl":"Ken je het buurthuis? Ik ben er elke week.","en":"Do you know the community centre? I'm there every week.","hint":["kennen","buurthuis","zijn","er","elke week"],"note":"Here er means 'there' and refers back to het buurthuis. It is unstressed and stands right after the verb ben.","alt":["Ken jij het buurthuis? Ik ben er elke week.","Ken je het buurthuis? Ik ben daar elke week.","Ken jij het buurthuis? Ik ben daar elke week."]},{"nl":"Deze wijk? Daar woon ik al tien jaar.","en":"This district? I've lived there for ten years.","hint":["wijk","daar","wonen","al","jaar"],"note":"At the start of the sentence 'there' must be daar: er cannot stand first in this meaning. Daar comes first, so the verb follows and then ik.","alt":["Deze wijk? Ik woon er al tien jaar.","Deze wijk? Ik woon daar al tien jaar."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Er ___ overal fietsenstallingen in het centrum.","options":["is","zijn","heeft"],"correct":1,"en":"There is bicycle parking everywhere in the town centre.","note":"The verb agrees with the noun after it: fietsenstallingen is plural, so er zijn. Dutch never says er heeft."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Er ___ een pakketpunt in de supermarkt.","options":["zijn","heeft","is"],"correct":2,"en":"There is a parcel collection point in the supermarket.","note":"Een pakketpunt is one thing, so er is. Er is not the subject; the real subject comes after the verb."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ken die bakker goed. ___ koop ik elke zaterdag brood.","options":["Er","Daar","Het"],"correct":1,"en":"I know that baker's well. I buy bread there every Saturday.","note":"In first place 'there' is always daar. Er means 'there' only when it stands later in the sentence: Ik koop er elke zaterdag brood."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Er ___ (zijn) geen parkeerplaatsen in deze straat.","answers":["zijn"],"en":"There are no parking spaces in this street.","note":"Parkeerplaatsen is plural, so er zijn, also in the negative with geen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (zijn) er nog vragen over de nieuwe voorzieningen?","answers":["Zijn"],"en":"Are there any more questions about the new facilities?","note":"In a question the verb comes first and er follows it. Vragen is plural, so zijn."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Volgend jaar ___ (komen) er een speeltuin achter de school.","answers":["komt"],"en":"Next year a playground is coming behind the school.","note":"The verb agrees with een speeltuin, which comes after er: one thing, so komt."},{"type":"order","lead":"In onze wijk","words":["zijn","er","veel","voorzieningen"],"answers":["In onze wijk zijn er veel voorzieningen."],"en":"There are many facilities in our district.","note":"The place comes first, so the verb zijn follows, then er, then the indefinite subject. Here er could also be left out."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["is","er","een markt","op het plein"],"answers":["Morgen is er een markt op het plein.","Morgen is er op het plein een markt."],"en":"Tomorrow there is a market in the square.","note":"After Morgen comes the verb, then er, then the subject. After a time word, keep er."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["ben","er","nog","nooit","geweest"],"answers":["Ik ben er nog nooit geweest."],"en":"I have never been there.","note":"er (there) stands directly after the conjugated verb ben; nog nooit follows, and the participle goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Woon","words":["je","er","al","lang"],"answers":["Woon je er al lang?"],"en":"Have you lived there long?","note":"In a question the subject je follows the verb, and er (there) comes after the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative with geen.","from":"Er is een glasbak in onze straat.","answers":["Er is geen glasbak in onze straat."],"en":"There is no bottle bank in our street.","note":"Een becomes geen; er and is stay where they are."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with In het centrum.","from":"Er zijn veel winkels in het centrum.","answers":["In het centrum zijn er veel winkels.","In het centrum zijn veel winkels."],"en":"There are lots of shops in the town centre.","note":"The place comes first and the verb second. When a place stands first, er may stay after the verb or be left out."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a yes/no question.","from":"Er is een zwembad in de buurt.","answers":["Is er een zwembad in de buurt?","Is er in de buurt een zwembad?"],"en":"Is there a swimming pool nearby?","note":"The verb moves to the front and er follows it directly: is er …?"},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace in de bibliotheek by er.","from":"Ik werk graag in de bibliotheek.","answers":["Ik werk er graag."],"en":"I like working there.","note":"er takes the place of the whole phrase in de bibliotheek and stands directly after the verb werk."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Er is de bibliotheek naast het buurthuis.","answers":["De bibliotheek is naast het buurthuis.","Naast het buurthuis is de bibliotheek."],"en":"The library is next to the community centre.","note":"De bibliotheek is definite: the listener knows which one. A definite subject does not take er."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Er woon ik al tien jaar.","answers":["Daar woon ik al tien jaar.","Ik woon er al tien jaar.","Ik woon daar al tien jaar."],"en":"I have lived there for ten years.","note":"er cannot start a sentence when it means 'there'. At the start use daar, or put er after the verb."}]},
  {"id":"a2-27","level":"A2","icon":"☕","title":"A cup of coffee","grammar":"Diminutives: -je, -tje, -etje, -pje, -kje","summary":"A diminutive is always a het-word, plural -s: het kopje, de kopjes. Mostly -je (boekje); -tje after a vowel, -el, -en, -er (tafeltje); -etje after a short vowel + l, m, n, r, ng (balletje); -pje after a long vowel + m (boompje).","explain":"Dutch adds a small ending to a noun to make a diminutive (*het verkleinwoord*): `kop` becomes `kopje`, `tafel` becomes `tafeltje`. It can mean that something is small, but much more often it sounds friendly and informal, or it means one portion. In a café you hear diminutives in almost every sentence.\n\n### Always het, plural -s\n\nEvery diminutive is a **het-word**, whatever the article of the noun was. The plural adds **-s**, and like every plural it takes `de`.\n\n| | singular | plural |\n| de kop | het kopje | de kopjes |\n| de tafel | het tafeltje | de tafeltjes |\n| het glas | het glaasje | de glaasjes |\n\nSo it is `dit kopje`, `dat tafeltje`, and with `een` the adjective has no -e: `een lekker koekje`, `een klein tafeltje` (unit A2 · 10).\n\n> Dit kopje is te heet. — This cup is too hot.\n> Er liggen twee suikerklontjes op het schoteltje. — There are two sugar lumps on the saucer.\n\n### Which ending?\n\nThe ending depends on how the word ends. Say the word aloud: you need the sound.\n\n- **-je** after most consonants: `boek → boekje`, `huis → huisje`, `kop → kopje`, `brood → broodje`, `dag → dagje`.\n- **-tje** after a vowel or diphthong (`ei → eitje`, `thee → theetje`), after a long vowel or diphthong + l, n, r (`stoel → stoeltje`, `bier → biertje`, `telefoon → telefoontje`), and after unstressed -el, -en, -er (`tafel → tafeltje`, `keuken → keukentje`, `kamer → kamertje`). A final a, o or u is written double: `oma → omaatje`, `foto → fotootje`, `menu → menuutje`.\n- **-etje** after a **short** stressed vowel + l, m, n, r or ng; the consonant is doubled: `bal → balletje`, `kom → kommetje`, `pan → pannetje`, `ster → sterretje`, `ring → ringetje`.\n- **-pje** after m with a long vowel or diphthong, and after lm, rm and unstressed -em: `boom → boompje`, `raam → raampje`, `film → filmpje`, `arm → armpje`, `bezem → bezempje`.\n- **-kje** for unstressed -ing, which becomes -ink: `koning → koninkje`, `woning → woninkje`.\n\nA few common words change their vowel or add -etje: `glas → glaasje`, `blad → blaadje`, `gat → gaatje`, `pad → paadje`, `schip → scheepje`, `weg → weggetje`.\n\n> Ik neem een broodje kaas en een glaasje water. — I'll have a cheese roll and a small glass of water.\n> Ik neem een kommetje soep. — I'll have a small bowl of soup.\n\n### What a diminutive means\n\n- **small**: `een tafeltje bij het raam` (a small table by the window).\n- **friendly, informal, one portion**: `een biertje` (a beer), `een kopje koffie` (a cup of coffee), `een ijsje` (an ice cream), `een dagje uit` (a day out), `een momentje` (just a moment), `een telefoontje` (a phone call).\n\n> Zullen we een terrasje pakken? — Shall we go and sit outside at a café?\n\nSome words exist only as a diminutive, or have their own meaning: `het meisje` (girl), `het jongetje` (little boy), `het sprookje` (fairy tale), `het koekje` (biscuit), `het toetje` (dessert), `het kaartje` (ticket, or a small card), `het cadeautje` (a present), `het kopje` (cup; `kop` is a larger cup, a mug, or a head). Note: `het meisje`, but you say `ze` about her.\n\nA few adverbs take **-jes** or **-tjes**: `even → eventjes` (just a moment), `zacht → zachtjes` (quietly), `net → netjes` (neatly).\n\n> Wacht eventjes, ik moet nog afrekenen. — Wait a moment, I still have to pay.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **het, not de**: *de kopje → `het kopje`; *deze koekje → `dit koekje`.\n- **No -e after een**: *een lekkere koekje → `een lekker koekje`. With `het` the -e comes back: `het lekkere koekje`.\n- **The right ending**: *stoelje → `stoeltje`; *balje → `balletje`; *glasje → `glaasje`.\n- **Plural -s**: *twee broodje → `twee broodjes`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has diminutives too (`чашка → чашечка`), and they also sound warm. Dutch uses them even more for everyday things, and the article always changes to `het`, which has no parallel in Ukrainian.","words":[{"nl":"kopje","art":"het","pl":"kopjes","en":"cup","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schoteltje","art":"het","pl":"schoteltjes","en":"saucer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"theezakje","art":"het","pl":"theezakjes","en":"tea bag","pos":"n"},{"nl":"suikerklontje","art":"het","pl":"suikerklontjes","en":"sugar lump","pos":"n"},{"nl":"koekje","art":"het","pl":"koekjes","en":"biscuit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"broodje","art":"het","pl":"broodjes","en":"bread roll / sandwich (in a roll)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rietje","art":"het","pl":"rietjes","en":"drinking straw","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ijsje","art":"het","pl":"ijsjes","en":"ice cream (one portion)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tosti","art":"de","pl":"tosti's","en":"toasted sandwich","pos":"n"},{"nl":"terras","art":"het","pl":"terrassen","en":"terrace (outside a café)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"dienblad","art":"het","pl":"dienbladen","en":"tray","pos":"n"},{"nl":"slagroom","art":"de","pl":"","en":"whipped cream","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"frisdrank","art":"de","pl":"frisdranken","en":"soft drink","pos":"n"},{"nl":"trakteren","past":"trakteerde","perf":"heeft getrakteerd","en":"to treat (someone) / to pay for everyone","pos":"v"},{"nl":"afrekenen","past":"rekende af","perf":"heeft afgerekend","en":"to pay (the bill)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Mag ik een kopje koffie, alstublieft?","en":"May I have a cup of coffee, please? (formal)","hint":["mogen","kopje","koffie","alstublieft"],"note":"kop → kopje: after a consonant the ending is -je. Een kopje koffie is the normal way to order a cup of coffee."},{"nl":"Zullen we een terrasje pakken?","en":"Shall we go and sit outside at a café?","hint":["zullen","terras","pakken"],"note":"een terrasje pakken is a fixed expression: a drink outside at a café. The diminutive makes it sound relaxed, not small. Terras → terrasje with -je after the s."},{"nl":"Ik neem een broodje kaas en een glaasje water.","en":"I'll have a cheese roll and a small glass of water.","hint":["nemen","broodje","kaas","glas","water"],"note":"brood → broodje is regular. Glas is irregular: the vowel becomes long, glaasje."},{"nl":"Dit kopje is te heet, ik wacht eventjes.","en":"This cup is too hot, I'll wait a moment.","hint":["dit","kopje","heet","wachten","eventjes"],"note":"Kopje is a het-word, so dit, not deze. Eventjes is the adverb even with -tjes: it sounds a little softer.","alt":["Dit kopje is te heet, ik wacht even."]},{"nl":"Bij de koffie krijg je hier altijd een koekje.","en":"Here you always get a biscuit with your coffee.","hint":["koffie","krijgen","hier","altijd","koekje"],"note":"Koekje is the normal word for a biscuit; it only exists in this form with this meaning. Bij de koffie comes first, so the verb comes before je.","alt":["Je krijgt hier bij de koffie altijd een koekje.","Je krijgt hier altijd een koekje bij de koffie.","Hier krijg je bij de koffie altijd een koekje."]},{"nl":"Wil je een rietje in je frisdrank?","en":"Do you want a straw in your soft drink?","hint":["willen","rietje","frisdrank"],"note":"Rietje (drinking straw) is a diminutive with its own meaning: het riet is the reed that grows by the water.","alt":["Wil jij een rietje in je frisdrank?"]},{"nl":"Er liggen twee suikerklontjes op het schoteltje.","en":"There are two sugar lumps on the saucer.","hint":["er","liggen","twee","suikerklontje","schoteltje"],"note":"The plural of a diminutive adds -s: suikerklontjes. Schotel ends in unstressed -el, so the ending is -tje: schoteltje.","alt":["Op het schoteltje liggen twee suikerklontjes."]},{"nl":"Ik trakteer, want ik heb een nieuw baantje.","en":"It's my treat, because I have a new (little) job.","hint":["trakteren","want","nieuw","baan"],"note":"baan → baantje: long vowel + n takes -tje. Baantje is a het-word, so een nieuw without -e.","alt":["Ik trakteer, omdat ik een nieuw baantje heb."]},{"nl":"Wacht eventjes, ik moet nog afrekenen.","en":"Wait a moment, I still have to pay.","hint":["wachten","eventjes","moeten","nog","afrekenen"],"note":"Eventjes is even with the diminutive ending -tjes. Like even, it makes the request friendlier.","alt":["Wacht even, ik moet nog afrekenen."]},{"nl":"Het meisje bestelt een ijsje met slagroom.","en":"The girl orders an ice cream with whipped cream.","hint":["meisje","bestellen","ijsje","slagroom"],"note":"Meisje exists only as a diminutive and is a het-word: het meisje. Een ijsje is one portion of ice cream."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ kopje op tafel is van mij.","options":["De","Deze","Het"],"correct":2,"en":"The cup on the table is mine.","note":"Every diminutive is a het-word: het kopje. That is why de and deze are wrong; 'this cup' would be dit kopje."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik neem een ___ koekje bij de koffie.","options":["lekkere","lekker","lekkers"],"correct":1,"en":"I'll have a nice biscuit with my coffee.","note":"Koekje is a het-word. With een and a het-word the adjective has no -e: een lekker koekje (but het lekkere koekje)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Zullen we aan dat ___ bij het raam gaan zitten?","options":["tafelje","tafeltje","tafeletje"],"correct":1,"en":"Shall we sit at that little table by the window?","note":"Tafel ends in unstressed -el, so the ending is -tje. -etje is only for a short stressed vowel: bal → balletje."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wil je nog een ___ wijn?","options":["glasje","glaasje","glassje"],"correct":1,"en":"Would you like another glass of wine?","note":"Glas is one of the irregular diminutives: the a becomes long, so it is written double: glaasje."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik bestel een ___ (bier) en een tosti.","answers":["biertje"],"en":"I'll order a beer and a toasted sandwich.","note":"Bier has a long vowel (ie) + r, so the ending is -tje: biertje. It means one glass of beer, not a small one."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op het dienblad staan drie ___ (kop).","answers":["kopjes"],"en":"There are three cups on the tray.","note":"kop → kopje, and the plural of a diminutive adds -s: kopjes."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Kunt u een ___ (foto) van ons maken?","answers":["fotootje"],"en":"Could you take a (quick) photo of us?","note":"A word ending in o gets -tje, and the o is written double so that it stays long: fotootje."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Voor het café staat een ___ (boom).","answers":["boompje"],"en":"There is a small tree in front of the café.","note":"After m with a long vowel (oo) the ending is -pje: boom → boompje, like raam → raampje."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Bij het broodje krijgt u een ___ (kom) soep.","answers":["kommetje"],"en":"With the roll you (formal) get a small bowl of soup.","note":"Kom has a short stressed vowel + m, so the ending is -etje and the m is doubled: kommetje."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zullen we zondag een ___ (dag) naar zee gaan?","answers":["dagje"],"en":"Shall we have a day out at the seaside on Sunday?","note":"After a consonant like g the ending is simply -je: dagje. Een dagje means a relaxed day out, not a short day."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make both nouns plural.","from":"Het theezakje ligt op het schoteltje.","answers":["De theezakjes liggen op de schoteltjes."],"en":"The tea bags are lying on the saucers.","note":"A diminutive takes -s in the plural, and like every plural it takes de: de theezakjes, de schoteltjes. The verb becomes plural too."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace de tafel by its diminutive.","from":"De tafel bij het raam is vrij.","answers":["Het tafeltje bij het raam is vrij."],"en":"The little table by the window is free.","note":"The diminutive is a het-word, so de becomes het: het tafeltje."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace kamer by its diminutive.","from":"Wij hebben een kleine kamer boven het café.","answers":["Wij hebben een klein kamertje boven het café."],"en":"We have a small room above the café.","note":"kamer → kamertje (unstressed -er takes -tje). It is now a het-word, so after een the adjective loses its -e: een klein kamertje."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Deze koekje is heel lekker.","answers":["Dit koekje is heel lekker."],"en":"This biscuit is very tasty.","note":"Koekje is a het-word, like every diminutive, so 'this' is dit, not deze."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"We hebben twee biertjes en drie broodje besteld.","answers":["We hebben twee biertjes en drie broodjes besteld."],"en":"We have ordered two beers and three rolls.","note":"The plural of a diminutive ends in -s: drie broodjes."}]}
];
